        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPITS,</p>
        <p>It takes two to make a betrothal, and both are to -blame. No man is ignorant who knows enough to con- teal what he doesn't know. The man who thinks he can make a success of several things isn't a successful thinker. Dollars are dross—pan can take his ashes to the grave, but must leave his "clinkers" behind. A HOMELY TntrrH.—Some people have " speak- ing " countenances—and as plain as words can be.</p>
        <p>"You seem fascinatedwith cycling'?" "Oh, yes ; now I have once got into the habit of it," she rejoined, glancing at her costume. HE ENVIED THEM.—" Scientists say there are microbes in kisses," said Miss Kittish to Mr. Hunker. "Happy microbes'!" exclaimed the young man, ecstatically. -</p>
        <p>- WANTED No QUAY:MELLEN:G.—He (e-ncouragingly) : "rm sure of one thing, my angel—you and I will never quarrel as that couple are doing." She (with decision) : "Indeed we won't. If you ever speak to me as he did to her, call the police." A WISE PROVISION.—LittIe Ethel (who has been looking at pictures) : "When boys go to heaven, they just take their heads an' put wings on them, an they fly around that •way." Little Johnny: " Wot's that for ? " Little .Ethel "I guess that's so they can't fight:" AN OFF Div.—Miss Newage : "What was done at the New Woman International Progressive Club today?" Bachelor Girl: " Nothing. You see, Mrs. Sweetie happened to come in with her baby, and before we .111 got through kissing the little cherub, it was time to adjourn." NOT QUITE FULL.—Parker : "I would join the church if it wasn't fullsfuf hypocrites." Tucker: "Oh, you are mistaken about that. There's always room for one more. - - - - - -</p>
        <p>LITTLE Miss Freckles: " I made ugly faces at your stuck-up sister the other day, but I guess she didn't see me." Little Johnny:• "Yes, she did ; but she thought they were natural."</p>
        <p>THE SUPERJORITY OF WOMAN.—Ada: "Men are so slow! It took :.him nearly two hours to propose to me last night." Helen: "And how long did it take you to accept him, dear?" Ada: "Just two seoonds ! "</p>
        <p>DUTY NEGLECTED.—An old song says that Britons never, never will be slaves, And boasts al3out Britannia, the ruler of the waves; But I've just crossed the stormy sea and I regret to state That if Britmnia ruled• the waves she didn't, rule 'em gtraight. Too CLOSE QUARTERS. —Sweet Girl (in photo- graph gallery) : "I wish you wouldn't put that big camera so clase to me." Photographer: " Don't you want the picture cabinet size?" Sweet Girl: " Yes ; but I don't •want the freckles cabinet</p>
        <p>HANDLED WITH CARE. —CHSIOIner (Who has just purchased a wonderfully cheap set of furniture) : "Do you always pack furniture carefully before delivering it ?' New Boy: "This kind we do, 'cause the jarring would shake it to pieces." A CERTAINTY. —,Thins are coming to such a pass that pretty soon when we see a young man who is walking ahead of us carefully lift one coat tail as he crosses the street ,we may feel morally certain that he is an advanced young woman. ALL's PAIR IN LovE.—,lliss : "So you know Miss Tiptop, the belle cif the season ? " Mr. Sharpwit (after Miss Million's heart, hand, and bank account) : "011 yes a-she and I move in—in—all— much the same set." Miss _Million: "By the way, here comes Miss. Tiptop now. We will meet her face to face.... . Why,. the did not recognize you." Mr. Sharpwit: "She ,always acts that way when I'm with a prettier girl than she is."— , FORGET HIS NamE.---;Magistrate why didn't you answer to your name?" Vagrant: "Beg parding. jedgeabut I forgot wot name I gave las' night." Magistrate: "Didn't you give your own name ?" Vagrant: No, jedge, Tan travellin' incog." DIFFERENT. —Chase (to dentist) : "I won't pay anything extra &amp;or gas. ',Just pull the tooth. out .even if does hurt a little." Dentist: "I must say you are very plucky. Just let me see the tooth." Chase: "Oh, I harem' t . got any toothache. It's Mrs. Chase; rh&amp;ll be here in a minute." Mercx TO ANSWER FOR: —Nagger : "There goes the wretch who ruined my life." Wagger: " What ? Explain yourself, old man." Nagger: "He was engaged to my :,:wife, and jilted her before I met her." His REconmszn VrirrriE.—Bootles (the maker) : "Young Howling swell is lead;( he was :a mighty wild young mane -hut he always paid his bills." Tapes (the tailor) " Yes; I always gave him credit for that." EXCHANGE ,Or CIVILITIES —First Good Fellow: ." Havea cigar ? " Second Good Fellow: "Thanks. Trynne of mine." Each (toddinsell, a few moments later).: " This miserable weed is even worse than theone I gave him." HORROLE.—"Did ye everehear of dese priests in India," said Reginald de Bunime, " dat sticks needles through dere cheeks an' burns (lore skin jes' fur fan?" "Dat -an% nothins," replied Clarence Fitz Hautheau. "I knowed unman dat tuck a bath everyday 'cause he.,said he liked it !" NOT SATISFIED.—" And you saw Niagara?" Yes" -"And did you notcontider it wonderful ?" "Net so v'ery. The water simply fell over the rock. If it were the other Lay, now, and the water climbed them, it would „have been quite marvellous. HE ,(after popping tire question) : "Why do you .cry,.nry, angel? Did I effend you ymy proposal ?" She (still soblaing)-: "Oh no, dear, it is not that; I am crying for joy. Mother always said to me, 'May,' she said,. - you.are such ae idiot that you 'would not get even a donkey for a lover,' and now I have got one.after all." HANDY DEFiNITIoNS.—" Pa," „asked Johnnie Wilkins, ." what:is a pathologist?" "He's a pan who lays out paths in parks and tisewhere, my boy. Now, don't bother papa any more. He's busy." "Well, I only went to know one more thing, papa," said. Johnnie. "Well, what is it?" "What's a maneipated woman ?" -" Why don't you reason it outfor yourself?" asked - the father. "What -does spell?" "Man," said Johnnie. "Well, then, a Man-cipated woman is a womeaa that thinks she's ,a man. .Now, run away." Areismaa ,ont of thelate stoppage in thecoal trade, capitalitory is worth repeating concerning Lord Derby and a collier. Wandering on some land belonging .te Earl Derby, the collier there, chanced to meet the owner of ...Knowsley face to face. His lordship inqUired if the collier knew he was walking on his land. "Thy land ? Well, I've got no land mysel '," was the reply, "and Fin like 'to walk on somebody's. Wheer did tha' get it fm' "Oh," explained his lordship, .4‘ Igot it from tny ancestors." "An' wheer did you get it fro' ? " eueried the collier. " They got it from their ancestors," was the reply. "And wheer did their anyestors get it They fought for it." " ih begad;" said the collier, squaring up to thenoiae earl, "flu feight thee for it..!" CLOTHING THE FAMILT.—•,` My dear," said Mrs. 13loobunaper to herb usband, " 'really i a gown. I am ashan3ed to go out any more with the (oily one I have." " Very well,' replied Mr. Bleobitinper. "Get youraelf a gown.' By good riellts I ought to have a bonnet, too. I haven't ha' m mew one for three :months, and it's getting\re. to :say nothing of its being utterly tee e• 1 to this weather." "Get a bonnet, too, deal " Then Annie needs a few more things. cant's pos- sibly put off getting a new dress mei .1 iiew hat any longer.. A young lady of her a.ge absoliitsely required to dress well. You know tiisit yourself, Harry." "It is true, dear. Get a lettever is necessary and proeer for Annie." -• I;aby Bess will have to have aeine new dresses, 'leen'. It is wonderful what a lot of clothes a hit givi of eight JTquires. You wouldn't believe it pai didn't have to provide them And keep to' ii looking at least semi-respectable. "That is t..ee., little Wiley. Baby Bess deserves all the nice 'clothes she gets, and it is quite right for you to take a proper pride in dressing your daughters neatly.' "Then Harry, dear, you need an entire new suit yourself, and a new hat. I elon't think you e,ie hare noticed flow shiny your cat is in the while your trousers have commenced to fray a:e at the ends as well as bag at the knees. Wee ,ou get; a new suit love ? " "I don't kno,7.t.i, suit, dear, but I . Annie anl Baby Bess welv, pro,.tOed 1(.5, I'd get myself a cheap pair of trousers ii I had t hat mach money left."</p>
