        <head>" WifITA.L" CIiAT.</head>
        <p>" WifITA.L" CIiAT.</p>
        <p>1 I understand that there is a misapprehension as to the present dip omatic position of the Cretan . question. There has as yet been no formal oroposal for a blockade of the Cretan coasts in order to pre- vent aid from Greece to the insurgents. The idea, ' . I am told, has been mooted in a more or less in- formal and irresponsible way, but there has been ' no proposition, and the British Government has i certainly formed no decision, or at least announced : none, on the subject. But it is equally certain that Lord Salisbury will do nothing to help the Turks, even though he may be withdrawing from the i "Concert of Europe." It is believed that the talk , of a blockade was a feint to ascertain the views of this country, and that in consequence of the scorn and repudiation with which the notion has been i received nothing more will be heard of it. What is I regarded as serious in this and similar matters is the campaign of scurrility and insult carried on by the semi-official German press whenever any pet , notion or theory of the Prussian bureaucrats in Berlin is not instantly adopted by every nation. Germany is the only country in the world which carries on its diplomacy in this way, and the prac- tice is a legacy from Bismarckian days. Indeed ' the "reptile press," as he once styled it, is his own ! creation, and was described to me to-day as an i angry boil on the face of European diplomacy.</p>
        <p>I do not think our Foreign Office is as fond of the 'Concert of Europe "as it was up to the Armenian fiasco, and I have the best reason for believing that this country will not belong to the" Concert "for some time to come when it acts on behalf of the Sultan. Moreover, the official view here is that the attitude of the Greek Government in regard to Crete has been one of self-restraint and good sense and absolutely correct in the diplomatic sense of that adjective. No doubt one great difficulty in standing aside from the "Concert of Europe" is our position in Egypt, for if the Powers grew ac- customed to acting without is they might speedily begin to act against us, and Egypt is our only vul- nerable point. But, this has to be taced sometime- and when it comes, after all that has passed latrily to aiding the Sultan against his Christian subject - the time has come to run the risk of Egypt being made a worry to us. This, at any rate, is the view which I understand to prevail in official circles just at present.</p>
        <p>The two stiff-necked Johannesburgers who still linger in Pretoria Prison, because they will nor stoop to petition President Kruger for clemency, formed the subject of several questions in Parli.— ment on Tuesday evening. Mr. Chamberlain de- clines to take any further step in their behalf unt.l they make an appeal to the President,which he haN more than once advised them to do. They are tun fortunately under the belief that they laid dowt their arms under a promise of the protection •of a British Government, a belief which migh- very easily be formed under the influence of th,- pressure exerted on the raiders at the instigation of Mr. Chamberlain. it is, no doubt, humiliating to high-spirited men to make an appeal for clensenc, but the Reformers were not troubled with any scruples about personal pride, and most of :them were effeiire lit their gratitude to Uncle Paul as soon as they found themselves once more at liberty in Johannnesburg. But the two prisoners have sufficiently testified to the toughness of their :fibre. and they have evidently to chose between a wasted life or at least several wasted years, and p. life of liberty and nsefulness. If it is to be a trial of obstinacy -between them and President Kruger, the President, has all the advantages on his side.an ' can better aff )rd to play the waiting game than they. This much must be said for them, that, the have not harrassed the Colonial Office with thei.. whinings.</p>
        <p>Li Hung Chang duly paid his visit to the House of Parliament on Tuesday. He was assisted into a bamboo chair on leaving his carriage,and carriad two policemen under the direction of the inspector to the entrance of the inner lobby of the Houses of Commons. Then he descended from his chair and, still assisted by his secretaries,walked through the lobby,howing right and left with a pleasant smite to the throng of members who were awaiting him most of whom uncovered and bowed as he paesed. Li Hung Chang wore the yellow jacket, with tt,,, semi-State hat of deep red with gold button, and with the trailing peacock feather ; and be had or the common Chinese shoes and white stockings. He was accompanied by Loh Fung Lu,who is, or we, until recently, Secretary to the Chinese Navy, an:l who was at one time in the suite of the Marquis Tseng. Loh Fung Lu acted as interpreter. Hp speaks English excellently. In the House of Com- mons Li flung Chang was provided with a seat un- der the gallery,aa he is averse from mounting stairs and would not therefore take a place in the distin- guished strangers' gallery. He was at once joined by Mr. Cll rnia. A little later Mr. Chamberlain went up,and an animated conversation, which was evi dently highly amusing to all concerned, took plop,- through ttse interpreter. Li Hung Chang showed a great interes in Mr. Chamberlain's eyeglass. Two Irish members who were wearing yellow waist coats were tainted at by the ex-Viceroy, and it -romoured that he ventured the query that they were probably the " great men" of the House.</p>
        <p>-Some time ago the formation of the British Board established to advance the 'interests of the New Australia co-operative colony in Paraguay, was announced, and I have now received a repor recently presented to that organisation respectii g the, present state of affairs at the settlement. F row the beginning the chief difficulty which appears to have facE,d the colonists has been that of direction and government. In the early part of this year there was no definite policy; the officials had faith in the con,titution,and were actively oppose;i to it. A later account records the appointment of a new president and a strong board of management determined to carry out the provisions of the origi- nal constitution. There is nothing now, it is stated, to stop the colonists from going ahead and acquir- ing solid comforts in addition to health, liberty and congenial work. Their stock is in a fine condition, always increasing and improving; they have an ample supply of food for the coming year,and mtw "dead " 'work is done. The health of the community is excellent. They have a Government subsidy 01-800 dollars a month, and the Govern- ment is Rl9O to send them schoolmasters and grant them deEnite titles to some 26.000 acres of land. They are propo-ing to concentrate their labour on one or two settlements, and plant tobacco, cotton and other articles in large quantities for revenue purposes. Above all, they have two and a half years' v6luede experience to guide them. " l'here is no reason.now." declares the report, " why peo- ple should be deterred from going out."</p>
        <p>Two of the great Commissioners are,l understand. on the point of sending in their reports. The Com- mi,‘sion on vaccination will have their report pi e- sented, at any ratcin dummy, before th?.. proroga• Lion, and the report itself may be expected to be issued and available in a few weeks. While thene will be at Iraq two minority reports, which will represent the more extreme view as to the tote, abolition of the vaccination system and the , machinery by which it is carried out, and on the other hand possibly the extreme views of the be- lievers in vaccination, it is underatood that the main report will directly, as well ,as indirectly, affet the position in a sense hostile to the old , official attitute. The evidence in support of the vieW that saniteion and isolation have bad a pre- poneerating i-ffect in curtailing the ravages r small-pox will be found to have had great w-it with the Commission. The Welsh Land Commi.- sion will also, I hear, be in a position to report ht an earlier date than was expected a few months :Ivo The report is said to be of a very advanced char- acter, and it is anticipated that the conclusions wi)t have a marked effect on the future development of reforms in agrarian tenures. ,</p>
        <p>IA lady who has just returned from the scenes 04 I the recent masteacres of Armenian Christians visite, . the House of Cotamons on Wednesday, and ha. interviews with Mr. S. Smith and other members, who propose to have a series of questions put ti Ministers upon her startling information. states that shortly before she left Armenia the Con- suls and missionaries calculated that the lives of na less than a million native Christians had beer</p>
