      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>TH X A f 'T)if k V A ITGITST 8, 1' 96</head>
        <p>TH X A f 'T)if k V A ITGITST 8, 1' 96</p>
        <p>Ile used to be MU atraid to turn over for fear that we should bump our heads against the timbers of the ceiling 2" "Yes, indeed," said Traverse. And thereupon the two friends launched into la discussion of old times, when the two widows and their sons lived together—the two women occupy- ing one bed, and the two boys the other. And this discussion they kept up until long after they retired, and until sleep overtook them. The next morning Traverse conducted his friend down to the breakfast parlour, to introduce him to Doctor St. Jean, who, as soon as he perceived his young medical assistant, sprang forward, exclaim- ing: "Grand Heaven 1 Is this then you? Have you then returned? What for did you run away with my horse?" "I went to New Orleans in great haste, upon very Important business, sir." "Grand dieu ! I should think so, II when you dde off on my horse without saying a word 1 If it had been my ambling pony I should have been in despair, II Your business so hasty and so important was accomplished, I hope 2" " Yes ; I did my errand with less trouble than I had anticipated, owing to the happy circumstances A meeting my friend here, vrio has come down hither connected with the same business."</p>
        <p>" Ah, very happy to see your friend. In the med- ical profession, I suppose 7" "No, sir; in the army. Allow me to present him. Major Herbert Greyson, of the—th Regiment of Cavalry." "Onil ay! Grand ciel this is the brave, the aistinguished, the illustrious officer, so honourably mentioned in the dispatches of the invinci 'lle Tay- lor and the mighty Scott F' said the Itttle French- man, bowing his night-capped head down to his Blippered toes. Herbert smiled as he returned the bow. And then the little French doctor, turning to Traverse, said:</p>
        <p>"But your business, so important and so hasty, which has brought this officer so illustrious down Sown here ;—what is it, my frieni 7" "We will have the honour of explaining to Mon- sieur le Docteur, over our coffee, if he will oblige is by ordering the servant to retire." said Traverse. WOO ClillGh adopted in speaking to the tau Frenchman his own formal style of politeness. " Oui, oui, certainment 1 Allez done, John Go, then, John 1" As soon as the man hal gone, Traverse said: "I propose to discuss this business over our coffee, because it will save time without interfering with our morning meal, and I know that immediately afterwards you will go your usual round of visits to your patients I" "Ei then! proceed, my son proceed" Traverse immediately commenced and related all that was necessary concerning the fraud practisfrd upon the institution by introducing into it an unfortunate woman, represented to be mad, but really only sorrowful, nervous and excita 'le. Aud to prove the truth of his words, Traverse desired Herbert to read from, the confession the portion relating to this fraud, and to show the doctor the signature of the principle and the witness. To Maya seen the old French doctor then I I rejoice in a Frenchman, for the frank abandon with which he gives himself up to his emotions Our doctor, after staring at the confession, took hold of the top of his blue tasselled night-cap, pulled it off his head and threw it violently upon the floor I Then remembering that he was exposing a cranium as bald as a peeled potato, he suddenly 05u4ht it up again, clapped it upon his crown and exulaimed : " Sa.cre I Diable I" and other ejaculations dreadful to translate, and ethers again which it would be profane to set down in French or English. G tbriel Le Noir was no longer an officer illustrion3„ a gentleman noble and distinguished, compassionate and tender; he was a robber infamous! a vilin atrocious! a caitiff, ruthless, and without remorse! After breakfast the doctor consented that his young hero, his little knight-errant, his dear son, should go to the distressed lady and open the good news to her; while the great Major Greyson, the warrior invincible, should go around with himself to inspect the institution. Traverse immediately repaired to the chamber of Mrs. Le Noir, whom he found sitting at the window, engaged in some little trifle of needlework, the same pale, patient woman that she had first appeared to him. " Ah, you have come! I read good news upon your smiling face, my friend! Tell It! I have borne the worst of sorrows! shall I not have strength to bear joy 2" Traverse told her all, and then ended by saying: "Now, dear Madam, it is necessary that we leave this place within two hours, as Major Greyson's regiment leaves New Orleans for Washington to-morrow, and it is advisable that you go under our protection. We can get you a female attendant from the St. Charles." "Oh, I can be ready in ten minutes I have no Went out lady's wardrobe to pack up 1" replied Mrs. Le Noir, with a smile. hoTorravaefrtseerwarbodweedthe wholeand party yut totook l from proeacuvere of carriage om the next village. And in half an Doctor Pierre St. Jean and his "institution incom- parable," and set forth on their journey to New Or- leans, whence in two days afterwards they sailed for the North. And now, dear reader, let you and me take the fast boat, and get home before them to see our little Cap, and find out what adventures she is nAur pnMiked in. and how she is getting on.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER LX</p>
        <p>CAPITOLA A CAPITALIST. "Plumed victory Is truly painted with a cheerful look, Equally distant from proud insolence</p>
        <p>And sad detection.".</p>
        <p>'-M.ASSINGER.</p>
        <p>How glad lam to—g-e-1,-ZO-k—a;Lilittle Li" ; or I know very well, reader, just as well as if you had told me, that you have been grumbling for some weeks for the want of Cp. But I could not help it, for, to tell the truth, I was pining after her myself, which was the reason that I could not do half justice to the scenes of the Mexican War. Well, now let us see what Cap has been doing— what oppressors she has punished—what vict,ms she has delivered—in a word, what new heroic adventures she has achieved._ .</p>
        <p>Well, the trial of Donald Bayne, alias Black Donald, was over. Cap, of course, had been com- pelled to appear agaibst him. During the whole course of the trial the court-room was crowded with a curious multitude, "from far and near," eager to get sight of the notorious outlaw. Black Donald, through the whole ordeal, deported himseif with a gallant and joyous dignity, that would have better become a triumph than a trial. He was indicted upon several distinct c mnts, the most serious of which—the murder of toe solitary widow and her daughter in the forest cabin, and the assassination of Eugene Le Noir in the woods near the Hidden House—were sustained only by circumstantial evidence. But the aggregate weight of all these, together with his very bad reputation, was sufficient to convict him, and Black Donald was sentenced to death.</p>
        <p>This dreadful doom, most solemnly pronounced by the judge, was recesed by the prisoner with a loud laugh, and the words: "You're out o' your reckoning now, cap'n! I never was a saint, the Lord knows, but my eyes are free from blood-guiltinesz I There's an honest little girl that believes me—don't you 7" he said, turning laughingly to our little heroine. "Yes, I ao I " said Cap, bursting into tears; "and I am so sorry for you as ever I can be, Donald Bayne." "Bother! it is sure to come to this first or last, and I knew it! Now, to prove you do not think this rugged hand of mine stained with blood, give it a friendly shake!" said the comdemned man. And before Old Hurricane could ,prevent her, Capitola had jumped over two or three intervening seats and climbed up to the side of the dock, and reached up her hand to the prisoner saying: ' God help you, Donald Bayne, in your great trouble, and I will do all I can to help you in this world. I will go to the Governor myself, and tell him I know you never did any murder." " Remove the prisoner." said the judge, peremp.</p>
        <p>_ torily. • _ _ The constables approached and led away Black Donald.</p>
        <p>_ _ OA Hurricane rushed upon Cap, seized her, and shaking her fiercely, exclaimed, under his breath: "You—you—you—you New York hurraw boy you foundling 1 you vagabond! you vagrant ! you brat! you beggar! will you never be a lady! to go and shake hands with that ruffian I" "lure, uncle, that's nothing now; I have shaken hands with you oiten enough!" ' " Dammy, you—you—you New York trash, what do you mean by that 11 "Of course I mean, uncle, that you are as rough a ruffian as ever Donald Bayne was!" " Datnmy, I'll murder you!" "Don't node; they have an uncivilised way here of hanging murderers," said Cap, shaking herself free of OA Hurticane's grasp, and hastening out of the court-room to mount her horse and ride home.</p>
        <p>One night after tea, Ckpitola and her 'wee occupied their usual seats by the little bright woud fire, that ,the chilly evening and the keen mountain air )pacte agreeable, even la Maze</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>HIDDEN. HAND;</head>
        <p>THE HIDDEN. HAND;</p>
        <p>CAPITOLA THE MADCAP. BY Miss. E. D. E. N. SOUTHWORTH.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER LIS. PROSPECTS BRIGHTEN.</p>
        <p>*Thus far our fortune keeps an onward course, And we are graced with wreaths of victory."</p>
        <p>-SHAKiSPZARE.</p>
        <p>Leaving Mrs. Le Noir, Traverse went down to the stable, saddled the horse that had been allotted to his use, and set off for a long day's journey to New Orleans, where late at night he arrived, and put up at the St. Charles.</p>
        <p>He slept deeply from fatigue until late the next morning, when he was awakened by the sound of drums, trumpets and fifes, and by general rejoicing.</p>
        <p>He arose and looked from his Windows to ascer- tain the cause, and saw the square full of people in a state of the highest excitement, watching for a military procession coming up the street. It was the United States troops under their gallant commanders, who had landed from the steamboats that morning and were now marching from the quay up to their quarte-a at the St. Charles. As they advanced, Traverse, eagerly upon the lookout, recognised his own regiment, and presently saw Major Greyson himself. Traverse withdrew from the window, hurriedly cora- pleted his toilet, and hastened down stairs, where he found himself face to face with Herbert, who soon warmly grasping his hand, exclaimed: 'You here, old friend? Why, I thought you were down in East Feliciana, with your interesting patient 1" "It is for the interest of that interesting patient' that I am here, Heruert I Did I tell you she was one of the victims of that demon, Le Noir 2"</p>
        <p>; but I know it from another source! I know as much, or more of her, perhaps, than you do 1"</p>
        <p>" Ah 1" exclaimed Traverse, in -surprise. .41Yes 1 I know, for instance, that she is Capitola's mother, the long lost widow of Eugene Le Noir, the mistress of the Hidden House, and the ghost who drew folks' curtains there at night*" "Then you do know something about her, but how did you arrive at the knowledge 7" "By the last dying speech and confession' of Gn brie] Le Noir, contided to me, to be used in resci ution after his decease I But, come I there is the second bell ! Our mess are going in for break- faik ; join us and afterwards you and I will retire and compare notes," said Herbert, taking the arm of his friend, as they followed the moViag crowd into the breakfast parlour. After the morning meal was concluded the friends witrvzaw together to the chamber occupied by Traverse Rocke, -where they sat down-for mutual ex ols.natiOns.</p>
        <p>Herbert first related to Traverse all that had occurred from the time that the latter left the city of Mexico, including the arrival of Craven Le Noir at the dying bed of his father, the subsequent death and funeral of Colonel Le Noir, and the late emi- gration of Craven, who, to avoid the shame of the approaching revelation, joined a parry of explerers bound for the recently discovered gold mines of California. " The civiliz 'd world is then rid of two villians at once," said the uncompromising Traverse. Herbert took from his pocket the confession of 'Colonel Le Noir, which he said he was now at liberty to use as he thought proper for the ends of justice. That certain parts of the disclosure intim- ately concerned Traverse ; ro whom he should, therefore, read the whole. Tut, confession may be briefly summed up as follows:</p>
        <p>The first item wps, that he had soug. t o win the affections of M.irah 11,)cke, the supponed wife of Major Ira Warfield ; he had sedulously waylaid and followed her with his suit (luring the wnole summer; she had constantly repu;sed and avoided him; tie listening to his own evil passions, bad bribed her maid to admit him in the dark to M ixah's cabin, upon a certain night when her husband was to be absent; that the unexpected return of M.jor War- field, who had tracked him to the house, had pre- vented the success of hisevil purpose; tint had not saved the reputation of the innocent wife, whose infurated husband would not believe her ignorant of the presence of the villain in her house.-' that he, Gabriel Le Noir, in hatred as well as in sham-, had forborne until now to make the explanation, which he hoped might now, late in life as it was, bring the long severed pair together, and establish Miirah Rocke and her son in their legal and social rights. The second item in the black list of crime was the death of his elder brother, whom he declared he had not intended to kill. He said that, having contracted large debts which he was unable to pay, he had returned secretly from his distant quarters to demand the money from his brother, who had often helped him; that, meeting his brother in the woods, he made this request. Eugene reproached him for his extravagance and folly, and refused to aid him; an encounter ensued, in which Eugene fell. He Gabriel Le Noir, fled, pursued by the curse of Cain, reached his own quarters before even his absence had been suspected. His agency in the death of his brother was not suspected even by his accomplice in other crimes, the outlaw called Black Donald, who, thinking to gain an ascendancy over one whom he called his patron, falsely pre- tended to have made way with Eugene Le Noir for the sake of his younger brother 1 The third item of confession was the abduction of the nurse and babe of the young widow of Eugene, the circumstances of which are already known to the reader. The fourth in the dreadful list comnrised the deceptions, wrongs and precautions ptact sad upon Madam Eugene Le Noir, and the final false impri- sonment of that lady under the charge of insanity, in the private mad-house kept by Doctor Pierre St. Jean, in East Feliciana. In conclusion, he spoke of the wrongs done to Clara Day, whose pardon, with that of others, he begged. And he prayed that in conbideration of his son, as little publicity as was possible might be given to these crimes. Daring the reading of this confession, the eyes of Traverse Roche were fixed in wonder and half inorsdulitt upon the face of Herbert, and at its conclusion he said: "What a mass of crime! But that we may not</p>
        <p>dare-i-c,...ciu-esii-O-n-tii-e-M;r-ci of the Lord, I should ask if these were sins that He would ever pardon! Herbert, it appalls me to think of it!" Then, after deep thought, he added: "This, then, was the secret of my dear mother's long unhappiness! She was Major Warfield's for- saken wife I—Herbert I I feel as though I never, never, could forgive my father!" "Traverse, if Major Warfield had wiliully and wantonly forsaken your mother, I should say that your resentment was natural and right 1 Who should be an honourable woman's champion if not ker own son? But Major Warfield, as well as his wife, was more sinned against that sinning I Your parents were both victims of a cruel conspiracy, and he suffered as much in his way, as she did in hers," said Herbert. "I always thought, somehow, that my dear mother was a forsaken wife. She never told me so; but there was something about her circumstances and manners, her retired life, her condition, so much below her deserts, her never speaking of her husband's death—which would have been natural for her to do, had she been a widow—all, somehow, went to give me the impression that my father had abandoned us. Lately I had suspected that Major Wai field had something to do with the sad affair, though / never once suspected him to be my father I—so much for natural instincts," said Tra- verse, with a melancholy smile. "Traverse," said Hernert, with a design of draw- ing him off from sad remembrances of his mother's early trials. "Traverse, this confession, signed and witnessed as it is, will wonderfully simplify your cause of action in regard to the deliverance of Madam Le Noir." "Yes; so it will," said Traverse, with animation. "There will be no need now of applying to law; especially if you will come down with me to East Feliciana and bring the confession with you." "I will set out with you this very morning, if you wish, as I am on leave. What 1 to hasten the release of Capitola's mother 1 I would set out at midnight, and ride straight on for a week 1" "Ah I there is no need of such etxravagant feats of travel. It is now ten o'clock; if we start within an hour we can reach the "Calm Retreat" by eleven o'clock to-night." "En avant, then," exclaimed Herbert, rising and ringing the bell. Traverse ordered horses, and in twenty minutes Vie friends were on the road to East Feliciana. They reached the "Calm Retreat " so late that aighl, that there was none but the porter awake to admit them.</p>
        <p>Traverse took his friend up to his own dormitory, saying, laughingly: "It is an unappreciable distance of time since you and I occupied the same bed, Herbert." "Yes; but it is not the first, by five hundred times. Da you remember, Traverse, the low attic where we used to sleep, and how on stormy nights we used to listen to the 'rain pattering on the roof, hJtwQ o'tbautnQkeQf4_uy flices, and how</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>(Ma hurricane was smoking nits pipe ana reacting his paper. Cap was siting with her slender fingers around her throat, which she, with a shudder, occasionally compressed. "Well, that demon Black Donald will be hanged the 2tith of Jilly," said old Hurricane, exultingly, "and we shall get rid of one villain, Cap." "1 pity 13‘aek Donald, and I can't bear to think of his being hanged! It quite breaks my heart to think that I was compelled to bring him to such fate 1" "Otfl that reminds me I The reward offered for the apprehen,ion of Muck Din aid, o which you were eui,iticd, Cap, was p ii over to m t for youo I placed it to your account in the Agricultura Bank."</p>
        <p>"I don't want it! won't touch it! The price of blood. Is ould burn my fingers said Cap. "On, very wel I a thousand dollars won't go a beving," said Ol'i Hurricane. U:icle, it breaks mv heart to think of Back ! I just does 1 I', must be orea.iful, tots hangiii 1 I have put my fingers - ,r, anl q z,id it, to know boa it 'feels, and it is awful! Even a little sqo. makes my bead fei 1 as if it would burst, 'and I have to let gel 01,it Is horrible to think oi 1' 11, C p, it wasn't intended to be as pleasant as tickling you know! I wish it was twe-nty times worse I It would serve him right, the villain I I wish it was lawful to break him on the wheel—l do l'' " Ui.cle, that is very wicked in you! I declare I won't have it! I'll write a petition to tht• Cl wernor to commute his s.intence, and carry it all around the courtry myself 1' "You woulcn't get a soul to sign it to save your life, much less li,s." "I'll go to the Governor myself, and beg him to pardon Donald Bayne!" "Ha! ha! ha! the Governor would not do it to save all our lives; and if he were to do uith an out- rageous thing, be might whistle for his le-election 1" "I declare, Donald Bayne shall not be hung— and so there!" slid Cap, passionately. "Who-evil You'll dEliver him by the strength of your arm, my little Donna Quixote." "I'll save him in one way or another, now mind I tell you! He sinned more againsl me than against anybody else, and so I have the best right of any- body in the world to forgive him, and I do forgive him! And he shan't be hung! I say it !" " You Fay it 1 ha 1 hal ha! Who are you, to turn aside the law 2"</p>
        <p>"I, Capitola Black, say that Donald Bayne, not having deserved to be hung, shall not be hung 1 And in one way or another I'll keep my word 1" And C,p did her best to keep it. The next morning she mounted G p and rode up to Tip Top, where she employed the village lawyer to draw up a petition to the Governor for the commutation of D )nald Blyne's sentence. Ad then she rode all over the country to try to get signatures to the document. Bat all in vain! People of every age and condition too thoroughly feared and hated the famous outlaw, and too earnestly wished to be entirely and forever rid of him, to sign any petition for a, commutation of his sentence. If a petition for his instar t execution had been carried around, it would have stood a much better chance of suc- cess!</p>
        <p>Cap spent many days in her fruitless enterprise, but at labt gave it up—but by no means in despair, for— _ _</p>
        <p>"I'll save his life, yet! by one means or another I can't change alothes with him as I did i-h Clara, he's too big! but one way or other, I'll save him,' said Cap, to herself. She said it to no one else, for the more difficult the enterprise, the more deter- mined khe was to succeed, and the more secretive she grew as to her measures. In the meantime the outlaw, double-irened, was confined in the condemned cell, the strongest Portion of the county jail. All persons were strictly prohibited from visiting him, except certain of the clergy. They did all they could to bring the outlaw to a sense of his condition, to prepare him to meet his fate and to induce him to make a confession and give up the retreat of his band. And Donald listened to them with respect, acknowledged himself a great sinner, and knelt with them when they knelt to pray for him. But he denied that he was guilty of the murders for which he had been doomed to die, and he utterly refused to give up his old companions, replying to the ministers in something like these words:</p>
        <p>"Poor wretches; they are no more fit to die than am, and a condemned cell, with the thought of the scaffold before him, are not exactly the most favourable circumstances under which a man might experience sinctre repentance, my masters 1" And so, while the convict listened with docility to all that the ministers had to say, he steadily per- sisted in asserting his own innocence of the crimes for which he was condemned, and in his refusal to deliver up his companions. Meantime, Capitola, at Hurricane Hall, was doing all she could to discover or invent means to save the life of Mack Donald. But still she said no More about i_t, even to Old Hurricane. One evening, while Cap was sittting by the fire with her thoughts busy with this subject, her uncle came in, saying: "Cap 1 .I have got some curiosities to show you I"</p>
        <p>"W6at are the 7" said Cap, languidly. "A set of burglar's tools, supposed to belong to some member of Black Donald's band! One of my tegroee found them in the woods in the neighbour- hood of the Devil's Punch Bowl! I wrote to the sheriff concerning _them, and he requested me to take care of them until he should have occasion to tall for them. Look I did you ever see such things ?" said Old Hurricane, getting down a canvas bag upon the table, and turning out from it all sorts of strange looking instruments—tiny saws, files, punches, screws, picks, etc., etc., etc. Cap looked at them with the most curious Interest, while Old Hurricane explained their sup- posed uses. "It must have been an instrument of this sort, Cap, that that blamed demon, Donald, gave to the Imprisoned men to file their fetters off with 1" he said, showing a thin file of tempered steel. "That 1" said Cap, "hand it here I let me see it 1" and she eximined it with the deept-s' interest. "I wonder what they force locks with I" she Inquired. “Why, this, and this, and this 1" said Old Hurri- Car e, producing a burglar's pick, saw, and chisel. Cap took them and scrutinised them so attentively that Old Hurricane burst out into a loud laugh, "You'll dream of house-breakers to-night, Cap 1" and taking the tools he put them all back in the little canvas bag, and put the bag up on a high shelf of the parlour closet. The next morning, while Cap was arranging flowers on the parlour mantlepiece, Old Hurricane burst in upon her with his hands full of letters and newspapers, and his heart full of exultation— throwing up his hat and cutting an alarming caper for a man of his age, he exclaimed: "Hurrah, Cap! Harrah 1 Peace is at last pro- claimed and our victorious troops are on their way home! It's all in the newspapers! and here are letters from Herbert, dated from New Orleans! Here are letters for you, and here are some for me! I have not opened them yet! Hurrah, Cap I Hurrah 1" "Hurrah, uncle! Hurrah 1" cried Cap, tossing up her flowers and rushing into his arms 1 "Don't squeeze me into an apoplexy, you little bear," said OW Hurricane, turning purple in the face, from the savage bug of Cap 'S joyful arms. "Come aleng and sit down with me, at this table, and let us see what the letters have brought us." They took th, ir seats opposite each other, at a small table, and Oid Hurricane threw the whole mail between them, and began to pick out the letters. "That's for you, Cap. This is for me," he said, pitching out two in the handwriting of Herbert Greys n Cap opened hers, and commenced reading. It was in tact Herhert's first downright practical proposal cif mer.:ege, in which he begged that their union might t,ke [.):,cg as soon as he should return, and that as he had written to his uncle by the same mail, upon another subject, which be did not wish to mix up with his own iterriage. she would, upon a proper opportbnity, let her uncle 'snow of their plii Lig "Upon my word, he takes my consent very coolly ase matter of course, and even forces upon me the diseereeeble duty of asking myself of my uncle! Whoever heard of such proceedings? If he were not coming home from the wars, I declare I should get angry; but I won't get upon my dignity with Herhert,--dear, darling, sweet Herbert —if it wete anybody else, shouldn't they know the difference Lel- wren their liege lady one Tom Trotter However, as it's Herbert, here goes ! Now, I suppose the best way to as, myself of uncle, for Herbert, will be jute to hard him Over this letter. The dear knows it isn't so over and above :-.ffectionate thet Is} ciii heeitate. Uncle," said Cap, pulling Old Hurr'eant "</p>
        <p>t f.leeve</p>
        <p>"Don't tir...Fitm- nin„ Op," exclaimed Major War field, who vr4t Tht,,,ro hoidip.a• wzitt en</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>crocnment in ms nano, with his great round eyes strained from their sockets, as they passed along the lines with devouring interest. ." Well, I do declare! I do believe he has received a proposal of marriage himself," cried Cap, shooting much nearer the truth than she knew. Old Hurricane did not bear her. Starting up with the document in his hand, he rushed from the room, and went and shut himself up in his own study. "I vow, some widow has offered to marry him," said Cap, to herself. Old Hurricane did not come to dinner, nor to supper. Bat after supper, when Capitola's wonder was at its climax, and while she was sitting by the little wood fire that the chilly evening required, Old Hurricane came in, looking very unlike himself, in an humble, confused, deprecating, yet happy manner, like one who had at once a mortilying coniession to make, and a happy secret to tell. "O -p," he said, trying to repress a smile, and growing nurplc in the face. --" Oh, yes! you've come to tell me, I suppose, you're going to put a step-aunt-in-law over my head, only you don't know how to announce it,' answered Capitola, little knowing how closely she had come to the truth; when to her unbounded astonishment, Old Hurricane answered: "Yes, my dear, that's just it I" " WHAT I My eyes! Oh crickey !" cried Cap, breaking into her newsboy's slung, from mere con- sternation. "Yes, my dear, it is perfectly true I" replied the old man, growing furiously red, and rubbing his face. "Oh I oh! oh! HOLD rI I'M KILT!" cried Cap, falling back in her chair in an inextinguisha:ile fu of laughter, that shook her whole frame. She laughed till the tears ran down her cheeks. She wip.zd her eyes and looked at Old Hurricane, and every time she saw his confused and happy face, she burst into a fresh paroxysm that seemed to threaten her life or her reason. "Who is the happy--, Oh, I can't speak. Oh, I'm kilt entirely l'" she cried, breaking off in the midst of her question, and falling into fresh con- valsior S.</p>
        <p>"It's no new love,Cap. It's my old wife 1" said Old Hurricane, wiping his face. This brought Capitola up with a jerk I She sat bolt upright, gazing at him with her eyes fixed as if in death. "Cap," said Old Hurricane, growing more and more confused, "I've been a married man more years than I like to think of 1 Cap, I've—l've a wife and grown-up son 1 What do TOU sit there staring at me for, you little demon 7 Why don't you say something to encourage me, you little wretch 2"</p>
        <p>_ "Go on," said Cap, without removing her eyes. "Cap, I was—a jealous—passionate—Demmy confession isn't in my line! A diabolical villain made me believe that my poor little wife wasn't good 1" " There I I knew you'd lay it on somebody else. Men always do that I" said Cap to herself. "He was mortally wounded in Mexico. He made a confession, and confided it to Herbert, who has jmt sent me an attested copy. It was Le Noir. My poor wife lived under her girlhood's name of Mandl Rocke." Old Hurricane made a gulp, and his voice broke down. Cap understood all now, as well as if she had known it as long as old Hurricane had. She com- prehended his extreme agitation upon a certain evening, years ago, whey Herbert Greyson had men- tioned Marah Rocke's name, and his later and more lasting disturbance upon accidentally meeting Marah at the Orphans' Court. This revelation tilled her with strange and con- tradictory emotions. She was glad; she was angry with him; she was sorry for him; she was divided between d.vers impulses to hug and kiss him, to cry over him, and to seize him and give him a good shaking! And between them she did nothing at all. OH Hurricane was again the first to speak. "What was that you wishedlo say to me, Cap. when I ran away from you this morning 2" "Why, -uncle, that Herbert wants to follow your example, and—and—and—" Cap blushed and broke down. "I thought as much. Getting married at his age! a boy of twenty-five 1" said the veteran in contempt. "Taking a wife at your age, uncle an infant of sixty-six 1" "Bother, Cap! Let me see the fellow's letter to you." Cap handed it to him, and the old man read it. "If I were to object, you'd get married all the same! Denamy I you're both of age. Do as you please." "Thank you, sir," said Cap, demurely. "And now, C ip, ene thing is to be noticed. Herbert says, both in your letter and in mine, that they were to start the day after these letters were posted. These letters have een delayed in the mail. Consequently we may expect our hero here every (14. Bte Cap, my dear, you must receive them. For to-morrow morning, please the Lord, I shall set out for Staunton and Willow Heights, ad go and kneel down at the feet of my wife, and ask her pardon on my knees 1" cav, was no longer divided bet_weeil the wish to pull O'd Hurricaue's grey beard and to cry over him. She threw heraelf at once into his arms and excleimE d : "OJ uncle! God bless you! God bless you! God °less you It has come very late in life, but may you be happy with her through all ages of eternity." 0:d Hurricane was deeply moved by the sympathy of his little madcap, and pressed her to his bosom, saying: "Cap, my dear, if you had not set your heart upon Herbert, I would marry you to my son Traverse, and you two should inherit all that I have in the world! But never mind, Cap, you have an inheritance of your own! Cap, Cap, my dear, did it ever occur to you that you might have had a father and mother 2"</p>
        <p>11- Yes 1 often! But I used to think you were my father, ani that my mother was dead." "I wisn to the Lord that I had been your father, Cap, and that Marah Rocke had been your mother! But Cap, your father was a better man than I, and your mother as good a woman as Marah. And Cap, my dear, you vagabond, you vagrant, you brat, you beggar, you are the sole heiress of the Hidden House estate and all its enormous wealth 1 What do you think of that now, what do you think of that, you beggar I" cried Old Hurricane. A shriek pierced the air, and Capitola starting up, stood before Old Hurricane, crying In an impassioned voice: " Uncle ! Uncle! don't mock me ! don't overwhelm me Ido not care for wealth or power; but tell me of the parents who possessing both, cast off their unfortunate child—a girl too! to meet the suffer- ings and perils of such a iife as mine had been, if I had not met you 1"</p>
        <p>"C .p, my dear, hush! your parents were no more to blame for their seeming abandonment of you, than I was to blame for the desertion of my poor wife. We are all the victims of one villain who has now gone to his account, Capitola. I mean Gabriel Le Noir. Sit down my dear, and I will read the copy of his whole confession, and afterwards, in addition, tell you all / know upon the subject 1" C ieitola resumed her seat, and Major Warfield read the confession of Gabriel Le Noir, and after- wards continued the subject by relating the events of that memorable Hallow Eve when ho was called out in a snow storm to take the dying deposition of the nurse who had been abducted with the infant Capitola.. And at the end of his narrative, Cap knew as much of her owe history as the realer has known all along. "A u I have a mother! and I shall see her soon! you iold we she was cow ne home with the party --did you not, Ilacte V said C4pitola. " Y my cold. ody think of it? / saved the daughter from the streets of N.:3W York, and my son s4ve,: bile mother from her prison at the madhouse! A id now my nnt C sp, I mast bid you good night and go to bed, for I intend to rise to-morrow morn- ing Infer before daylight, to ride to Tip Top to meet the St tnun stage," said the old man, kissing C J Ist as he was about to leave the room, he was arre-ted by a loud ringing and knocking at the door. W ,o 1 was heard mailing along the front hall to answer the summons. "C p, I shouldn't wonder much if that was our party. I wish it may be, for I should like to wel- come them before I leave home to fetch my wife," sale Old Hurricane, in a voice of agitation. A Id. while they were still eagerly listening, the do', -7as thrown open by Wool, who announced: e e, H rbert, which I mean to say, M .jor II: G on ;" and H.-3rbert entered and was geisped te the two haues of Old Hurricane who EXChime, • : Herbert, ray lad! I have got your letters. It is ail right. Herbert, or going to be so. You shall marry Cip when you like, And I am going to- merrow morning to throw myself at the feet of my wife."</p>
        <p>"No need of your goiug so far, dear sir, no need. Let me speak to my own dear girl a moment, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>then I shall have something to say to you," Herbert, leaving the old man in suspense, and gnii'D to salute Capitola, who returned his fervent embrace by an honest, downright frank kiss, that made 116' secret of itself. " Capitola I My in:14..1e has told you all?" "Every single bit! so don't lose time by telling it all over 'wain I Is my mother wEli you 7" " Yes ! and I will bring her in, in one moment ; first I must bring in some one else," said II irberti kissing the hand of CApitola and turning to 01 Hurricane, to whom he said: "You need not trrtvel far to find M irah. toek Staunton in our way, and brought her ee,a Clara along—Traverse I" he said, going to t/59 door—" bring in your mother." And the next instant, Traverse entered with tile wife of Mijor AVarfield upon his arm. Old Hurricane started forward to meet bet' exclaiming in a broken voice: "Marab, my dear M trah, Gad way forgive ree'd but can you—can you ever, do so 2" nI he Wool have sunk at her feet, but that she prevented. meeting him and silently placing both her handol_: his. And so quietly Marah's for_iveness vlv expressed, and the reconciliation sealed. Meanwhile Herbert went out, and brouglat 113 Mrs. Le Noir and Gera. Mrs. L Noir with Frenchwoman's impetuosity, hurried to her date ter, and clasped her to her heart. Cap gave one hurried glance at the beautiful p 518 woman that claimed from her a d Inghter's love, and then, returning the caress, she said: "Oh, mamma! Oh, mamma! if I were onlY! boy instead of a girl, I would thrash that be NO',, within an inch of his life! But I forgot! he is Oa" to his account." Old Hurricane was at this moment shaking hah6g with his son, Traverse, who presently took occasi°! to lead up and introduce his betrothed wife, Cl3r" Day, to her destined father-in-law. Major Warfield received her with all a soldier! gallantry, a gentleman's courtesy and a f.,,ther'' tendern c Rs. He next shook hands with his old acquaintance' Mrs. Le Noir. And then supper was ordered, and the eveoll,l was passed in general and comparative reraiLO aances and cheerful conversation. (To be continued).</p>
