        <p>SIJ LT OF IJ'N HAPPY MARRIAGE, DIVORCE PROCEEDINGS, &amp;c.. perhaps the most lavourable opportunity ever offered to these about furnishing k'a Rood style is the present. The furnishing in question has 'Leen removed from a very la ge house in the vicinity of Ply- 'Mouth Grove, and everything was made to order only 14 Months ago by one of the best houses in the city. The prices (Inoted are exactly one-half the cost. Nothing like a full des- can be given. Inspection is invited. Firstly. the all is furnished with a grand dark mahogany Louis Stand !nd pair chairs to match; price 9 guineas. The dining-room in solid black walnut of magnificent ant uncommon gn. To attempt anything like a description would be a 'a'aste of time ;to say the least. it is the most charming and Unique, the dining table extending with extra leaves. sup- Ported by square legs which are beautifully carved ; the over- Udantel is such that, can only be made by workmen of the 'Lighest standard ;itis a work of art. Next comes the truly Magnificent Suite, which, of course, are also in solid black alnut, and consists of 12 standard Chairs (the price quoted 1s for six chairs, more can be had if required). witbdouble- Padded tacks, two extra large lounge chairs, and a grand conch of large size, sideboard alone is worth £5O Os 9d. the tlole forming a noble lot for- high-class dining-room. Price guineas. hare also the carpets, paintings, and engravings from ;Ile same room. The drawing room is furnished in the Latest style, and surpasses everything for elegance I have fter seen. Firstly, the Carlo display cabinet at once ttrikes you RS being a marvel of beauty and workmanship Possesses all the requirements o, such an artistic article -xt comes the handsome music cabinet with face fron ‘t‘nrl various divisions. lined with plush; two curious shape ables. with fancy cut and carved legs; the overmanted With diamond-shaped glasses, and recesses for the display nick-nacks ; lastly, but by no means the least, the truly In,agnificent suite of double gondola settee, for any part of 11e room ; two charming easy chairs, four occasional chairs. 4... and two gossipy chairs. all Hre upholstered in a rich silk ',Destry, with raised flowers and sprays, and finished with aak cord and gimp. It may be remarked that these fittings are suitable for only a high-class drawing-room. There are in Z 1 nine bedrooms, space prevents all being described. The t. St room of all, I may here state, is suitable for a lady of title or a lord it cost 12 and is offered for 60gs. The suite iniply magnificent, the wood is perfectty solid bright- 'shed walnut of the choicest quality, the wardrobe is wide, fitted at either end with hanging compartments 4.,nich are enclosed with brilliant bevelled Vaxhan plate glass °1 shield shape, between the doors are drawers, and above e bay-shaped cupboards, with massive carved panels, which 51°size :,isplays great dexterity with the chisel. the whole is '.neti with. a cornice of tower like shape,the dressing table tl'it 6in, wide, pdestal design, and 6ay-shaped between (' Pedestals, which are fitted with drawers, the glass, which f,„"Pported by carved brackets, is shield shape and extra Vse- Next comes the 4lt 6in bay-shaped washstand, with rble slab, tile, and gl as back, about 4ft high, the pedestal ,°ll‘,Yel rail, and u; holstered chairs forms one of the finest money can buy. There are eight other bedrooms 4„Llch contain suites at all prices, some as low as 4 guineas some at 14, 18, and 25 guineas. B he suite from the breakfast room is in saddle bags, price hquineas ; dining table, 355; sideboard. £7 15s; two recesses I)okeases. 75s each. Another carvesloak bookcase and a host uptLi other aftieles are on view. Carpets, rugs, fenders, orna- h:uts- paintings. china, and glass, all removed to 44. Old- 'ln-street, Manchester. •</p>
