      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE, The principal point to which attention shoula be paid when deciding on a hat for cycling is that it should permit of being fixed firmly on the head—a plain sailor shape, similar to a man's "boater," is perhaps best of all, and next to this comes the boat-shape. A band of ribbon, a bow, and perhaps a stiff wing form all the trinanin,g permissible; flowers and feathers are as mit of place as are "jam pot" and " hour-glass " crowns and other eccentricities in the way of head-gear. A rather wide elastic should keep the hat on, but if the hair is dressed high a pin through the crown is some- times necessary as an additional safeguard. The use of hat pins is not devoid of danger in case of a hOwever.</p>
        <p>, My reference to hats reminds me—for the dash- ing off at a tangent you must blame the headgear and not me—that an extremely effective sample which I saw a few days ago was a quite simple shape of Panama, trimmed right round the crown with a ruche of white glac6 silk ribbon edged with narrow black velvet ribbon, an aigrette of white cock's feathers stood up on one side, and a rosette of pale green ribbon was tucked in on the other. The hat might be copied in almost any material, and it would not in the least matter if the founda- tion were of an ordinary coarse straw. Another very pretty specimen which met my eye was a toque composed of a crown of rose foliage and a border of pink velvet roses, with a high spray of birds, foliage and green grass at one side.</p>
        <p>Shirts with removable fronts are a novelty, and quite as useful as -skirts with detachable collars. As a shirt material, grass lawn is admirable, because of its neutrality of texture and tone. To one gross-lawn shirt there may be many fronts. Cream-coloured muslin edged with frillings of butter-coloured lace, and divided by three narrow rows of black velvet ribbon converging. at the waist, and worn with white linen collar and cuffs, black neck-tie and belt, looks well with grass-lawn; but pink, blue, and green muslin are pretty with it also; whilst fronts of grass lawn with rows of white embroidery worn with frilled collars and cuffs of soft book-muslin and waistband and neck- bow of heliotrope satin ribbon, are very quakerish and demure.</p>
        <p>You will be expecting, no doubt, some reference to the latest modes in gowns. One which was pointed out to me just lately was a very smart arrangement in pale lime,green alpaca, the coat being ornamented with gold buttons and edged by a frill of black chiffon while the waist was girt with a folded band of black satin. Another gown of a work-a-day sort was a navy blue silk alpaca with draped vest of ivory silk crepe, flowered' with dull green and cornflower blue. Between the hard edge of the blue bodice and this vest intervened a narrow pinked outer vest of white cloth prettily jewelled. A distinguishing feature of the gown were the orna- mental strappings on the sleeves, the basque, the collar, and the skirt. Some charming costumes for bridesmaids demand description. The skirts are of pale pink glace silk, the bodices of the thinnest ivory chiffon, closely tucked, and mounted over pink silk. Then there is an arrangement of silver pearl and turquoise embroidery, which is caught in at the waist. The hats are of white straw, feathers and chiffon, lined with pink lisse. The bride's dress was of white crepe de chine made over white glace silk and trimmed ith a collar of old point lace, and a eorslet of siivev worked with pearls and diamonds. The train of white twat; fell from both shoulders.</p>
        <p>Bo(lices. are truly one of the very best inventiims of the • modistc., and there are some really lovely examples going just now. I admit to having felt quite envious at a recent fashionable function when I noticed the exquisite taste displayed in many instances. One of the very smartest of the blouses was of heliotrope chine silk of the =aye shape. and it was trimmed with fine cream lace appliqué.. The front was of lime tucked, and insertion of lace. • Your loving Jennie:</p>
        <p>It appears from a comparison a Bank Holiday traffic returns with those of last year that 5,870 more pasBen- gers were carried on the Liverpool Overhead Railway, 10,000 on the Wallasey Ferries, and 9,791 on the Wood- side Ferries.</p>
        <p>A Chicago telegram of Tuesday announces the failure Messrs. Moore Brothers, financiers and promoters ot Diamond Match Company,of New York,and several biscuit companies,owing to unremunerative speculation. T he loss is estimated at 24010001000d015.</p>
