      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The statement that has appeared in some of the Len don papers of late, to the effect that at one time a mar- riage was almost arranged between the Emperor of Reesia and Prencese Maud, could not passible' he a cor- rect one, as first and foremost, members af the Orthodox Greek Church are not allowed to marry their first cousins. Moreover the Czar was always intended to marry one of the'daughters of the Prince of Montenegro, but the match went off for various reasons. By the way, many of my readers may hot be aware of the fact that the Czarina unites in her own person, the nmea of all the Queen's danehtere. She was baptised Victoria, Alice, Helena, Louise, Beatrice. There is little doubt Pope Leo XIII has very strong predilections in faveur of England.and hi beautiful gift to Princess Maud on ker weeding goes further to prove it, This is the first time, for some centuries, that the Vatican has taken notice of an English Royal wedding, a fact which has caused a great deal of comment in eccles- iastical zircles.—The gift consisted of an antique gold bracelet, which contains a cameo cut most artistically, and was accompanied by a congratulation and very gracious wishes for the future happiness of both bride and bridegroom. His Holiness, who is between eighty and ninety years of age, affords as remarkable an in. stance of mental activity in extreme old ego as Mr. Gladstone himself, for besides his attention to the weighty duties of his office, the Pope is deeply interested in literature and art, and dabbles in poetical composi- tion—Only a short time back he wrote a graceful and spirited Latin hymn, which was set to music, and sung in the Sistine chapel. It appara that the health of Prince George of Cum- herland,the eldest son of the 5 oungest sister of the Prin- cess of Wales, is again causing great anxiety to his rela- tives. He has, from time to time, undergone severe operation, and only lately it was found necessary to operate still more extensively. In the event of a relapse rit is ..aid no hope of his recovery could possibly be en- teitained. The question of constructing a new Royal Yacht is again "under consideration;" it frequently is, and as frequently comes to nothing. That archaic Noah's Ark, the Victoria and Maud, and the tin tub euphemistically called the Royal Yacht Osborne are scarcely fit for a Passage across the Solent, and as a matter of fact there is nothing in the royal fleet fit to andertake a month's cruise in Norwegian waters, to convey a royal party to Copenhagen or to St. Petersberg, to run round to Athens, or to spend a fortnight in the Western Hebrides Lady Henry Somerset who recently celebrated her birthday lb the elder daughter of the late Earl Somers. On the death of her father, she inherited much pro- perty and two fine estates. Lady Henry is a very able speaker and has spent much time and mcney in the cause of temperance. She married many yeart ago, Lord Henry Somerset, who is still veiy much alive though people are apt to forget the fart. Owing to certain scandals however, in which Lord Henry was im- plicated, his wife separated from him and he found it convenient to live abroad. Their o ly son, Mr. Henry Semers Somerset, married a short time back, Lady Katherine Beauclerk, the fourth daughter of the Duke of St. Albans. . Al! of us who have enjoyed the exquisite playing of M. Paderewski.will be glad to know that at last a great London specialist has given some hope of a permanent cure of the special complaint from which his only son has so long been a sufferer. By the way M. Faders- wski's agent has just acknowledged how much money the distinguished pianist earned during his five months tour in America. It comes to £50,000. M. Paderewski it appears, has become passionately fond of Chinese music, and is full of admiration for its remarkable aim- nlicity and intensity, the result of centuries of elabora- tion. He says, with regard to its dramatic power, that it is not necessary to understand the words in a Chinese theatre, the music tells the story. Dress lovers will not have failed to notice thattrimmed or unduly wide skirts have been carefully avoided in Princess Charles' trousseau. Her Royal Highness has one of those small, finely proportioned little figures which would be overweiehted with trimmed or draped skirts.and therefore the Princess keeps to the style she likes best and which she knows is best suited to her. If only middle class women would follow the example in this matter,ef those of highest rank,it would be an item to be grateful for,aii it is to them, in great degree, that we owe grotesque exaggeration. Once let them be con- vinced that a certain thing is really new, and recognised as such, they are determined to show all and i.undry that they are aware of the fact, and regardless as to whether the mode suits them or not,they indulge in it to such an extent, that it is no longer recognisable. Pendant watches are still correct wear, and green, violent, blue,red and heliotrope are the favourite colours for enamel ones. The turqueise is tbe favourfte stone of the moment and long gold chains are much eit evi- dence; but it is not good corm to load them with trinkets as I have noticed many women doing of late. For millinery purposes, one of the new autumn manu- factures is a mixture of chenille and felt, forming a sort of braid, es hich is to take the place of straw. It has the advantage of being able to stand any amount of hard wear while looking extremely dressy. Zeuaves, boleros and Eton coats are übiquitous. I espied sow+ especially smart little boleros last week opening inftlwav up the back, built in astrachan. They had high medici collars of chinchilla, and wide revers of embroidered velvet. Unless a very severe change in the weather takes place these bid fair to carry us to the end ef the autumn. Chinchilla is one of the features in autumn millinery. Its fineness and softness make i a very favourite fur. A spirited discussion is going on in one of the London pspers just new as to whether widows should, or should not, marry again. Evidently the " silly season " has once more come round and I suppose we shall have many more discussions of a similarly unanswerable des- criotion. There are' hundreds of bereaved women whose only hope for happiness lies in their taking another com- panion, indeed it is often the case that the happier a women has been in her first marrittee, the quicker she is, on becoming a widow, to make a second. On the othe:r hand the view held by the Queen, and the Empress Frederick is very beautiful and ideal ; a view evidently shared by Princess Henry of Battenbilig who wrote " Till death us do part, till, death *us do unite again.' upon the wreath which she placed on her husband's' erffin. Circumstances, however, alter cases, and it would he quite imnossible to lay down a hard and fast rule cencerning second marriages, if we could go on discussing the subject from now till Doomsday. I never could see any use in books of the" How to keep house on sixpence a day," or " How I dressed like g. Princess on a shilling a week,"tyne. School figures can be made to prove anything,and nothing is more delusive than the average" table" of household expenses. One frequently finds some imnartant. necessary item left nut altogether, and several shillings devoted to dainties quite beyond the ordinary middle-class housekeeper, while the proportions of the different articles of food and drink, compel one to marvel at the tastes and digestive capabil- itieß of the supposed consumer. Individual tastes must always differ so widely, that books of the type I have mentioned are positively worthless. A good sound education in everything appertaining to household matters is what is really necessary for every middle class girl, then her own experience and knowledge will teach her far better than all the books ever written, how to arrange the cum set apart for housekeeping,and her own dress Powa nee.</p>
        <p>STEAMED BATTER PUDDING.—PIace half-a-pound of flour in a basin and add to it a good pinch of salt. Make a hole in the centre of the flour,break in separately two eggs, and add a little of one pint of milk. Stir in the flour. slowly and smoothly, and keep adding the milk till hPlf of it is used. Then beat the batter well for ten minutes and add the rest of the milk. Well grease a pudding basin, pour in the batter and cover with a floured cloth. Then place the basin in a saucepan with boiling water to come halfway up the basin, and steam it for one and a half hours. Serve with jam or stewed fruit. .</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
        <p>DANGERS OF DELAY.</p>
        <p>A Flagman upnn the Railway delayed—the express rushed nn and a score of lives was lost. A nnrse girl neglected the child in the park and the</p>
        <p>little one was drowned. A father was so busy he had no time to think of him- self, and so common symptoms ran into a fatal disease. A mother was so absorbed by her children that she did not notice her own danger until it was too late. A million people neglect and delay, when, if they only realised it, their only happiness and very lives are at stake.</p>
        <p>A PaVt; in the hack, Lassitude, Melancholy, Faintness, Irritability, Prostration, all indicate a serious kidney difficulty. A Heartache, Coated Tonne, Irrezularities, Cold Feet, Weak Pulse, Constipation, unmistakabiy prove de- ranged kidnors or liver. _ _</p>
        <p>A delay or ne2lect at such R time may mean everything</p>
