      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <p>THE EX%MINI. s NT!' liI)AY, AtTGUST 8, 1896.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <p>PT UMP AGAINST A BIG FACT.</p>
        <p>It is not properly any part of my business to enforce lessons in ethics ; therefore I commonly leave that res- ponsible task to those whose vecation it is. But no man can continually write on the subject which constitutes the burdens of these essays without now and then run- ning plump against a mighty fact in morals. If you will be good enough to read the following short letters I will then try to show how I was moved to speak as I have spoken. "My daughter Annie Jane," writes that young girl's mother," now five. years of age, was a fine healthy child up to March, 1891, wis en she began to sicken and tall away. She had no appetite and every particle of food he took came up. She lost strength rapidly and with- in a fortnight she was thin as a rake,being not much else than skin and bone. For days and days she laid in a half conscious condition, scarcely moving hand er foot, and to all appearance lifeless. I had a doctor attending her for four warks,and he said the child was suffering from indigestion, yet, so far as we could see,his treatmert had no effect. My husband and Land all that saw the poor baby,thought she was slowly dying,and we were almcst heart-broken at the thought of losing her. "Nothing that we gave her did the slightest good, and the child, was fading away,when one day, towards the end of April,a lady called, and after seeing Annie Jane,advised us to use Mother Seigel's Syrup. She said she had known the lives of many children saved by this medicine who were down with the same complaint. I hurried to get a bottle from Mr. Routly. the chemist, in Susan's-r oad, and began giving it in small doses. In less than twenty-four hours the child began to eat, the sickness stopped,and we could see a change for the bet- ter. We kept on giving the Syrup, and in two weeks Annie was as well as ever, and fast getting back her flesh. Since that time—now four years ago—she has never been ill. We consider that Mother Seigel's Syrup saved her life. You can publish this statement and re- fer anyone to me. (Signed) Mrs. Annie Alexander, 35, Melbourne-road, Eastbourne, AIIRUSt Ist, 1895." "My son Joseph," writes Mr. Joseph Bond, of Salter's Green, Mayfield. Sussex, "was never strong. He did not come on like other :children. He was weak, sickly, and puny. He ate but little,'and was usually in pain until he vomit( d most of it up again. Nothing gave him strength. In February, 1894, his feet and ankles began to fester. Next three abscesses formed on his neck and under the chin, making deep holes. He was merely skin and bone.. The abscesses seemed to be exhausting his ltfe's blood. He was in a doctor's care five month's, but got no better. From July (1894) he had four months treatment at the Tunbridge Wells Hospital, without benefit. The doctors gave him medi- cines and cod-liver oil, but nothing strengthened him. "In Decembe.r (1891) I concluded to take the ease into my own hands, and gave him a m edicine that had cured my wife—Mother Seigel's Syrup. To our astonish- ment and delight be began tdirnprove in a few days. He could eat and was stronger for it. We kept giving .him the Syrup, and be grew better every day. The absceases soon healed, and he is now a fine healthy boy, nine years old. and strong for the first time since he was born. Publish this letter if you wish and refer inquirers to me. (Signed) Joseph Bond. July 26:h, 1895." What,now,is that mighty fact in morals ? Ask yourself the question. What justice was there in the suffering of these two little children ? For whose sake was it ? Why do the majority of the human race die in infancy and childhood? That bundle of laws and forces called " nature " has no pity, no mercy. Obey and live ; dis- obey and perish, that's the whole story. Then how does Mother Seigel's Syrup cure ? It cures by bringing tha deceased and suffering body back where nature's hand can reach it. It puts the derailed coach back on the metals, it re launches the stranded ship The radical trouble of both Annie Alexander and Joseph Bond was of the digestion, the first (a mere baby then) having been seized with acute indigestion, and the boy having, as his father tells us, been born with a feeble stomach. Hence, in his case, the bad blood and the abscesses by which nature sought to remove it. Will parents take warning from these instances ? I hope so. Watch the little ones and use Moteer Seigel's Syrup whenever you see them inclined to droop or languish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>SHOCKING CRUELTY TO HORSES AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING CRUELTY TO HORSES AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>HEAVY PENALTIES IMPOSED</p>
        <p>On Friday, at the Warrington Borough Court before Alderman Harrison (in the chair), Councillors Pierpoint and Roberts. Dr. Gornall and Mr. S. Wright, Richard Haddock, 16, Percival's-fold, was charged with ill-treat- ing a horse by working it in an unfit state on the 17th July; Thomas Pilkington, Bewsey-road, was charged with causing it to be so worked.—Mr Pilkington was unable to appear.—lnspector Luckings, R.S.P.C.A., said that on the day named he was in Arpley when he saw the defendant Haddock in charge of a brown gelding attached to a heavy cart. The animal was very lame, and its foot had almost rotted away through canker. It was banging in flakes. Defendant admitted that it was in a dreadful state. Witness subsequently saw Mr. Pilkington who admitted sending it out.—Haddock said he had only obeyed orders, and was discharged, while Mr. Pilkington was fined £3 and costs, (£3 10s. 61 in all).</p>
        <p>William Burke, 35, Gee-street, wag charged with working a horse while unfit on the 18th July ; Frank Lee, 6, John-street, was charged with causing it to be worked.—lnspector Lnckings said that on the 18th inst. he was in the horse fair when he saw Burke riding in old black horse. The animal's mouth was bleeding very much and wiLess stopped defendant who was jag- ging the tr-Tse's mouth. On looking into its mouth he found under-its tongue,a deep laceration,4 inches long, and on each side of the cheek an abscess. The whole of the mouth was infilmed and in it was an old rusty bit which looked as if it had been exposed to the weather for a year or two. In answer to questions Burke said "I am only riding it and trying to sell it for Lee." The latter said the horse was his and that another bit caused the horse's bad mouth. By request be produced this bit and it was also very rust.—lnspector BiIscn,R.S.P.C.A. corroborated. He added that these cases frequently arose at the fairs —Burke said he was only on the horse a few minutes and the Bench dismissed him on paying the costs, but the conduct of Lee was condemned in strong terms by the Chairman,and he was fined £3 and costs.—At this feint Dr. Gornall objected entirely to the defendant having the chance of paying a fine. He thought he4ought to be committed to gaol. The ill- treatment of horses in Warrington was intolerable, he bad seen it himself.</p>
        <p>Thomas Sheridan, 22, Green-lane, Ormgkirk, was charged with cruelly beating a horse on the 18:h July.— Inspector Bilson said that on this date he was on the fair ground when he saw the defendant riding a black horse. It was galloping and Sheridan was severely thrashing it with a waxed whip stock. Witness examined the hone and found on its hind quarters a number of lumps almost the size of an egg and on its flanks were innumerable weals. It was perspiring profusely and quite exhausted.—lnspector Luckings added that a man complained to him about the treatment of a horse by de- fendant.—ln this case also a fine of £3 and costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>gelina Johnson, 59, Brick-street, St. Helens, was charged with working a horse in an unfit state on the 21st July. Selina Eckersley, her mother, wt.; charged with causing it to be worked.—Mr. Browne represented the defendants.—lnspector Bi!son said that at 4.45 on the 21st inst., he saw the .younger defendant in charge of an old pony in Itlam-st. It fell down nearly opposite the police station and was unable to rise, It had to be placed on its feet by bystanders. Witness then examined it and found on the point of the hip a wound about the size of a five shilling piece, raw and rubbed by the harness. Under the saddle and on the neck were two similar wounds both gilded by the harness. Witness got the girl to walk the horse a few steps, and found that it was lame from ringbene and badly sprained ten- dons. She said the horse belonged to her mother, and she had brought it from St. Helens, and was going to take it back again. He allow^4 her to walk it back without the cart. On the following day witness exa- mined the horse at St. Helens and found it in the same condition. Its stable was also in a very filthy state. The elder defendant thought it very hard indeed that they should stop her horse in the streets, and said she should continue to work it.—ln answer to Mr. Browne : He did not know that the pony was 16 hands high and should be surprised to hear that it was. He knew it had slipped down in the street. None of its wounds could have been ciused by the fall. It was not alwaye very difficult to get a horse up again. In this case the animal was too weak. He would swear that the horse was suffering from ringbone and strained tendons.—P.C. Rylett corrobor- ated as to the condition of the horse. He added that it took about six police officers to lift it np.—The Meat Inspector for the borough of St. Helens, said that he was in company with Inspector Bilston when he saw the</p>
        <p>Tits PUBLIC ARE WARNED against the use of Foreign cocoas conta.,ning alkoli, producing highly coloured prepara- tions offered as" pure eocoa." Cadbury's Cocoa is absolutely pure and unadulterated, and is therefore the beat. The " Lancet" describes it as" the standard of highest purity at present attainable,"—Advt.</p>
        <p>animal at St. Helens, and supported his statement.—Mr. Browne, for the defence, contended that the horse, not a pony, came fr•-m St. Helens alright, and fell in War- rington owing to the slippery nature of the setts, and its injuries were thus caused.—Mr. Day,veterinary surgeon Sr. Helens, had known the horse for some years, and its stable was close to his forge. He saw it on the Saturday morning and there was nothing wrong with it then. rho fact that Impector Bdson allowed it to walk home snowed that it was not so bad.—Mary Johnson, another daughter of the elder defendant, said the horse was quite sound when she and her sister left home. It fell down opposite the Catholic Church, Irlam-st., and was slightly lame when it was lifted up by the police —Mr. D ,y, veterinary surgeon, St. Helens. said he had known the horse five years. Defendants' house was close to his brge. He saw the horse on the 18th July, and it was not lame. On the 23rd he saw it again, atter the visit to Warrington, an,l it was not suffering from any- thing except a stiff shoulder as the result of the fall. There was a slight abrasion on the nesk and the horse had a contracted foot, but he was sure it was not suffering from ringbone.—Councillor Roberts : Has the horse got ringbone.—Mr. Day : It was not suf- fering from ringbone.—Mr. Roberts : That is not the question. Has it got ringbone.—Mr. Day : It has not. It is the inspector's opinion that it has and mine that it has not.—Mr. Roberts. It is a case then where doctors differ.—Mr. Day : There are a great many cases where veterinary surgeons might error. All the horse suffered from was the stiff muscle of the shoulder caused by the fall.—Selina Johnson, supported Mary Johnson's state- ment.—The Chairman said they had come to the con- clusion that the case was to a great extent proved and that there had been cruelty. They ceuld not pass over that cruelty and must therefore inflict a penalty of 10s. andfcosts on Mrs. Eltkersley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>IMPE VIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPE VIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commonson Monday Mr. WOOTTON Ise.scsoN suggested to the Coltinial Seer, tary that por- ters and police from west Africa should be employed in Rhodesia. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN said he was advised that the porters would be useless, and with regard to combat- 4nts, there was ample material in South Africa from which to recruit.</p>
        <p>Sir C. Gull, called attention, by a question, to the fre- quent instances in which intoxicated persons were landed from excursion steamers,and the fact that the Licensing Act of 1872 did not apply to the licenses of these vessels. Sir M. W. Ridley acknowledged that a strong case could be made out for fresh legitlation,but would only promise at present to bring the matter specially before the Licensing Commission. TUESDAY. _</p>
        <p>_ In the House of Commons on Tuesday Mr. Bryce asked if before the session closed the Government would give some indication of their views as to the solution of the present troubles in Crete, and partimalarly as to the necessity of requiring that the governor to be :.ppointed should be irremovable, except with the consent of the Powers, and that the plan of autonomy to be settled for the island should be placed under the guarantee of the Powers. Mr. Curzon replied that the question was under the consideration of the Powers, and that he was not likely to be able to make an announcement before the end of the session. Replying to Mr. Macloan, Mr. CHAMBERLMN said the two " Reform " prisoners—Mr. Sampson and Mr. Davies —who refused to sign a petition to the Government of the Transvaal were still in gaol, where, according to their own statement, they were treated with every con- sideration. The allegation in a memorial on their be- half sent to the British Agent at Pretoria, that the " Rsformers" only laid down their arms under the pro- mise of British protection, was contradicted by the High Commission.: and Sir Jacobus de Wet. It was impos- sible to appeal to the Magnanimity of President Kruger to release the prisoners when they retused to petition themselves. Mr. Maclean asked a further question as ts the employment of British troops in Rhodesia. The Colonial Secretary replied that Sir F. Carrington was told A hen he was in London, and again upon his arrival in Capetovvn, that the Government would supply what- ever forces might be asked for, and the question of ex- pense was not to be taken into consideration. Sir John Brunner asked a question in reference to the accommo- dation at Nirthwich s•ation. Mr. Ritchie replied that the manager of the Cheshire Lines denied that there was any danger to passengers at the station, but stated that the matter would be specially brought to the notice of the Committee at their next meeting. The Board of Trade had no power to compel the Committee to re- model the station or supply additional accommodation. Sir C. DAke brought in a bill to amend the law relating to shops, and stated that the National Union of Shop Assistants were the promoters. The bill was read a first time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>HA PPI NESS.</head>
        <p>HA PPI NESS.</p>
        <p>Graceful thy form, harmonious each part, Oh happiness how beautiful thou art; Affection shines within thy beaming eyes As brightest stars through all the azure skies. No wrinkles on thy perfect face are seen, For peace unsullied with thy soul hath been. With anxious cares thou never wilt grow old Or tinge with grey those sanny locks of gold. No hoarded wealth has withered up thy heart, Or lured thy spirit from a nobler part, Or shut thy mind to music's pleasing spell Awl brightest dreams which gifted poets tell. From mean injustice thou art ever free, No selfish thought can find a place with thee ; Revenge and malice thou canst never know : Will sympathy thy joyful footsteps go. Discord can ne'er possess thy tranquil breast, Or vain emotions keep thy soul from rest ; Nor tiost thou court ambition's envied place; True honour dwells with thy deviner race. Pure love and friendship with thy nature twine And all the graces in thy form combine, No princely mortal may with thee compare, And only virtue all thy beauty share. S. KENYON. August, 1896.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>BEVERAGES OF THE PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>THE BEVERAGES OF THE PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>Lt us glance at the ordinary breakfast beverages of the people.</p>
        <p>Tea, even if properly infused, is only a stimulant. It is not a nourishing beverage, and as usually decocted is washy, trashy, and deleterious. Coffee, even when of the best, and prepared in perfec- tion .s you will find in the East, where Mahommedans are forbidden by their religion to use alcohol, is only a cardiac or heart stim lant. It increases for a short ti m- the power of that organ without being in any sense of the Word a nourishing beverage. Cocoa.—The ordinary cocoa is not by any means a nourishing beverage. Its good qualities eitieer in the Eogli h or foreign varieties are smothered in starch or sugar that induce nd promote indigestion. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is a nourishing beverage, con- taining four great restorers of vitality,Cocoa,liola,Hop3, and Malt. It stands out as a builder up of tissues, a promoter of Vigour, and in short it has all factors which make robust health. Being a deliciously flavoured beverage it pleases the most fastidious palate. Its active powers of diastase give tone to the stomach, and pro- mote the flow of gastric juice, and however indigestible the food taken with it at any meal it acts as a solvent and assimilative.</p>
        <p>All the leading medical journals recommend Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, and D. G.ll. Haslam writes :—"lt gives me great pleasure in bearing testimony to:the value of Vl•Cocoa, a mixture of Malt, Hops, Kola, and Caracas Cocoa Extract. I consider it the very best pre- paration of the kind in the market, and, as a nourishing drink for children and adults, the finest that has ever been brought before the public. As a general beverage it excels all previous preparations. No house should be without it."</p>
        <p>Dr. Tibbles' VI-Cocoa is made up in 61. packets, and 93. and is. 61. tins. It can be obtained from all chemists grocers, and stores, or from Dr. Tibbles' Vi- Cocoa, Limited, Suffolk House, Cannon-street, London E.C. As an unparalleled test of merit, aMainty sample tin, of Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa will bs sent free on application to any address, if when writing (a postcard will do) the reader will name the Warrington" Examiner."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ENGLISH GOVERNESSES IN SPAIN.</head>
        <p>ENGLISH GOVERNESSES IN SPAIN.</p>
        <p>A WARNING FROM THE BRITISH CONSUL</p>
        <p>The. British Consul in Bilbao, in his latest report, mentions that it has happened more than once du-ing the past year that an English lady has gone to him in an almost destitute condition, having been discharged with- out warning frc?in a situation as governess in a Spanish family. No misconduct was alleged in au 5 case, but the governess was regarded by her employers as a servant, and had been treated in accordance with Spanish cus- %me, by which either master or servant can terminate the engagement without notice, It seems that it is not illegal to suddenly order a young girl, a foreigner, who may be without money or frieads and unable to speak the language, out of the house, however unseemly it may be. Accordingly, the Consul advises English governesses not to enter Saanish families without a written egreement defining the terms of the engagement and stipulating for notice. They would do well also to register themselves at the nearest British Consulate. The Consul also hopes that this warning may be widely cir- culated in the United Kingdom, and especially in Ireland.</p>
        <p>To MOTHER.9.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup nas been used over fifty years by millions of mothers fu their children while teething with perfect success. it will relieve the poet sufferer immediately, It is pleasant to taste ; it produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain, and the little cherub awakes 'as bright as a button." Of at chemists, is. 11d. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL FINANCE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>FURTHER REPLY BY COUNCILLOR. SH&amp;W GREEN.</head>
        <p>FURTHER REPLY BY COUNCILLOR. SH&amp;W GREEN.</p>
        <p>As we consider this subject is vital to the general welfare of the Borough we invited Councillor Shaw Green to give us his views upon the further critic- ism of our correspondent. The Councillor said he was willing to do his utmost to enable the ratepayers to comprehend our financial problems -, but, he added, these will hardly bear the title of Criticisms and certainly many are not new for they have in different forms been served up before. "1 have tried to classify them with a view to a better form of reply but as it is not possible,l must take them in the order they appear. First then we are invited to look at the water aanuities and interest account of 1893 and ta contrast it with the same account of 18116, and we are actually in a clear deinite statement in- formed by your correspondent that in the report of 1893 the assets of the water department are set forth whilst in the accounts for 1896 the assets are left blank. Your correspondent then naturally asks what has become of these assets since 1893 and he follows it with the proper statement that if it was necessary to set them out in 1893 it must also be necessary to di., so in 1896. Certainly I said to myself as I read it ; if such ever were set out' why not? but I rubbed my eyes to see if I had read aright, I had never heard ot the assets being set out and I could not possibly credit what your correspondent said. The assets of the water department set out! In what form, and how had the various parts lands,buildings, reservoirs,engines, tunnels, mains, pipes, statutory rights, priveleges, revenues, goodwill, &amp;c., been stated and by whom appraised, because in the general acceptance of the term these are the assets of such a concern? I turned up the volume for 1893 and lo there was no such record, but I found under the head of assets in this particular account, the annuity and interest account, that it had cash in the bank £144 9s. 10d.; and that the balance represented the net amount of the debt at that date. Is it possible, thought I,that our cri- tic can be poking fun at us or that he wrote this after having had a short nap following a too heavy dinner, and then allowed his imagination too much liberty! I then turned to 1896 and found under the head of assets a blank, but a look upon the other side at once showed the cause. In 1896 this account owed to the bankers £1,163 Is. Bd., and so there was no necessity to set out a balance an the asset side, for as the book-keepers say it was a balance down and that on the debit side. To make and write such errors may or may not be to write nonsense, but the labour of correcting them is cer- tainly no joke.</p>
        <p>Now about the alteration of the date of making up the accounts from the 25th to the 31st March. There is a very simple explanation of this wonder- ful alteration, and I beg to assure you there 0 no undisclosed purpose, no attempt to gain an untair advantage, no trying to get 53 weeks in a year. No, it was just done to bring our accounts into har- mony with all others in the kingdom, for by the Act which created Parish and District Councils the financial yew. was fixed to end on the 31st March, and the resolution to carry this into effect was passed by the Council on the sth June, 1894. Objection is next taken to the alteration of the annual report in setting out only the initials of the bondholders and not their full names. Surely everybody ought to commend us for this. Complaint was frequently made by the bondholders that it was unfair to expose their private affairs to the public, and as no requirement of the Local govern- ment Board was violated by putting the initials only we adopted it, and what objection can there be to it ? The public are safeguarded by the full list being published, certified by the auditors, and the bondholders can find by their initials whether the amount they have lent the Corporation is cer- , rectly stated. It is not to such unimportant de- tails as this that the "lynx eye" of the Local Government Board is directed.</p>
        <p>Next follows a reference to my observations that we are not a private firm or trading concern that had to be balanced up, and we have a short disser- tation upon the value of good book-keeping a ith all of which I fu:ly agree;but in the most emphatic manner 1 assert that the accounts of the borough are most excellently kept, The book-keeping is clear and satisfactory. So much is this so that two of our local accountants, two of the borough auditors, have recently published such in their annual report. This, however, is running away froqi the previous position which was that all the assets arid property of the Corporation ought to be set out in the report, and I venture again to assert that any attempt to do so would only be the creation of a mass of figures very, if not almost entirely, difficult to follow, and would require explanatory notes too numerous even to mention, besides being of no practical value to the rate- payers.</p>
        <p>_ I will next take one of the subjects out of its order. Your correspondent, I think, admits that I proved his remarks upon the assessment of the borough to be based upon an assumption that had no foundation. I am pleased to read that he is satisfied. I will grant him anything he can get for the part he has taken, but I 11 cannot admit that there is any credit due to him for lurging a consideration of the assessment of the borough upon the overseers. Referring now to the chief criticism, that with reference to the Consolidation of the various debts and the suggestion that we could save £3,000 a year. Does any reader of the " Examiner" imagine for one moment that the Finance Commit- tee is so stupid, so blind to the benefit of such a saving,as to refuse it if it were possible? But with respect to Consolidating and the issue of a stock, there is much to say on both sides. No good, however, can follow the repetition of my previous statement that such a Consolidation as your corres- pondent suggests could not be carried out. Ido not think your correspondent expects me to take his statement as gospel. Ido not know what oppor- tunities for forming an opinion he has, but I know what opportunities we as a Committee have. In company with the Town Clerk and the Borough Treasurer, I have, at the request of the Committee, seen the officials of the Local Government Board 1 haveput the matter before them, and in my last reply I gave your reeders the offer they made to us. Does your correspondent seriously suggest that if we were to promote a Bill for such a purpose we could carry it? What carry a Bill througg the House with the whole Dapartment opposed to it! I venture to suggest that your correspondent does not know and cannot even guess the powers of the Board. Let him accept it as a fact that if anything better than we had offered to ue could have been got we should have left nothing undone to have obtained it. Your correspondent mentions some corporations who have consolidated. Consolidated to create a stock I admit; but have they been carried out on an extension of time for existing loans ? and have these been done in recent years? I have recently been to Brighton, and there were present the financial officers of the boroughs of Liverpool, Blackburn, Bolton, Halifax, Huddersfield, and a host of other towns. We talked financial matters over in private, and we got to know what they had done and on what terms they had done it. We had a most ani- mated public discussion upon the question of short terms versus stock. We told them publicly what we had done and we weie congratulated u?on the way we had financed our loans. What better testimony could we have ? We all see the possi- bilities of the money market. We know the great and increasingly pressing difficulties of those boroughs who have consolidated and issued stock, and we feel that in the light of piesent events we have done well,in what we have done,and many of the towns who have issued stock would be proud to day to be as we are. We had no infallible oracle to consult, no local prophet who could tell us what the money market would be thirty years hence,but we acted upon the best information we could ob- tain in good faith with a sincere desire to get all the ad antages possible for the ratepayers and we must leave it to the future to reveal whether we were wise or not.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>CARD</head>
