        <p>IT may at the present time be Foreign Affairs. said without exaggeration tha politiciaLs in every country Europe are watching with anxious interest the development of the varying contest between the Sultan and his misgoverned Christian subject:24 Turkey is just now regarded, in a political sense, as the powder magazine of the world. It is evi- dently feared that at any moment something might happen which would act like a sparl‘ amongst explosives, and thus bring about the coil' flict among the great military nations of the world which has been so often predicted. Despot' ate expedients are being resorted to in order, s° to speak, to localise the fire. The Europe powers, though supposed to be acting in " cop' cert," have, owing to their mutual jealousies' stood idly by while the Turks have, it is nocl computed, destroyed no fewer than a million Armenian Christians. But this cowardly inaction has not saved Europe from the dangers that were anticipated. The old troubles are breakii4, out ill new and more critical places. A year or two age it was feared that the agitation in Armenia might hasten the dissolution of the Ottoman Empire ; now the rebellion in Crete is threatening the same result. The Armenian danger was got rid of, temporarily at all events, by the summary pre- cess of wholesale massacre, but the Cretan troubles cannot for two or three reasons be ee easily dealt with. One of these reasons is to be found in the fact that the Greek Christians are hl a majority in Crete and are in a position to offer a much more formidable resistance to thei: oppressors than were the comparatively helples° Armenians. Another reason is tnat interventio° by outsiders is much easier in the ease of Crete than in the case of the Armenians, and intervelr tion of one kind or another is almost bound tre take place in the event of further Tuirl ish atrocities on a large scale. As Yet the Cretan insurgents, though fighing in a just cause,have nothing to thank the great Powers ler It is in fact now understood that a proposal hee actually been made by one Continental Goverr ment that the powers should combine their flee° to blocka.!le the island and thus cut off all outside assistance. Lord SALISBURY has, it is said, de' dined to have anything to do with the scheme s° far as this country is concerned, and he will be commended for his refusal. Under the c stances he could scarcely have adopted any course. It is not likely that Great Britain '4l ever again act as the" bailiff" of the Turk. to singular and significant result of this refusal take any part in suppressing Vie strugglill6; Christians of Crete, is another display of sPleed and malice towards this country by the Gera"' newspapers. According to these newspal3'l* which are understood to voice the sentiments of majority of the German people, the hidde: object of England is to provoke a gre3t• war amongst the nations of the Contllielbl4 Englishmen, we are told, are not influenced blo generous enthusiasm for the welfare of a 11° race. We are, says these exasperated Geravies..l too cold-blooded for anything of that kind. °toe, only object in connection with the Ottoman iasc pire is to seize "those/morsels of its heritage In</p>
        <p>ose est' or Ole rig</p>
        <p>be ;r-</p>
        <p>Cle F C. HULION, CLERK or THE PEACE. - of the P Offi:1•, Preston, 25 July, 1896.</p>
        <p>I"</p>
        <p>calculated to gladden an English heart." In this 1 'e9nutry we are of opinion that peace is the •greatest of British interests, but the Germans do Act give us credit for any such sentiments. Ac- cording to these uncharitable and indeed malevo- l.snt critics, the sole aim and object of our diplomacy is that of bringing about a continental conflagration," and all for the benefit of British trade. This remarkabie outburst of feeling in Germany should have its lesson. for our Govern- illent. The German newspapers point to our oc- eopation of Egypt and practically tell the French that, with the aid, of Russia, 11()* is the time to oust -us from th%t ,atleient country. Yet, •if the country has ueen rightly informed, it was no less a person than the German EMPEROR himself who origin- allY suggested the expedition up the Nile which wai3 ordered by our government a few months back. That expedition, which is absolutely un-. necessary at the present time,undoubtedly caused a great deal of irritation in France, and now we !have the German press working upon this feeling m order. to incite France against this country Our Government was warned at the time by the PPosition in Parliament and by the Liberal press 111 the country of the injurious effect which the liongola expedition might have upon our foreign relations and of the danger of squandering its Military resources, and there can be no doubt now 41 view ot the unsettled condition of European etairs of the wisdom of that warning. It is quite Clear now, that Lord SALISBURY should have been extremely wary of accepting Garman advice.</p>
        <p>THE French people are, it is The Population of said, not pleased with the re- sult of the last census of popu- lation—in fact they are rather alarmed -by the figures which have been disclosed. This census shows that population in France is practically at a standstill. There are now 38,228,969 persons in Prance, which is only 133,819 more than were in the country five years ago. What is more, the small increase that there has been is entirely accounted for by the growth of some of the big 3wris. In many country districts the population I,as declined. This is serious, because in more than in most other countries, the Peasantry are the wealth and strength of the thttion. This stagnation of population if it . eo,htiones is bound to affect the position of %nee in several ways which, will be any- tll but pleasing to the national pride. 434 the present time France can fairly claim to in the very front rank of the great nations of 41e world. If her population decline or even l'ehaain stationary whilst that of neighbouring ecgintries continue to increase rapidly she will in theend forfeit that position of honour. It was N'apoloen who said that Providence is on the 41de of big battalions. Now, in these days of .4hiversal conscription on the Continent of Europe 4 country must have a large population in order tt provide its" big battalions." The Germans 14ready exceed the French in number by many killions and the population of Germany was Lillown by the last census to be growing rapidly. same is the case with regard to Grgat Britain. t'tt ere is now we believe a larger population in islands than in France,and it shows a steady blrlcrease. In the colonies, to say. nothing of the Y'lited States, the Anglo-Saxon race multiplies it3elf with even greater rapidity. France, on the OP Other hand, does not gain any accession of real Power by the acquisition of colonies. Its colonies are not colonised in the proper sense of the word. It has no surplus population to send abroad, and t. I"' huge tracts of territory which it has acquired n Tong:min. and Madagascar are retained as More or less ornamental I—` ---,Art certainly not profitable aPpendagea.</p>
        <p>In the death of toe Rev. 0. de P. Gliddon the 4ncorn Wesleyans have lost a minister who dur- -144'1 his period of ministration in the circuit has kuch for tie reiigous life of the town. An p ()bent preacher, and an interesting lecturer, his onality ensured him a hearing in the pulpit, b.hue in routine work he was gteativ respected by 'lnns of his scholarly attainments, his powers of ?rganisation and the sympathetic manner in wigich met those who cama, in contact with him. His Ilte of usefulness makes his untimely end the more latnentabie.</p>
        <p>A short list characterised this week's Petty ?"kions at Roncorn, indeed, had it not been for one 'allies Harrison, a nomad, described as of B3dford there would ',have been a clean sheet for the Illaßimtrates. This gentleman arriving a Runcorn during the week end, got intoxicated on Saturday 4igat, and through his disorderly action in Church- Sktreat deprived the chairman (Sir R. Brooke) of the "(labored white gloves. Harrison was discharged °ll giving a solemn undertaking to leave the town departed the sitlls day for tields,pastures,public 11311ies,and police courts new.</p>
        <p>Friendly Society work seems to possess a strong- "ld in Halos. It is only some few weeks since recorded the added prosperty of the Ladies f:riendly eociety, and now we are informed that the Sing Sun lodge of Oddfellows is worth .Cll6l 16 Zhich is ad increase on the year of £72 184. 101. 4‘ecent valuation of Friendly Societies all over tbe country have ;satisfactorily proved that this kind Of provision for troublous times is becoming more PoPular than ever, and it is pleasing to see local branches of Friendly Societies keeping up with the titnes and establishing for themselves sound finan- cial foundations.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn C3-operative Society has made a tel) iu the direction which Sir. J. T. Brunner, at a 14" cent meeting gently hinted they might travel,for 41,t the half yearly meeting a sum of money was de- to educational work. The spread of co-opera- thiveMeas by means of lectures should materially 1:IP the institution and the money will no doubt flg in many ways, an adequate return.</p>
        <p>INTI)TICE is th•retht 'given, that a tieueral ES.SION (-)1 the Peace f•-,r the County. Palatine of Lancaster Co holden at. tnP County Sessions House, Islington, tipk,"rtiool, ,311 'TUESDAY, the 25,:b day of AUGUST' at, elei.•en o'clock in the forenoon precisely. Solicitors engaged in prosecutions at the. said ,k are requested to take notice that all imetructions tr) tr.",,ettnents and depositions are required to be sent lan: Clerk of the Peace's Office seven days at 'Past the said Session.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>REWARD FOR BRAVERY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The youth named Riley, who has been instrumental in saving several lives, was awarded the Humans Socie- ty's thanks and a money reward for the gallant conduct in rescuing a child trom the canal about a week ago.. LOCAL SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. B. Chadwick, ot Crofton Lodge, Higher Run- elrn, was awarded a champion prize of £l5, for the best shire filly foal )Itien to all England, UT his exhibit at the Whalley Agricultural Society's Exhibition, held on Monday. RUNCORN PARISH CHURCH CHOIR.</p>
        <p>The adult members of this choir travelled for their annual outing to Buxton where the pleasures of that dis- trict were made the most of. Dinner and tea were served at the Cheshire Cheese while in the afternoon the party visitei Toddington and inspected the ancient church there. Runcorn was reachad at eleven o'clock. COMING MINISTERIAL CHANGES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The pastoral sessions of the Liverpool Wesleyan Con- ference were resumed at four o'clock on Monday after- noon, the President in the chair. It was announced that the Revs. Claude de P. Glieldon, of Runcorn, and J. B. Harry, of Doncaster, had died suddenly. It was decided that letters of sympathy be sent to the families of the deceased ministers. The Conference proceeded to the discussion of the stations in open conference, and the following local changes were made :—Runcorn : R J. Clifton, vice C, de P. Gliddon (deceased). Frodsham : J. R. Hargreaves, vice R. J. Clifton. ANOTHER REICUE BY A RUNCORN YOUTH._</p>
        <p>_ At the excursion organised by the Seamen's. Mission on Aionday,John Riley,who only last week distinguished himself by pulling a boy out of the Ship Canal, again came into prominence by another gallant action. While proceeding with the boat, Riley heard a cry for help and going to the spot saw a little fellow struggling in the water. Ho at once went in for the lad, and brought him with the assistance of Mr. Shaw to the canal side. The child was aged three years, and was a son of Mr. Thomas Tonks, of Portland-st.. Runcorn. This is the fifth life saved by the youth Rday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. GEORGE MARSHALL, RUNCORN,</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. GEORGE MARSHALL, RUNCORN,</p>
        <p>On Monday, Mr. George Marshall,died at his residence Beaconsfi,ld. Higher Runcora, about noon,at the age of 60 rears. The deceased gentleman carried on business in Runcorn for many years as a chemist and druggist in the shop now occupied by Mr. Weston, and retired from that business some six years ago. Mr. Marshall was keenly alive to the interest of the town, and for some time occupied a seat on the Board of Improvement Com- missioners of which body he was honoured with the chairmanship in the year 1880. Though formerly a Wesleyan he latterly occupied himself in mission work, and conducted the services in the Gospel Mission Church which building he erected. Deceased had been in failing health for some time past,and on Thursday last a serious relapse took place ending fatally on Sunday. The body was interred in the Cemetery on Thursday afternoon. The cortet e left Beaconsfi.ld House about 2 30, amongst those in attendance being Messrs. R. Morris, W. Boote,James Turnbull, A. Dodd, and several members of the Free Gospel Mission. The mourners were Mr. Marshall (brother) and widow, Mr. and Mrs. Thompson, (brother-in-law and sister), Mr. and Mrs. S. Brereton, (nephew and niece),Messrs. S. Tickle jun., and senior (nephews), Mr. and Mrs. A. Brereten (brother-in- law and sister), Mr. Gibbs, and Mr. and Mrs. Hitchen. Mr. Boote conducted the funeral service at the cemetery, and also delivered a brief address. At the close of the service the hymn "Give me the Wings of Faith" was sung. RUNCORN POOR iIIILDREN'S TRZAT.</p>
        <p>Over 600 poor childreti were taken by special train to Mersey View, Overton, the funds being provided by the balance left over from the Aged People's Treat together with subscriptions from the following: Sir J. T. Brunner M.P., £3, Dr. Steele £2 23., Sir R. Brooke, Bart , Sir Joseph Verdin £l. Is., Mrs: Garrett, £l, Mr. T. Ward, IP., 10a. 61., Dr. Robinson, 10a. 61., Messrs. T. Rigby and Sons, 109. Mr. J. R. Frith, 104, and Mr. F. J. Nor- man J.P., sm. At Frodsham,the members of the League of "he Crsaanl Weston Church Brass bands were in atrooda.nce and gave their services free. Football, cricket Pnd racing were warmly followed up, for toys given by .ir. Thomas Wood white there were unlimited doiik.c rides through the kindness of Mr. Meredith and oth.rs. in ascending the hill the lame children found the climb very difficult but they were well looked after nine tieing conveyed en donkeys and others on the broad and willing backs of membets of the committee. A substantial tea was eagerly partaken of by the young- sters who were served by Miss Crosby, nurse Bucker'- ham, Mrs. W. Shaw,Mrs. T. C.Williapas,and Mrs.Leath- wood. Amongst those who accompanied the trip were the Rev. Dr. Gastald', Rev. W. H. Staples, Rev, W. Scholes, Mr. Grice, Mr. Taylor, J.P.. C.C., Dr. Fulton, Messrs. T. C. Williams, W. Shaw, W. Beheld, George Robinson, D. Littlemore, P. Leathwood, Thomas Cook, and Walter Millington, the secretary, each of whom were indefatigable in their efforts to keep the children amused, and did everything in their power to make the outing enjoyable to the your.), ne,nle</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER,- StilirC:oA.L iiSD FORE- SHORE RIGEITS.</p>
        <p>ORRED v. MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL COMPANY. In this case at the Liverpool Assizes, on Thursday,the plaintiff, Major Orred, brought this action to recover from the Manchester Ship Canal Company, possession of a piece of land in the manor of Weston which lies be- tween Frodshane and Runcorn and forms part of the foreshore of the river Mersey. Mr. Bigham, Q.C.. and Mr. Stuart Moore were far the plaintiff,and Mr. M'Call, Q.C., and Mr. Rockill appeared for the defendant com - pany. In opening the case Mr. 13ighans said the Canal Com- pany had set up twe defences, the Opus of one of them beige upon Major Orred and the onus of the other upon the defendant company. The first matter the Canal Company laid down was that they were in possession of this piece of land, that they had built part of their canal on it, and that therefore it was for Major Orred to show that he had got a title to the land. Of course the onus of showing that Major Orred had a title rested upon him. The ether Wile raised WAS, the Canal Com- pany said, that if this land belonged to Major Orred, he steed by and saw the company build part of the canal upon it ; that he allowed the company to go on building upon the impression that it. was their land and that they were entitled to put their canal there; and therefore Major Orred could not now be heard to say that the land was his. That, it other words, meant that the defend- ants now contended that: it was too late to claim—that it should have been done before the canal was Made or the land in question. The onus of proving that Major Orred had lost his right to recover the land, was upon the people who; alleged such a curious state of affairs. It would be for the jury to decide—firstly, whether the land which Major Orred sought to recover was his, and, secondly, whether, if it was his, had he behaved in such a way as to prevent him from saying, as against the persons who had now got it, that they eueht to give it back to him ? fin action, in law, was really one foe ejeatment of the people in possession, but that was not what Major Orred desired to do. He was net'soa,unreasonable. By this action all he desired was that the Canal Company should give him reasonable compensation for tbe land that had been taken from him, calculating the value of the land upen its value before the canal was built, and not upon its improved value. Counsel next dealt with the history of the manor of Weston ,with the object of proving that the land in question was part of that manor, of which pl -dn'off was lord. The forest/ire of a tidal river belonged prima facie to the Crown,and to alter that it would be neces- sary to show that the Crown had made a grant of the foreshore to some one. Dseds, records, and ancient do- cuments would be handed to his Lerdship to show that such a grant had been made to the ancestors of the plaintiff and that it had descended to him. They further showed that the lord of the manor became pos- sessed of the fishery of the Mersey adjoining the manor if Weston, and, therefore, had a title to the soil of the river to mid-stream. For the purpose of proving that plaintiff had a good title, he mentioned that in 1860 and • in 1868 the Orreds conveyed part of their foreshore to the Trustees of the River Weaver Navigation, who paid the Orreds as well as the Crown for that foreshore. The Judge: They paid the Crown something to shut its mouth Mr. Bigham said that was so,for sometimes the Crown was greedy.—(Laughter). The Minchester Ship Canal became a real scheme by 1883, but by that time Widnes and Rencern had destroyed the fisheries.—(Laughter.) Widnes was a place that his Lordship might have smelt. .(Renewed laughter.) The title to the land had, however, net been destroyed if the fishery had. The Canal Com- pa.ny in course of time bought from Major Orred certain marshes, and they were also informed what would he the price of the foreshore opposite the marshes. The Company told the plaintiff that they were arranging as to the foreshore with the Crown' and they were taking care that if any of the money for it belonged to the plaintiff—and he could prove his title—the money would be paid over to the plaintiff. Now he (counsel) did not know really who was defending this action—the Crown , or the Manchester Ship Canal Company. The latter had been proceeded against. as they were in possession of the land, and as plaintiff had not been paid for the foreshore. Mr. M`Call submitted that the question of title was one entirely f n. the Judge. Mr. Righam asked that the whole matter might go to the jury. Mr. Moore then explained to the Court the documents, which he formally handed to the Judge. Mr fligharn contended that those, documents raised the presumption of a grant from the Crown before Dameelay of the fee of HaVon, with the manor of Wes- ton, including the foreshore of the Mersey and Weaver to mid-channel, and, secondly, of a grant to the ances- tors of the Duttons, from whim the plaintiff claimed title. In ernirse of further areumenr, Mr. Bigham asked, did Mr. &amp;Mall suggest that the plaintiff had ever con- veyed to the defendant Company this foreshore Mr. M'Call Certainly not. Evidence in support of counsel's statement was given by Charles Lee Evans, agent for Major 'Dried. • Witness related in detail particulars el the transactions betvieen ths parties, and erndueed letters that had passed be- tween them relating to the foreshore. The Court adjourned until t9414y, wlun the witness will be cross-examined.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CO-OPERATIVE INDUSTRIAL</p>
        <p>AND PROVIDENT SOCIETY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURPAY, AUGUST 8, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURPAY, AUGUST 8, 1896.</p>
        <p>HALF YEARLY MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night the laalf-yearly meeting of the mem- bers of the Runcorn Co-operative, Industrial and Provi- den- Society took place in the Bethesda Lecture Hall, Runcorn. There was a fair attendance of members,p re- sided over by Mr. W. E. Dadley, sho was supported on the platform by the following members of the commit- tee—Messrs. W. Dyer (vice chairman). W. Riodes, J. Leadstone, J. J. Bowyer, G.F. Dean, J.Stubbs, F.Cosier, W. Willan, and T. Riley. In the statement of accounts read the total sales were set down at £42,426 184. Oil. ['he amount dispssable after charging all expenses, including depreciation and interest on capital and deducting the checks purchased left the nett ameunt -£6,116 18s. id., and the committee recommended a dividend of 2s. 11d. in the £ on £4.1950 on check account books brought in, which will amJunt to £6.117 14s. 2d. The Chairman at the outset referred to the coal trade, and said that the members would notice that that business was a little down on the half year. That was not due to cempetition but to a mill winter experience. Since then there had been keen competition but notwithstanding that they were doing 5) tons per week in Runcorn. Mr. Dudley then referred to the pro- posal of the Wholesale Society to establish a tobacco manufactory and stated that the Runcorn committee had put before the proper Tr trters the claims of Runcorn as a suitably situstnd place for the ere,,tion of a tobacco manufactory. The Wholesale Society had not yet fully considered the matter, therefore it could not be said that the chalces of Runcorn had gone ba.</p>
        <p>A nurnber of questions were asked by members relat- ing to separate iterni on the balance sheet acid satisfac- torily answered by the Chairman.</p>
        <p>AN AUDITOR'S EXPLANATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Watson,before the formal passing of the acrounts, said he would like to make a few remarks. After the rumours which had been going about tor the past few weeks, hs would like to ask the auditors whether every- thing during the last Rix months had been fair and above board and whether they had any grounds for the suspic- ion of miy dishonesty.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brodrick one of the auditors said that as they would -see from their report:—" They had examined the books and accounts of the society, for the year ending July 30th, 1896 and subject to the completion of the ex- amination of the Share Pass BlOk3 at dividend time certified that the foregoing was correct." They could not get members to send in their pass book; before hand for the examination of the auditor,,and it was inaportan' and must be patent to all of them that unless b ,oks were to be sent independently the auditors were always m,-)re or less in doubt as to whether everything was regular or not. From his experience as an auditor, very often manipulations in pass books were only found out on ex- amination. In matters of detail the accounts had not been satisfactory to them during the 'last six months but as very important matters might arise out of it, he did not think they as members ought to force the auditors to say anything very de- finite that night. The whole matter was" sub-justice," and if matters of importance' arose out of the ex amina- tion they might trust the committee to take the action necessary for the purpose of safe guarding their intertit. He could assure them that bore was whine serious as far as their affairr. were concerned, and nothing they need trouble about. (Applause.) Mr. Watson said that seeing that the audit was incom- plete he would not press the matter, and would move the passing of the balance sheet. Mr. Cowell secondeu the resolution which was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>TEE DINNER HOUR QUESTION</head>
        <p>TEE DINNER HOUR QUESTION</p>
        <p>This matter was Imlay referred to by the Chairman, who pointed out that they as co-operators ought to try to se': an example to private traders. Business was also much better conducted when they had the manavr, continually in charge, from the time of starting to clos- ing tinse. The Chairman's remarks were endorsed by Mr. Ford, and as no resolution was proposed the shops will remain closed in the dinner hour as befnre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>EDUCLTION QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCLTION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that sow months ago they had Sir J. T. Brunner down to their annual gathering and hs expressed his surprise that a society of such a siz as the one at Runcorn was dein., n)thing towards educa- tional work. The committee would like to make a sug- gestion that they spend £lO for the purpose of bringing lecturers down to Runcorn and Widnes to spread the principles of co-operation. Mr.Millington proposed a resolution to that effect and Mr. Clarke secondad. The resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>ELECTION OP SECRETARY</head>
        <p>ELECTION OP SECRETARY</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the Conamittee thoroughly realm- mend Mr. Shaw as a steady and industrious man for the position of secretary. The auditors have several times expressed their approval of the work he had done al assistant secretary. The committee unanimously re- commended him for the position of secretary that night. Mr. Bradbury moved. and Mr. Brown seconded, the appointment of Mr. Shaw, which was carried. . .</p>
        <p>A Member: What was the salary. The Chairman: £lO4 per year. Mr. Watson movel that the secretary, treasurer, and manager be raised 104. each per week. The Chairman mentioned that the secretary, as a proof that he was capable of doing the work, was pre. pared to do it for 12 months at his present salary. Mr. Ford thought it had been a reproach long enough to ask the secretary to do work of such a responsible character on the salary they had bsen paying. Fle pro- posed that tho salary be £2 ss. per week. Mr. Clarke, in seconding Mr. Watson's resolution, said they cenld not get honest servants for the small mousy they were paying. A member suggested that as they wore raising salaries of the heads of the work they might turn their attention to some of the lower officials. Fie was told that some of the branch managers only got 30s. per week. The Chairman pointed out that they could not raise the manager's salary which was a matter for the com- mittee to deal with. The committee had been desirous of making an improvement in his salary and n 4 doubt before long would have a proposal to make. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Watson said as it was not in order he would alter his reselution,and was quite prepared to move that the secretary and treaderer s salaries be increased by 10s.— Mr. Clarke seconded. The resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>COMMITTEE VACLECIES</head>
        <p>COMMITTEE VACLECIES</p>
        <p>The Chairman announced the result of the voting for candidates for committee vacancies to bl a; folhws T. Cozier, 207; J. Ha,rtles, 201; W. WlHan. 193 ; J. Millington. 35 ;G. Mayhue, 23; P. Leathwoed, 21. The three first who were retiring nsembers were accordingly declared re-elected. The meeting shfortly afterward; ellsed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>WEDDING AT RUNCORN'.</head>
        <p>WEDDING AT RUNCORN'.</p>
        <p>On Thursday afternoon at St. Paul's Church,Rii tacorn, a marriage took place between Mr. James Lee Patin, ef London, and Miss Carrie Littler, late head mistress of the Granville-st., Board School. The ceremony was performed by the Rev. W. Gorge. The bride was at- tired in a ckeviot serge travelling lress, aud had for her brid,smaid Miss Jackson, a cousin of the bride. Mr. A. Palin acted as best man, and the bride wee given away by her father. Subsequ-ntly the party adjourned to the house of the bride's father, where luncheon was served, after which the happy couple left .for Penrnaenintw, where the honeymoon will be spent. The follow- ing is a list of the presents :—Mr. Littler, bed- room furniture ; Mrs. Littler, overmantel and linen : Mr. W. Littler, cruet ; Mrs. W. Littler, art needle work; Mrs. C. Littler, sofa cushion ; Mr. W. E. Eden, pair fotstools ; Mrs. W. E. Eden, chenille cover ; Master A. Eden, serviettes; W. and Mrs. J. Molyneux, eider down quilt; Mr. Patin, skin rug etc. ; Mrs. Patin, copper kettle and vases ; Mr. A. Palin, time- piece; Mr. and Mrs. J. Patin, trinket set; Hr. W. Palma, vases ; Mr. and Mrs. Woodward. vases and tea pot ; Mrs. and Miss Bowyer, tea service; Mr. G. Bowyer and Miss Crowther, cream and sugar sand ; Mr. R. Bowyer and Miss Ellison, set of jigs; Mrs. Myers, Bradford, damask table cloth; Mr. Rnd Mrs. Bailey, shawl, toilet cover, etc.; Miss Rougley, London, tea cosy and toilet set; Miss Bramald (Sheffield), silver jam dish ; Miss Kilvington, d'oyleys and toilet mats ; Mr. and Mrs. Rushton, silver desseat spoon; Miss A. Rushton, silver tea spoons: Mrs. Sternway, hand- painted plaques; Mr. Le Couteur, tea pat; Mrs. Mrs. Yarwood, brass stool; Mr. A. Dutton. case of carvers ; Mrs. Millington, jardiniere ; Miss R. Milling- ton, trifle dish; Misses Holt, flower stand ; Mr. and Mrs. Bisbrowne, bamboo table; Miss A. M. Taylor, painting on porcelain ; Mrs. Newell, glass water jug ; Mr. and Mrs. Pearson, pair of vases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>ODDFELLOWS' DEMONSTRATION.—On Mondly,the mein- -1 bers of the Rising Sun Ledge of the G.U.O. of Old- fellows held their hi-annual procession. The members r assembled at the ledge roorn,Halton Castle. shortly after nine, and forming into procession, paraded the village, and then walked to Runcorn. A return was made via High-street to the headquarters of the Star of Freedom Lodge where refreshments were served out. The pro- cessionists were preceded by their handsome banner and headed by the Volunteer band. The juvenile members of She lodge numbering 60 in charge of Bros.ThomaFort and S. Blain were next in order followed by the adult; mem- bers to the num net of 100. Amongst those present were —Dr. Ormesby, the Bros. R. Hughes (secretary), Rowe (treasurer). J. Hancock, J. Faulkner. J. N. Bosworth (trustees), Massey (district secretary), and William Mos- ton (secretary of the Scar of Freeciem Lodge) who wore the regalia, appertaining toe the officers. On arriving back at the Castle Hotel a substantial dinner was found to be prepared and was excellently served by the pro- prietor, Mr. Walker. Letters of apology for non-attend- ance were read from Sir R. M. Spoke. Dr. Carruthers, Messrs. S. Taylor, LP—C.C.. Thomas Knight. and J.Lea. The lodge io valued at £1.161 16s. which is a gain en the 12 months of £72 18s. 10-1. and the books embrace 254 I members. The festivities were kept up during the ro- mainder of the day, dancing on the green being I thorOughly enjoyed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Board was held on Tues- day at Dutton Workhowe, present. Mr. J. Knowles (in the chair), the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Mr. A. R. Norman and Mr. S. Yarwood, and the members of the Rural District Council, with Mr. Ashton (clerk). RECOMMENDATIONS FROM THE LADIES' COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Ladies' Committee recommended that as the adult inmates had nothing with which .to occupy their time after the service on Sunday, a small library be provided. The recommendation was referred to the consideration of the (louse Committee.—Mr. Williams asked if they could not provide spectacles for the old people as the books would be of little use to them otherwise.—Mr. Dansey replied that they could on the recommendation of the Medical officer. LOCAL GOYERNMgNT BOARD AND TB APPOINTMENT OF</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>INDUSTRIAL TRAINER,</head>
        <p>INDUSTRIAL TRAINER,</p>
        <p>Mr. Dansey made reference to the proposal of the Board to send tne girls to school outside the House. Of the 40 unions in his distrist there were only four work- house schools, lie bad to draw attention to the applica- tion of the Board to appoint Miss Ounnah, daughter of the workh Inv. master, as industrial trainer. He, found she was only 16 years of age, and he was afraid the Local G mornment Board would object to sanction it, considerieg that she would have under her girls but a few months younger than herself and perhaps some of the same age—Toe Clerk and several members pointed out that the aopaintment was only of a temporary char- acter. The efforts to get the children into Dutton School would he seriously considered after the holidays and they expected would be settled. The difficulty arose through certain parties' having objected to their going to the school.--Mr. Dansey said he had known cases in which such objections had been raised, but after the children had been there a few years the managers were loath to part with them as t •ey were found to be the most regular and well behaved and most amenable to discip- line.—lt was decided to leave the subject as to the appointment of industrial trainer as it was until they heard from the Local Government Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ASYLUM RATSS.</head>
        <p>ASYLUM RATSS.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Hornby, clerk to the County Asylum, Chester, intimated that the rate of maintainance of pauper luna- tics in the tsvlum for the quarter ending 30.;h Septem- ber next woull be fixed at 7.$ per head per week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD SANCTION</head>
        <p>LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD SANCTION</p>
        <p>The Local Goversimenr, Board wrote apnrovine of the appointment of Mr. El. Norcott, aq school attendance offiler, at a salary of £35 Der annum.</p>
        <p>This was the whole of the buqiness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>DEATH OF T tiE, IV. C. DE P.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF T tiE, IV. C. DE P.</p>
        <p>GLIDDON, HALTON.</p>
        <p>The news of the death of Rev. Claude de Putron Glid- don, on Sanday, at the age of-38 years, caused quite a gloom in Runcorn and Halton and the surrounding neighbourhood, and sympathy was expressed by local Wesleys,ns far whoa he ministered and the other deno- minations in the town. Mr. Gliddon was apparently well up to Friday week when he attended the Wesleyan conference at Liverpool, and it was only a week last Saturday when the illness began seriously to show it- self. The disease with which the rev, gentleman was ioliicted was of an internal nature, and despite the en- deavours of Dr. Wright and Dr. Ormesby he gradually grew weaker and succumbed about two o'clock on the Sunday morning. Artier the morning service at St. Paul's, the organist, Mr. Grice, played the Dead March in "Saul," and zhe same composition was performed on Sunday night in the Halton Road Chapel by the organist Me. H. eepe. The deceased, it may be said, leaves a widow- and a daughter to whew great sympathy has been shown in their sudden bereavement. The deceased mixister was a native of Guerniry, and on hie mother's side descended from an ancient and histori- cal French family. He , received a liberal education in a college in his native island, and continued his studies with diligence and perseverance, with the result that he was a man of wide and varied attainments. He was a splendid linguist ; had a strong admiration for art, and re his conversations demonstrated that he had a know- ledge of the chief works of the great masters. in 1883 he entered the ministry and for two years laboured in St. Mato, and a silt.' lar period in Alderney. He subse- quently conducted work for three years among the army and navy in D'evonport, and later rendered very great service among the military at Folkestone. As already stated he came to Halton after the Conference last year, and during his residence in the neighbourhood was un- remittiee in his.efforts in connection with Halton and Camden Wesleyan Cnapels, which were under his par- tkular care. Tilrough his instrumentality much good has been accomplidied in these two centres, the various srcieties having increased. is adminis- trative abilities were above the ordinary average, and he succeeded in establiehing new institutions, including the Wesley Guild and the Boys' Brigade. Mr. Gliddon was a very thoughtful, accamplished, and effec- tive preacher,and the careful attention he devoted to the principles of elocution made him frequently rise to heights of eltqa -nce. He was noted for his unconven- tionality. He was an • indefatigable worker, and paid attention to visiting, and among the young men especi- ally he was very successful. The deceased gentleman was also a very popular lecturer, and whenever he was announced to address the mutual improvement classes, or Sunday schools in the district, the attendance was invariably large. His death will he a keen loss to the Wesleyan ministrr, while his untimely end, at Run- corn, will be deep y and Nincerely regretted by the many friends he has made in the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>TFIE FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>TFIE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral ceremony took place on Wednesday after- noon aed the attendance in the procession proved the large circle of friends the deceased hid made in Runcorn. S ,1440 m if ever, has such an imposing funeral • taken p!ace in the town and seldom have the inhabitants of the town been more uninimmus in sincere expressions of re- gret. The funeral corteee started from the residence of the late minister at Haltonlo front walked the mem- bers of the congregation, representatives of the Parish •Church.aed the inhabitants of Halton village. Then came the hearse, through the *open sides of which the coffin wee seen to be covered with many beautiful fiend tributes of respect. The hearse was followed by two coaches, the only occupanz heing Mr. A. de Gliddon, brother of the deceased.the only relative who was able to attend, the other near relatives of the deceased being resident in parts of the world too distant to enable them to attend. A stoppage took place at the St. Paul's Chapel and the body was carried intn that edifice on the shoulders of the bearers,who were Messrs.J B. Houghton, J. H. Gamon, W Rowe, James Looker, N Moores, S Barrow, John Barrow, sen., and Joseph Astbury. The chapel was crowded with a congreeetinn whose sad de- meanour told plainly the solemnity of the ceremony they were attending. The Rev. A. de Meulpied preceded the coffin along the aisle reciting the solemn lines of the burial service with impressive effect. An appropriate anthem WAS rendered by the choir which was followed by the Rev. J. I'. Overton, a former superintendent of the circnit, who read the first portion of the burial ser- vice. The Rev. Charles Garrett, of Liverpoel, afterwards delivered an impressive and touchiog oration. He pointed out the sadness of the ceremony, which they were attending. It was hardly a time for words but a time fmethought, humiliation and prayer. The preacher after a eulogy on the life of the departed minister,said that the life bad ended just when they were expecting hang years of noble work. For him they had no sorrow, for he was where they all hoped to be. He saw the Kin g in his beauty and his joy would be for ever more. They could not help:though, sorrowing for those he had !nit behind. They sorrowed for the widow in her loneliness, for a child left without a father's hand to guide her.and with the rest of the family which was plunged in sorrow. The preacher concluded by pointing the effect that the sad death should have on them. Ile prayed that they would make Christ their friend, so that they would have nothing to mourn for. It was a blessed thiog their friend bad given his service to Gild, and he hoped they would let his God he their God. his Saviour he their Saviour, so that from East to West, North to South, they would meet where there was no meurning and where all was jev. From the church the procession lermed in the following. order :—Rechabites, Camden Boys' Brigade; representa- tives of Camden chapel and school, Westen and Weston Point chapel:Preston Brook chapel;St. Paul's and Halton- road chapels. St. Paul's choir, office hearers, • local preachers, ministers, Halton chapel congregation,Halton Parish Church, representatives, hearse and mourning coaches. As the procession moved slowly along it was watched by thousands of people who lined the sides of the streets while additional respect was shown by the dra wine of blinds. The service at the graveside was conducted by the Rev. T. Brackenbury and the Rev. W, George, while Mr. A. Gliddon made a brief bet ppro- prise oration. Floral offerings were sent by Mr. and Mrs. F. J. Norman, Mr. and Mrs. A. R. Norman. "Bert and Edith," Mr. and Mrs. Baguley and family. Mr. and Mrs. Bellamy, Rev. aoil Mrs. G D. \Wray, Mr. and Mrs. R. A. Po'snett,Cemden chei ,Camden Wesley Guild, St: Paul's choir, Weston chapel, and Halton chapel. The deceased clergyman was the first who had died in the Runcorn Wesleyan Circuit and a grave was purchased by the circuit for the reception of the body.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Si. THOMAS'S YOUNG MEN'S CLASS.—In connection with the abcve class a fishing match took place in the Duke's canal on Saturday afternoon. Various prizes had been offered by the Rev. S. P. Gray, Mr. E. Davies, Mr. Richardson, Mr. Caldwell, and others; but tnere was vety poor spoil., only one member. (Mr. J. Clayton) be- ing able to make a catch. Another match for the re- mainder of the priz-s provided will take place shortly. On Sunday afternoon the member:, with a good num- ber of lady friends, are tat a very pleasant tune at Apple- ton Hall, through the kindness of Mr. and Mrs. Lyon. After walking through the houses and pretty gardens, the party left for home, with very kindly feelings for the hion so generously granted.</p>
        <p>TRAVELLING- TRUNKS.—GARL IND. Ironmonger, Warrington —Advt. •</p>
        <p>IRON and BRNbS —GARLAND, Iron- mungar, Warrington.—Adut.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Council was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, preseot, Mr. J. Knowles (in the chair), Messrs. J. Brandreth, H. E. Heywood, J. M. Williams, S. Woodward, J. L. Jones, W. Pickering,J. Darbyshire, J. B. hadwick, T. Lewis, T. Wright, E. Aaron, Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), and Messrs. Swinton and Diggle (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>BABY FARMING QUEStION.</head>
        <p>THE BABY FARMING QUEStION.</p>
        <p>Colonel Hamersley, chief constable,wrote with refet- ence to the communication of the Home Secretary on the subj.ct of protection of infant children transferred by their parents to the care of other persons and prevention of what was known as baby farming by more rigid administration of the provisions of the Infants' Life Protection Act, 1872. He said he would be glad to receive any suggestions the council desired to make so that he might give the police such instruction as might be considered necessary. He would be obliged if the Local Authority would be good enough to communicate with the local police whenever they had reason to believe or any suspicion that the Act was being infringed.</p>
        <p>LIGHTING THE CANAL BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. Hunter wrote on this Subject, that in sub- sections 9 and 10 of section 83 of the Act of 1885, it was -enacted that the company should sufficiently light the swing or opening bridges provided for in the section and that such bridges should be constructed to a width of 25 feet. The bridges constructed under section 83 were those at Moore and Stag ' Inn and certain other bridges also provided for being abandoned under the Act of 1890. The bridges constructed under the Act of 1890 were those at Northwich-road and linutsford-read which were 36ft. wide and to which no obligation as to lighting was attached by the Act under which they were constructed and obviously could not be attached by the Act of 1885.—The Clerk said he did not see what they could do. The Warrington Gas Company had written to them and that was how the question arose.— Mr. Brandreth said it showed how important it was for them to study the Manchester Ship Canal Bill, because the company would take advantage of every little slip they possibly could do if the Council did not watch its own interests.—Mr. Whodward asked what their respon- sibility was in lighting the roads. Both roads were County Council roads.—The Chairman was afraid they were getting out of their place.—Mr. Brandreth moved that the Cierk write to the Warrington Gas Committee stating that the Council did not feel themselves liable for the lighting of the bridges.—The resolution was secmded and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>AN ALLEGED NUISANCE AT THELWALL,</head>
        <p>AN ALLEGED NUISANCE AT THELWALL,</p>
        <p>Mr. C. W. Harvey, ef Liverpool, agent to Mr. Stan- ton, wrote stating that the brook which passed along the old bed of the river Mersey, under Greenfield House, Thetwat!, and which afterwards passed under the Ship Canal into the river Mersey, was very much polluted by works and other means :higher, upli the brook in the parish of Lymm. It was not only offensive, but must be very prejudicial to health. Complaints had been made at various times to Local Authorities with no avail. He, therefore, urged the Council to have the cause of the nuisance removed. The Clerk stated that the letter was addressed to him as Clerk to the County Council. He had kept a copy of the letter and sent the original en to Mr. Potts.</p>
        <p>The Council decided to dhere to a former resolution on the matter which was to await of the action being taken by the Mersey and Irwell Joint Committees.</p>
        <p>THE ISOLATION HOSPITAL QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The Clerk of the Cheshire County Council writing with reference to the Isolation Hospitals Act, 1893, asked whether the Rural District Council intended providing an Isolation Hospital for their district as suggested by the County Medical Officer of Health, and if so, when?— Mr. Woodward: Had we not batter answer" We don't know." (Laughter.)—A committee was appointed to consider the matter. MBDICLL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams, the medical officer, reported that one case of scarlet fever had occurred at Aston, one at Preston Brook, three at Sutton Weaver, and one case of typhoid fever in Frodsha,m Lordship. He had also received noti- fication of a case of diptheria at Aston Heath and one case of scarlet fever at Dutton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>SERBRING OPERATIONS AT lIELSBY.</head>
        <p>SERBRING OPERATIONS AT lIELSBY.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the solicitor of the London and' North-Western Railway with reference to a proposed sewer near Helsby Station, work in course of construc- tion by the Courcil, having for its object the introduc- tion of sewage matter into the drain pipes of the com- pany passing under the railway and adjoining land. 'I he joint companies were advised that the Council were not entitled to do what they proposed and could be re- strained from coming out their intention. They were, however,desirous of exhausting every means by which an amicable adjustment of the matter might be arrived at without resorting to litigation with a public body. With this view the engineer of the company had pre- pared a plan and section showing a mode of carrying out the objects of the Council in such manner as the com- pany might, subject to certain conditions, be advised to agree to it. It would be seen that the main object of the suggestion was to secure that the sewage should be so dealt with before reaching the companies' property that a clear effiaent should pass into their drains, which as the Council were aware were constructed under the authority of Parliament for surface drainage purposes only.—Mr. Diggle said that\it was never intended that the Council should parify the sewage. Be would ask them to postpone the matter and in the meantime he would prepare a report and submit it to the next meet- ing. Subsequently Mr. Woodward moved and Mr. Aaron' seconded the motion, which was passed, referring the matter to the Parochial committee. This concluded the business of the council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>BANK HOLIDAY.</head>
        <p>BANK HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>This general holiday.was thoroughly observed at Run- corn and many of the inhabitants made the most of tbe many excursions advertised by the Railway Company. Southport, North Wales, Liverpool, Blackpool, Chester. Frodsham, and Elelsbv were favoured by Runcornians and the departure of excursionists at the station made quite an animated spectacle. The Seamen's Institute scholars under the care of teachers and other responsible persons held their usual outing which took the form of a trip by canal to Grappenhall where an enjoyable after- noon was spent. Throughout the day the weather was fine but somewhat gloomy but. the latter fact only de- preciated a little of the enjoyment of the holiday.</p>
        <p>FRODSH/01. Visitors from all the surrounding towns caused a crowd of something like 2,500 excursionists to make Frodsbam the rendevouz fe.r the day,and trains and conveyances of every description, together with bicycles were impresse4 into the service of the visitors. The many attractions were thoroughly patronised, the hills as usual forming a staple source of pleasure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>W ESTON.</head>
        <p>W ESTON.</p>
        <p>On Bank Holiday, the Weston Point Wesloyans orga- nised an excursion up the Weaver to N9rthwich, and a capital outing was liberally supported and greatly enjoyed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>– F ItO DS RAM.</head>
        <p>- F ItO DS RAM.</p>
        <p>SUM OF PROPERTY.-0a Thursday evening at the Bear's Paw, Frodsham, Mr. A. Elutchinson offered for public competition the freehold dwelling-house with the nursery garden adjoining situate in Church-s ~ contain- ing lacre Ird. 28. recently occupied by Mrs. flodg- kinson. The nremises were disposed of to Mr. Henry Davies for £875. The solicitor in the matter was Ddr.J. Ashton, Frodsham and Runcorn.</p>
        <p>INQUEST.—On Tuesday Mr. H. Churton held an in- quest at Bridgedon, Prodsham, touching the death gaf William Peers,son of Mary Peers, who has been staying at a ladging-house some two months.—The evidence snowed that deceased, who was six weeks old had been a healthy child up to last Stturday when he became un- well and was poulticed. N,) doctor was called mu. —The jury returned 'a verdict of death from natural causes.</p>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>--.-.1.</p>
        <p>RAPID GRowni OF TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>The steady growtn in the tonnage using the pert ef Manchester, which has been observable since the begin- ning of the summPr, still continues. The tonnage entered and cleared during the month of July was more than 40,000 tons in excess of the figures for any previous month. As compared with the returns for Arne the in- ward traffic incr. aged Ly 14.691 net registered tons, and the outward traffic by 3.3,504 tons. More than half of the total tonnage Entered from or cleared for foreign ports. rho exact percentage being—foreign 5].67; coast- wise, 48.33.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>MATCH AT GRAPPENHALL. At the Dg and Dart Inn, Geappooliall, on Monday afternoon, An interesting howling match took place be- tween Mr. Shaw,of Warrington,and Mr. E !weird Stubbs of Grappenhall. The stake at issue was £lO, and in a game of 01 up the Warringtonian was quitkly installed favourite. He ja titled the confidence reposed in hint, and though Stubbs was in receipt of an allowance ot five points sta.rt,he failed to maintain his advantage. Filen half the game had been gone through Shaw had gained' a substantial lead, and although Stubbs made a gallant bid for houours in the last stage it was of no avail, and Shaw finally ran out the winner bv seven points, the score at the close being Shaw 101, Stubbs 94.</p>
        <p>Joseph Flirst,who was convicted at the last Avczes ot the murder of his illegitimxte child, was executed ii Strangeways Gaol on Tuesday. To,, executiopi was carried out in secret, in the presence of officials only, some of whom were examined at the statutory inquest and declared that everything had gone off in proper form.</p>
        <p>RUN CORN URBAN DLSTRICT COlll% CIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held in the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Thursday morning. Mr. F. Wiswall was voted to the chair in the absence of Mr. J. W. Lightburn, and there were also present—Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P,. C.C., E. Imison, C.C., J. Horse&amp;ld, H. J. Grice, G. H. Kirkham, T. Dean, S. Hewitt, D. Bisbrowne, F. A. Lake (law clerk) and E. Marshall (secretary.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>HIGHWAY COMMITTER'S MINUTES.</head>
        <p>HIGHWAY COMMITTER'S MINUTES.</p>
        <p>The following are recommendations of the committee: —That the footway in Bridge-str• et between Penketh- lane and Ellesmere-street be reconstructed at an esti- mated cost of £9O; that 300 yards of new channels be put down in Halton-road; that the tender of Mr. J. Robinson, Church-st., for the repairing of street lamps for the ensuing three years be accepted; that the plan of Mr. John Ball of alterations and additions to Fern Cottage, Heath-road, be approved ; that the plan of Mr. Walter Collier of proposed conversion of house in Green- way-road into a lockup shop and a shop and dwelling- house be approved; that the plan of Mr. Charles Timmins of proposed alterations and additions to" Rockfield," Weston-road, be approved. Mr. Taylor proposed and Mr. Bisbrowne seconded the motion that the minutes be adopted, and this was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. McDougall reported that the number of deaths registered during the past month amounted to 30. Three of these were inquest cases, and of the remaining 27 two were due to phthisis, four to cancer, four to misen- teric disease, three to kidney disease, six to diarrhotA, two to naralysis. two to senile decay and one ccse to peritonitis, convulsions and heart disease. The thirty deaths show an average death rate for the twelve month of 19 04 and exclusive of the inqtsest cases, 17-05 ofa the estimated population. Nine deaths. were of infants under 12 months. Forty-three births and 65 deaths were registered during the correF- ponding months of last year.—Dr. Gastaldi asked the reason for the increased death rate.—Dr. McDougall pointed out that cases of diarrhea were prevalent at this time of the year. and there was an increase of that and similar diseases caused through errors of diet. The death rate was the ordinary death rate and there was no deaths from infectious diseases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>ERALTI COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>ERALTI COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee reccuwnended that' the tender of Mr. Thomas Leach and Son for the supply of the sanitary inspector's uniform consisting of a coat,a vest ,two pairs of trousers and a cap be Eccepted. Mr. Handley meved the adoption of these minutes. With regard to the health of the town they would have heard from the dr,ctor's report that there had not been one death during the month from infectious diseaseq. He thaught they might congratulate themselves on that fact,and it was perhaps one good result of the Notifica- tions Act that they were able te take steps to prevent the spread of infectious disease.—Mr. Dean seconded the resolution which was carried.</p>
        <p>AN ACCEPTED TENDER. In the minutes of the Water Committee. Messrs. Simp- son. Davies and Son's tender to supply 1,200 tons of Bridgewater Trustees Worsley Tip Stack to be delivered is such quantities as may he required within twelve months at the Top Locks. Runcorn, or at the Runcorn Railway Station in the event of the Bridgewater Canal being frozen be accepted.—Mr. Stylor moved and Mr. Kirkham seconded the confirmation of the committee's minutes. PARLIAMENTARY AND GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee submitted the following items :—That the bills of costs of Messrs. Baker and Co., Parliamentary agents, and Mr. F. A. Lake, law clerk, ia respett of the opposition to the Port Sanitary Order, be taxed,and that this council be not represented at such taxation • an that the expenses of deputation to London, Manchester and Liverpool in respect of the Port Sanitary Order, Diversion of Stream, tec., amounting to £9 103. be paid. These minutes were passed on the motion of Dr. Gastaldi, seconded by Mr. Horsefield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>FIN&amp;NCE COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>FIN&amp;NCE COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee recommended that the offer of the Corporation of St. Helens to lend £2,500 for a term of six years at three per cent. interest be accepted. (Requited for Private Street Work, urinals, water cart, &amp;c.)—Mr. Imison moved, and Mr. Handley seconded the resolution adopting the minutes. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>TRADE IMPROVEMENT COOMITTE E.</head>
        <p>THE TRADE IMPROVEMENT COOMITTE E.</p>
        <p>Dr. Gastaldi asked whethe-r—iflabcMittee formed for the purpose if possible of improving the trade of the town had anything to report. It was now four months since its formation, and it would be interesting to the town to know if anything had been done.—The Chair- man painted out that neither the chairman or Vic, - chairman of the committee were present to answer Dr. Gsstaldi.—Dr. Gasraldi said that he would give notice that at the next meeting he would move that a deputa- tion be sent to the London and North-Western Railway Company praying for a reduction of the charges in favour of the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>TRH WATER DRATTIRT AT VIE LAY-EYE.</head>
        <p>TRH WATER DRATTIRT AT VIE LAY-EYE.</p>
        <p>Dr. Gastaldi stated that he had been given to under- stand that 24 feet of water had to be permanently kont at the lay bye, hut the fact was that vessels drawing 22 feet of water could not approach the lay-bye. He thought the Council sir uld take some action. in the matter and see that justice was done. Mr. Hewitt said he considered it very necessary that the draught of water should be maintained. Several vessels had been unable to get to the laybye and it was impor- tant that it should be dredged out. The Chairman thought that if that was correct a letter should be sent to the Canal Company.—On•the advice of the Chairman Dr. Gastaldi moved that the surveyor make an inspection of the laybye, and if necessary write to the Canal Company.—Mr., Hewitt seconded the motion which was unanimously carried. This terminated the work of the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—MORE GOVERNMENT DEFEATS. The House of Lerda went. into Committee on Thursday on the Irish Land Bill. The Government were defeated on the first amendment that came to a division. On the vote, 125 voted far the amendment and 67 against, the Government being thus defeated by 58. The announce- ment of the figures was received with cheers LORD CLONCURRY moved an amendment to clause 41 limiting the operation of the Bill as regarded pas' era land to tenancies of the rateable value of £so.and under' and not £lOO as proposed by the Bill. Lords Erne' Macnaghten, and Clonbrock suppored the amendment • Lord Lansdowne could not accept the amendment, and the Duke of Devonshire opposed it. He said they had heard some observations from noble lords from Ire- land complaining of the conduct of the Government to- wards them, considering all they and their friends had done for the Government. Be thought they would do well to remember that ample notice was given before the general election of the general lines on which the Government intendld to legislate in this matter. If they rejected the proposal of the Government they would put an end to any prospect of the bill either 'giving tsatisfac- tion in Ireland or being a.settlement of that question for any length of time. If this bill did not pass it would he a denial of a measure of justice which had been offered by a Unionist Gosernment. The withdrawal of this concession which the Government, under no compul- sion, had offered to the tenants could not but produce keen disappointment. On a division the amendment was carried against the Government by 107 votes to 60 Clause 4, as amended was added to the hill. Lord MAO. NAGwrics moved to leave out clause 5, dealing with town parks, and the reanlutien was carried, again a ainst the Government, by 96 to 77. Clause 8 was under discus- sion when the House rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>FORESTERS AND OLD-AGE</head>
        <p>THE FORESTERS AND OLD-AGE</p>
        <p>PENSIONS.</p>
        <p>At the sitting of the Foresters' High Court at Dundee on Thursday the question of State-aided old•age pensions was raised,and Owr considerable discussion a resolution was passed declaring that" in the opinion of the meet- ing State-aided pensions would he detrimental to the best interests of friendly societies." There were 329 votes in favour of this motion and 56 against, and the decision against State aid was greeted with load cheers. In the course ot the debate it was stated that only three members of the Order were subscribing to the Order superannuation fund.</p>
      </div>
