      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>MOST READERS</head>
        <p>MOST READERS</p>
        <p>Have had some experience of sick rooms, either as patients or attendants. How many such are there who have not felt the weakness or debility which follows the sickness, more discouraging than even the weakness it- self P Though the disease may have given way before the timely application of pruper remedies, and though.in cnnsf-quence. no immediate danger is to be apprehended, the slow progress towards convalescence is very dis- heartening to patient and attendant alike.</p>
        <p>Hew often do we hear people say :—" Yes. The fever has left him. He is free from any immediate danger. But he is so weak. If only he could regain strength ! " This debilitating effect of fever and other diseases is indeed a great source of anxiety to medical men as well as to their patients; and anything which would tend directly to shorten the period between the departure of the fever and the complete recovery of strength would be naturally welcomed by both doctors and patients.</p>
        <p>This is one of the reasoas why Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is so popular, why doctors prescribe it so largely, why chemists sell it so generally, why patients have re- course to it so readily, and why everybody has a good word to say for it. Not only does it attack the disease in its stronghold, not only does it drive the evil from the system, but it gives back directly the strength of which the system has been deprived by illness; it infuses new life into the blood, and the recovery of health and strength, instead of being, as it otherwise would be, dis- couragingly slow, intermittent, and uncertain, becomes, when this excellent medicine is resorted to.encouragingly rapid, regular, and certain. It is therefore confidently recommended as an unrivalled tonic medicine, and may be safely used by all who suffer from any form of weak- ness or debility from whatever cause it may arise.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. YOUNG, of 107, Laxton-street, New Brompton, Chatham, writing to a friend, says : "I thank you very much for sending me the GWILY3I EVAINS' BITTERS handbill. I have taken two bottles, a 23. 9d. and a 4s. 6i. It has done me more good than anything I had ever tried before, and I am so pleased with it that I shall tecommend it to every one who comes to see me."</p>
        <p>Givilynt Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic, sold by 3,11 Chemists and Patent Madicine Vendors in the Kingdom. Used everywhere; praised by everybody. Agents in all parts of world. Prices—Pottles 23. 9d., 4s. 61., cases of three large bottles 123. Si. Sent at above prices free to any address, by parcel post, direct from the Proprietors—</p>
        <p>QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING Co., LTD., LLANELLY, SOUTH WALES.</p>
