      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>BEFORE TH E MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE TH E MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A HARDLT-TRIZATED TRAVELLER:-HB OVERPAID HIS FARE.- _</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning at the Warrington Borough Court, Jehn Welsby, 10, Birchall-street, was charged with travelling from Earlestown to Warrington on Mon- day evening without having paid his fare; he was further charged with assaulting Detective Parsons, at Bank Quay Station.—Railway Detective Parsons said that on Monday night at 9 5 the prisoner came from Rainhill and gave up a ticket issued from Rainhill to Earlestown. He was asked for a ticket from Earles- town to Warrington by Collector Blinston but be gave none up and refused to pay his fare or give his name and address. Eventually he seized witness by the throat and struck him on the chest. P.C. Turner assisted witness to take the man into custody.—C hector Blinston cor- roborated.—Defendant said he had paid his tare and produced his ticket from Rainhill to Warrington which he said he had found that morning while in the cell. He had had drink the night before and croild not find it.— The Chief Constable said he had paid ss. to the Railway Company too much. He had somehow lost his wits and bought two tickets.—The magistrates fined defendant is.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>AN UNFORTUNATZ MILITIAMAN.</head>
        <p>AN UNFORTUNATZ MILITIAMAN.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. Geddes and Fleming at the Warrington Borough Court on Wednesday, Henry D'Arcy was charged with absenting himself from the annual train- ing of the 4th Rtgal Dublin Fusiliers in June last.—The Chief Constable said that defendant was here on the le'h July on this charge and pleaded guilty.—Tlan magistrates committed him to Strangeway's gaol to await escort. He had remained there ever since, and the Governor of the gaol had had to write to the mili- tary authorities to remind them of his existence and deal with him. The result was that he had been handsd about from one tribune to another, and now remained for the magistrates to deal with. He remarked that such occurrences were not extraordinary with the military authorities. Defendant had suffered enough. —The Clerk informed the bench that the lQwest they could fine defendant was 403. or 7 days. They could sentence without hard lahour.—Defendant was fined the mini- mum, penalty 40s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGFI POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. r. Gerlle3 (chairman) and Mr. J.</p>
        <p>H. Fleming. ALLEGED Wire : ASSAULT.—Thomas Crane 6. Bank-street, was charged with assaulting and beating his w re. Mary Ann Cr,ine, on the 2nd August.—Comcl,inant stated that, on Sunday night defendant came home and demanded the key of the house from her. Some words passed and he eventually struck her and kicked her.—Mr. A. Browne appeared for defen 'ant and complainant in answer to him said she desired , a separation. Mr. Browne said his clie it was agreeable to this and applied for an adjournment so that a mutusi agree- ment, could be come to.—Complainant, asseated to this and left the Court for the settlement to be draws up. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY. —John Gilligan wee fined ss. and c for being drunk and disorderly in Citurch-street, on Twe,sclay night. THUftsDAY.—Before Alderman Sutton (in 'he chair), Alder- man Wright, and Mr. S. Chandley. AN OEqTESATE PAUPER.—No TOB &amp;CM, No WnRK.—An elderly inmate of the wor4house named Walter Lunn was rettising to perform his allotted task while an 'the workhous-, on Wednesday.—Defendant 131 aded Inmatell rgedo f vi not guilty, and said he wishel to be examined hy a doctor.— Mr. B. ihearer, enquiry officer, said the defendant. after being in prison for deserting his famay • erne back to the workhouse and slid he would not make any attempt to keep his family. He refused to work and told the officer on Wednesday morning that if he did no, get any tobacco he would do no wok. The Board had instructed that he be brought before the magistrates to he •lealt with. With regard to his being examined the doctor had ,OPII him daily while at the workhouse, and he could have been examined any time. He had cost the Union authorities £35 —A workhouse offidal corroi,orated, and said that defendant was examita-d by the doctor on Wednesday morning and pronouncA quit, fit for work. —The magistrates sent him to gaol for 21 days' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>WARRINGTON couNrY PE ['TY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON couNrY PE ['TY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. J. J. Bleckly (chairman,) mid Mr. William Houghton. TEMPIRARY AUTHORITY. —Henry' Smith was granted tem- porary authority to sell at the'- Church Inn " Golborne. CAW:LE/119 DRIVING.—John Ford wis fined 53, and costs for being asleep whilst in charge of a horse and waggon, in Sankey on the 11th July. INTENI,ERANCR —For being guilty of drunkenness and dis- orderly eonduat the following persons were dealt with.— Margaret Greener Burtonwood. fined 53. including costs ; James Beaty, drunk and dis nderly at Newtsn Station, fined 53. Including costs ; William Henderson. Croft, fined 53. including costs,</p>
        <p>A WOMEN'S QUARPF.L AT SANKEY BR/IXOE3. —F inn v Kirk, a marled woman, was charged with assaulting Elizab-th Jay. 116. Evelyn-street. Sankey Bridges on the 14th of July. —Mrs. Jay was charged with assaulting Mrs. Kirk.—Mrs. Jay said defendant called her out of a neighbour's house and some sy,rds ps-sed as to some alleged scandal. Defendant struck her -1. witness said she saw the blow at-tick. —Mrs. Kirk alleged that Mrs. Jay struck her first Ia the face and her statement was corroborated by a Miss Jackson.—Both summonses were dismissed.</p>
        <p>POACHING. —John Ar her pleaded guilty' to being in pursuit of game on land in the occupation of John sDombill at Great Sankey, on the 12th July .—Mr. B. A. White prosecuted on behalf of Lord Lilforri. He stated that defen lout with two other men with poaching dogs were se,n on the land in ques- tion by game-keepera They were chased and defendant was caught. —A fine of 59. and costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>FINP:D IXPEt CLIMBING A FENDR. —Junes tinsworth was eh rged wit damaging a fenee at Surtonwoad, on the 13th July, by climbing over it.—Jeremiah llayes, farmer,Burton- wood said he saw the defendant climb over his fence and told him he should summon him. The damage done was 3d. —A fine of is. and costs was imposed on defendant who was also ordered to pay th- damage.</p>
        <p>--.- STEALING Two TURNIPS. -Two boy;, William Latham and William Oakes, were final is. and costs each for stetting two turnios on the farm of Kr. Haye!..Bu!tnnwood.</p>
        <p>A VEvGkrui. EMPLoyE.—Patrick Clancy plea- dei guilty to a charge of ass tithing Samuel Pairclougb, on ¶le 3rd Aug.. at Burtonwood —Complainant stated that dgendant struck him beer use he had dismissed him from his employ—Defendant was fined 105. and costs. • A CYCLIST FINED.—For riding his bicycle on the footpath at Poulton on thq 29th July, Charles !.-7.icA man wts fined Is. nnd costs. UNT ST WEIGHTS.—Frank Worthington was charrd with havin in his possession at Rixton on the 20th July, two 531 b weights and one 141 b. weight which were unjust. and one wei,,hing machine unstamped.— He admitted the nffence, but explained that heilici not know tbe weights were wrong. —lnspector ..thenerd proved the case,,and defendant was fined 53. and L. with ths costs for the two offences.</p>
