      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>PARAFFIN LA.IIP TIIROWING AT WARRINGTO N.</head>
        <p>PARAFFIN LA.IIP TIIROWING AT WARRINGTO N.</p>
        <p>A WOMAN SERIOUSLY INJURED.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning at the Warrington Borough Police Court before Mr. J. Geddes (in the chair) and Mr. J. H. Fleming, John Hares, Berry's-yard, was charged with feloniously wounding Ann Hares,his wife,by throw- ing a lighted paraffin lamp at her on Tuesday evening. —Prisoner is a young man. He appeared before the bench without his jackal; and waistcoat. The Chief Constable said this was a very serious charge and it might possibly develop into a still more serious one. The offence. was committed under the fol- lowing circumstances. It appeared that for the last six weeks prisoner and his wife had been living at his wife's mother's house in Berry's-yard off Tanner's. lane. On Tuesday prisoner seemed to have commenced drinking at a early hour and during the day, being hard up,he pledged several articles of property for beer. Mrs. Hares remonstrated wito him and they quarrelled more or less intermittently the R hole of the afternoon. About 6 30 Mrs. Hares went home to her mother's with a black 'eye and complained of being assaulted by prisoner. She went into a neighbour's house and about 8 30 prisoner came horns and demanded to see his wife. The tnother-ie- law said she was not there, and prisoner said he would not leave until he got something to pawn for more beer. He threatened his mother-in-law with the result that the wife came to her mother's assistance. Prisoner on seeing her seemed to be lashed into fury and picked up an ash - pan which he attempted to thrc,w at her. Her mother kneeked it from his hand and he then picks up the fender but he was again disarmed. Then he seized the lighted paraffin lamp from the mantelpiece and said "I'll have a b--executien in this house." and then threw the lamp at her. It struck her with violence and then exploded and covered her with burning oil and flames. Her screams brought in the neighbours, and her mother threw a bucket of water over her—the worst thing to 10 as it made the fl 'mei worse. A woman then wrapped a garment round Mrs. Rarer, and prisoner apparently actuated by some feeling of humanity or fear for his neck, wrapped his coat round her. This put out the flumes and the woman was conveyed to the Infirmary. PC. Evans was informed and hastened to the scene. He told prisoner that he had come to arrest him, and he replied "I wish it had gone through her heart, I wish it had killed her." The Chief Constable concluded by applying for a week's remand, as owing to the wife's condition it would be a fortnight before she coeld anp-ar —if she lived. Her condition was such that she might die, and, he there- fore asked that the prisoner might be remanded to the cells so that if necessary her dying depositions might be taken in prisoner's presence. Prisoner was remanded until Thursday next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>VOLUNTEER SIGNALLING.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER SIGNALLING.</p>
        <p>On Bank .Holiday an a;tempt was made to accomplish something rather larger than is usual locally in volunteer signalling. A chain of stations was arranged extend- ing from Frodsham to Bowdon, a distance of sixteen or eighteen miles. Four intermediate stations, viz., on Norton Water Tower, Bellefield Hill (Hill Cliff.), Grappenh all Church Tower, and Lymm Water Tower, were used in addition to the terminal stations on Frodsham Hills and Bowdon Church Tower. Two of these stations were furnished by the signallers of the 3rd Volunteer Battalion Cheshire Regiment, under Sergeant T. Ellingham, two by the signallers of the first Volunteer Batta- licn South Lancashire Regiment, under Sergeant T. R Dennett, and two by the signallers of the 2nd Volunteer Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, under Sergeant S. Etheridge. The whole practice was arranged and supervised by Captain J. A. Edel- sten, Brigade Signaller, Cheshire and Lancashire Volunteer Infantry Brigade. The only instruments used were flags and telescopes, and as soma of the latter were not very powerful, a clear atmosphere became imperatively necessary. Unfortunately, during the earlier portion of the day, the sky was much overcast, but by four o'clock the haze to some extent cleared away and the sun had already made a slight appearance. Two hours of brisk signalling followed, and messages were quickly sent from end to end throughout the line, fully accomplishing all • that had been aimed at. It is an extremely rare occurrence for volunteer signallers in the North to attempt practical work of this magnitude. No branch of military work is more tedibus to master or more slender in its rewards than signalling, and the success of this practice is one more prof of the zeal of our citizen soldiers. VISIT OF TRH MERSEY MISSION TO GRAPPiNHALL.</p>
        <p>On Nlonday about 300 members of the Mersey Mission to Seamen, Runcorn, conducted by Mr. W. Shaw, visited Grappenhall. A field close to the village, which belongs' to Mr. Naylor, had been prepared for the party, who arrived about twelve o'clock. The afternoon was 'pleasantly spent in sports mid games. Tea was provided in the field, and after further games the party returned to Runcorn, leaving Grappenhall ahout half-past six.</p>
      </div>
