      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WATER. SUPPLY OF PENKETH.</head>
        <p>THE WATER. SUPPLY OF PENKETH.</p>
        <p>MEETING OF PAROCIEHAL ELECTORS. In accordance with a suggestion of the Warrington Rural Dktric, Council, the parochial electors of Penketh were on Tuesday evening given an opportunity of offer- ing any observations they might desire up-ma the subject of the water supply, when a meeting was held in the Day Star Technical Institute. Mr. J. C. Routledge (chairman of the Parish Council) occupied the chair, and amongst those pre&amp;ent were :—Messrs. Robert Garnett, J.P., C. E. Parker, J. Dignum, E. Aitken, W. Houghton, T. B. Tetlow' Longshaw, A. White, Newcombe, Wigfield, Hob- son, J. G. Withenshaw, R. Cook, E. W. Clarke, Eden, and J. Foster (clerk to the Council.) The Chairman said they would be aware that some two years ago the water question was very much to the front, and negotiations were made for a supply of water from Warrington but the matter fell through owing to the extravaganc9 of the quotations for the supply. The question of getting a supply from the Vyrnwy pipes was knocked on the head as Liverpoel could not supply Penketh without the consent of the Warrington Corpor- ation. They had now got an offer from the Warrington Cmporation to supply the township with their Winwick water which was certified to be first class, and the rate at which they offered to lay the pipes and fulfil all obli- gations was so reasonable that the Parish Council as well as the Rural District Council had decided to talc the supply. The meeting had simply been called to ob- tain the opinion of the township on the question. Mr. A. White wished to know why the authority at Warrington desired the opinion of Penketh if that meet- ing had no wvight, but simply to voice opinions. Mr. Dignum said the District Council while sensible of their own authority and power nought it would be cour- teous to the parishioners to first call them together and to have the scheme and the cost laid before them. There was no occasion to take any vote on the question ; the District Council alone had power to act. It was simply an act of courtesy on the part of the Council to ask for a meeting of ratepayers. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Eitm titoug. hi there should be a vote taken to see whether the people wanted water or not. Be did not think one cpaarter would vote for the scheme, and it was not right to push a thing upon them which they did not want.</p>
        <p>Mr. Newcombe said those who were opposed to the scheme should attend the meeting and express their views on the matter. The Chairman then read the letter from the Town Clerk of Warrington giving the terms for the supply of water. The initial cert would be £3,189, and towards this sum the Corporation would give £2OO. They would supply the township on agreeing to guarantee eight per cent, on the cost of laying mains including water rate, making an income of £240 per annum. Mr. Garnett : They have offered to charge Us id. per cent. lees than Padevate P—The Chairman : Yes. Mr. Newcombe asked how many houses there were in the township that would require a supply, and what was the income likely to be. The Chairman said there were 372 houses with a rate- able value of £4,000. Of these houses, 120 with an estimated rateable value of £1,350,had an unsatisfactory Supply of water both as regarded quantity and quality. He estimated that the in e)me for water would be as follows :—From houses. £l6O ; carriages, etc., £3O ; trade purposes, £5O ; total, £240. Even if the income did not come up to these exp .ctations, the deficiency would be met by the levying of a penny in the pound on the half-yearly rate. Mr. Aitken said great thanks were due to the Parish Council and the Chairman for the information which he had given them. Before saying whether he approved or disapproved of the scheme he should like to say that he thought it was very unfortunate that they should be called toeether to give an expression of opinion. If the Parish Council was elected for one purpose more than another it was to supply the township with water. The Council had made arrangements to do so, and the Rural Council had asked for an expre.esion of the ratepayers before aereeine to the request of the Parish Council. But the opinion of the ratepayers was worth very little. They had no power of resolution and no newer to vote. They might have a moral power. The ratepayer 3 had shown their disregard for the meeting by not attending in larger numbers. Could anyone doubt that they wanted a good water supply ? Many houies had no supply at all and the occupants were beholden to their neighbours for a supply. It was said at the Health Medieal Conference at Glasgow that a good water supply was as necessary to the health of the people as unadulterated food. He would move the following resolution "That this meeting thanks the Parish Council for the efforts made to procure a better water supply." WrightM. asked whether every effort had been made to get a supply of Vyrnwy water. Be saw a lot of rain tubs in use at Warrington. and he did not think the water from Warrington would be found very useful for some durp see. It would be better if they could get Vyrnwv water. The Chairman said they could not go to Vyrnwy un- less the Warrington Corporation were unable to supply them. Mr. Garnett said there was some little difference be- tween Vyrnwy and Warrington water, and he thought the diff.renee was a little in favour of Vyrnwy. But for drinking purposes generally there was no material difference. Some veers ago he had a correspondence with refereneei to Vyrnwy water and he made one or .two visits to Liverpool. He had been exceedingly anxious. to get Vyrnwy water, but he hoped the difficulties were such that they could not be overcome. T ley could not be supplied by Liverpool without the consent of War- rington • There was every possibility and some probabi- lity that in the course f time, as Warrington was in- creasing, they would he able to get Vernwy water in- stead of a supply from Warrington. He saw that the Vvrnwy water had now been tetnught as far as Cuerdley Moss, and the authorities would be glad to lay pipes as far as Penketh if they at Penketh could " square " War- rington, but they could not. He, (Mr. Garnett), had spent £lOO in trying to get water at Penketh, not only in sinking a well, but by putting down an engine. The only result was the finding of hard water. Mr. Carter Bell analysed it and pronounced it good wholesome water. hut in spite of that it was such that no one would drink it.</p>
        <p>Mr Hobson thought they would spoil themselves iby being too precipitate in the matter. If they held their hands a little longer they might be able to get better terms. He believed that the Warrington Corporation did not supply the whole of the tradespeople in the town with their water. If they deferred the matter at Pen- keth a little longer they mieht be able to get the Vyrnwy from Cuerdley. It would be far better if they could ob- tain that water. Mr. Garnett said he was not aware of any tradesman in Warrington who was not sappliediwith water by the corporaticn. For trade purposes water was brought from Hill Cliffe and for domestic purposes from Win. wick. The Winwick water was a little hard but there was no reasonable fault to be found with it.</p>
        <p>r. Dignum stated that he used Warrington water almost every day and he could say that it was as differ- ent as could be from the hardness of the water at Pen- keth. " Mr. Newcombe seconded the proposition. He hoped the expression of opinion would help the Rural District Council to come to a favourable decision. He had lived in Warrington and had had s me experience of the water. It was perfectly suitable for sanitary and do- mestic purposes generally. He did not see any objection to rain-tubs so Jong as they were kept clean. He thought from the more important aspect—the sanitary aspect— they ought to support the Council in their wishes and endeavours. On a vote being taken the resolution was carried,only two voting aaainst it. Mr. Parker in referring to the quality of the Warring- ton water said the amount of hardnf-is was 21 grains to the imperial gallon. whilat that which was got at Pen- keth ranged from 26 to 42 grains per gallon. It varied according to depth, but he had never seen it less than 26. He thought the District Council had been very much at fault in not getting Penketh a better water supply sooner. He advocated it some years ago but he was told that it was for his own end, viz., that of supplying the Tannery Company. When organic matter was entering the soil it was a source of danger to get their water supplies from wells, and inasmuch as the Distxict Council was the sanitary authority and res- ponsible for the sanitary condition of the township, he thought it was somewhat of a disgrace to that authority that they had not taken measures to do away with the constant dangers to health, long before the present time. The District Council had taken pains to remind them ' that the matter was in their hands, but if the matter rested with them, why had they been so negligent ? Wells were a source of danger and it was difficult to find out which were contaminated, until possibly an epi- demic broke out. He thought the District Council had been very weak. Possibly what had been done that night would enable them to have the strength of their convictions and let them have the water at once.</p>
        <p>_ The Chairman said that the London and North Wes- tern Railway Company had sent an official to the district with reference to the water supply to th 4 cottages at the station.</p>
        <p>Mr. White said they should write stating that through the 361fi911 action of the Warrington Corpqration the township had been kept out of a supply of water which they wanted.</p>
        <p>Mr. Garnett said the time for eetting the Vyrnwy water was when permission was applied for to lay pipes across the river near Penketh. He asked the Warrington %u- -thority at the titne to oppose the applicatien unless they agreed to supply Penketh with water, but he was out voted, and Penketh was suffering from it now. The meeting concluded with a vote of thanks to the Chairman.</p>
        <p>The Queen has caused a special medal, in silver, to be struck, for presentation to the humane French men and women of Ushant, Molene, and the neighbourhood, who rendered generous service to the few survivors of the wreck of the Drummond Castle and took reverent charge of the dead which the seas cait up on their shores. Isaiah Heaton was sentenced at Leeds Assizes on Tuesday to six months' imprisonment for the manslaugh- ter of his son at Bradford. The lad was killed by the discharge of a gun wlaich his father had fixed in a pigeon-cute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>The Paris correspondent of the "Telegraph "writes : —Some evenings ago the shrieks of a lady who was the solitary occupant of a cab which was passing down a dimly-lighted avenue attracted the attention of two pe- destrians who,atter ordering the driver to stop,advanced to the door of the vehicle to ascertain what was wrong and to offer their services. The cause of the alarm was explained in a moment. The cabman had left a leading thoroughfare for the purpose of reaching the destination by a short cut, and the lady, not knowing the locality, and being, moreover, unable to speak a word of French, had been under the impression that she was being con- veyed to some lonely spot where she would he plundered and perhaps murdered into the bargain. The affair was promptly arranged, and she proceeded on her way con- siderably relieved in her mind. Very different,however, have been the exporiences of various Frenchwomen of late,anG only on Saturday night a respectable Parisienne who was offered a lift by a passing cabman had good reason to regret her acceptance of the proposal. The man drove her off at a rapid rate in an opposite direc- tion, and when her appeals were unheeded ..he jumped out of the vehicle, only to be brutally assailed by the ruffianly cocher,who struck her repeatedly with his whip and fistP, and left her on the ground more dead than alive. The police are under the impression that these assaults are not committed by the regular Jehus, but by individuals to whom they sub-let their vehicles in defi- ance of the regulations. An investigation has been set on foot,and arrangements are to be made to remedy this unsatisfactory state of things.</p>
        <p>Very few people are aware (the "Chronicle" remarks) that the Sultan of Turkey is descended from a French woman. His great grandmother, Nachasadil Sultana, consort of Abdul Hamid 1., was born in the West Indian island of Martinique in the latter quarter of the eigh- teenth century. Her maiden name was Aimee Dubuc de Rivery. She was cousin and companion in childhood of Josephine de la Pagerie, who escaped from the guil- lotine, on which her first husband was beheaded to be- come Empress of the French. Mlle. de Rivery, on the completion of her education at a convent in Nantes, em- barked at Marseilles for the West Indies. She was ship- wrecked and rescued by a vessel on its way to Algiers. 'the vessel was captured by Algerian pirates, and the lady was taken prisoner and sold as a slave to the Bey, who in his turn made her a present to Abdul Hamid I. By him she became the mother of Mahmoud 11., Re- former, and grandfather of the present Sultan. In 1869 the Sultan Abdul Az:z gave the details of this story to the ex-Empress Eugenie, then in Constantinople on her way to Cairn, aid very gracefully claimed her as a cousin.</p>
        <p>John Burns is telling some interesting stories of the Socialist and Labour Congress last week. One would have thouglr, he remarks, that after all that had been said, the B-lgian, Dutch, and German delegates would have left him severely alone ; but, as a matter fact, he was more inquired for than ever last week. He showed many delegates over the House of Commons. Some he took over the House of Lords, and it was surprising to see the veneration with which they regarded the gilded Chamber. A few he regaled with tea on the terrace. One incident is significent and worthy of note. A veteran Dutch delegate accompanied the membPr for Battersea on a visit to the County Council Works Department,with 2,300 men employed, to the Lambeth Vestry depot with its 1,000 workmen, and to the big engineering works from which John was dismissed ten years since for tak- ing the part of the unemployed. " Mein Gott," said the Dutchman, when all had been explained to him. " I have seen mere real Socialism in five minutes than I have seen in all mine life in Amsterdam." " But," adds Mr. Burns with a sigh. "he had to go back and vote with his colleagues."</p>
        <p>It is not generally known why Mr. Aclison patents every little thing he discovers. In an interview a few days ago with a press representative, he gives his own explanation. " I discover," said Mr. Eiison, " a great many things that I would be glad to give to the public for nothing, but I dare not. I have to patent these things to save in% self defending lawsuits. There are a lot of sharks in this world, who are continually on the look out for new things. And when one of them hears of something new ha bustles to the Patent Office to see if it is patented. If it is not, he claims it as an original dis- covery, and files his claims. Then he will begin a suit against the very man who invented the thing, for mak- ing or using it. The inventor will say, " But I discovered this thing first ; Tarn the inventor." He is reforte :! to the Patent Office, where he finds the official claim of original invention. The fact that the papers are filed long after he made his discovery does not help him, for all the oth-r man does is to hire a fellow to swear that he made the discovery a month or two prior to the date the inventor claims. It sounds ridiculous, probably, but it is a fact that there are often races between the inventors and the sharks to reach the Patent Office, the sharks have had early information about the inventor's dis coverv. There are many such races, and thousands of pounds depend on each one. What I say is literally true. I patent things, and no doubt many other inventors patent things, just to save defending lawsuits.</p>
        <p>A private who belongs to the Derbyshire Regiment has received a letter from a comrade who, with his com- pany, is doing. patrld duty at Portland Convict Prison, in which he states that since he has been at Portland he has met with a number of notorious criminals, including Jabez Balfour and Wells of Monte Carlo. Balfour has been put to work in the quarries—the hardest form of work—and, in the words of the writer, "he appears to have lost all his old fat." Balfour is said to be in a very depressed condition, but Wells, it would appear, is quite the reverse. He is cheerful and a good conduct man, aril, being t musician of some proficiency, frequently plays the organ in the gaol school.</p>
        <p>It is indeed an ill-wind that blows nobody any good. The troubles in which the Chartered Company has . be- come involved have carried distress into many a home, but in the district of Beaufort West, the donkey-rearing region of the Cane Colony, there is jubilation. Such has been the demand for the humble but useful " Neddies " on behalf of the Chartered Company for transport pur- poses, that the poorest and most used-up Animals have been successfully brought into the market, and the owners have reaped a golden harvest to the tune of nearly £lO,OOO The only sorrowf,,l reflection in con- nection with the matter is that many of the animals will have to work in physical pain.</p>
        <p>A good innuse-trap is a desideratum. The house cat treats mice ton much as the sportsman treats foxes—does not get rid of them. The time-honoured wire-bars,with a piece of cheese dangling behind, are a warning rather than a trap. One of our readers says the "Daily Chronicle." calls the attention to the latest thing in mouse-traps, one advantage of which is that no patent rights are connected with it. He sends us a sketch of a photograph which is beinc, exhibited by Mr. W. Cran- dall in a fishmonger's shop in High-ltreet. Tunbridge Wells. It represents three mice held fast by an oyster. their heads not being visible. The fishmonger informed our corresianndent that the murderous bivalve was one of fifty taken home by a customer and placed on the pantry floor to be eaten for supper. It is supposed that the oyster gaying for water exposed to Messieurs Mice a tempting morsel, which they went for, with the calami- tous result shown in the photograph.</p>
        <p>A enmnyrrise betwern cremation and the ordinary syntem of burial has been arrived at in the conatructi DO of the new kind of coffin favoured by the Sanitary Burial Association. The coffin presents the same out- ward appearance an those customarily used.the improve- ment anrolYine solely to the interior. The bottom, sides, and inner lid are arranged in panels filled with fine powder of which 11,,e principal ineredient is quicklime. The panels thus filled are covered so as to resemble ex- nctly tha padding now in use. By the action of the nawder the body,after being in the coffin tweive months, is practically consumed. The inventors of the new coffin also assert that in cast.; of exhumation medical scrutiny is greatly facilitated by its use. Traces of poison are not destroyed, because the Preparation tined in filline the panels transforms it into insoluble precipi- tate. Thus one chief objection to cremation is removed, while every concnsninn is made to those who stand by funeral customs lone established. The adoption of the new system by workhouses and other institutionn would be unattended,it is asnerted,by any very apprzciable ad- dition to present burial expenses.</p>
        <p>The grim work of canipaionint- in Rhnd"esia is en- livened by some arruling incidents. Recently some volunteers. yearning for the garden ornduce of a mild Indian coolie cultivator just outside Bniawaro.conceived the brilliant idea of ridino• down the road, firing a fuaL lade, and shnutimi to the Coolie to clear to the !lager for his life. as the enemy were coming. 'Net morning." says a local paper. "four eminent agriculturists crept on hands anti knees amnrwst the plants, and filled a sack with the health-giving cabbage and the breath-scentinn orinn. We had just finished when we heard a Es.:ntle voice sac, Thlee pounds, please. You binnga Captain men ; thlee pounds, please,' and sad and sorrow- ful we shelled out."</p>
        <p>Mr. Ernest T. Honler. chairman of the cycling syndi- cate that recently acqnired Trafford Park, Manchester. bids fair shnrtly tn b one nf our largest landed pro- prietors. Since the taeginninrr of the rear ho cannot have purchased less than 12.000 acres. His latest pur- chase is Hatley Park. a magnificent domain in Cam- bridgeshire, which has been in the market for some time. Mr. Holey is a keen man of business.and the fact of his investing so largely in landed estee suggests better times in store for the market.</p>
        <p>Mrs PONSONBr'S HAIR ItslsToanc. a 'great discos Try, a Most Refreahinq Wash, Marvellous Results, Innumerable Testimonial, Patroniaed by Royalty. In great demand by Professionals, makes the Hair Grow, prevents Bald- ness, Invigorating, restores hair to its natural colour, gives healthy aryl youthful appearance. Most astonish- iing results ; 2s 6d Bottle, post free.—Address Mrs. PON. wax, 25, Linle-st., Liverpool—Advt:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>FREELAND PIONEERS.</head>
        <p>THE FREELAND PIONEERS.</p>
        <p>Few people in England have, says a correspondent the " Manchester Guardian." realised what iminen7i interest and excitement the Freeland movement arouse' in Europe during the first half of this decade. D.r: Hertzka's book was trinslated into English, Fren Norsk, Swedish, Danish and Roumanian ; Freeland groups were formed in all the Continental townS, fl collected weekly subscriptions for the scheme ; the Free. • land cyclists of Vienna and elsewhere covered the coati" try districts with an active prop iganda. Die hope vio to colonise a large tract of Eastern Africa with EuroPelf, settlers, on the basis of free capital and free land ; new State would lend capital and free lane ; the new State would lend capital to groups of associated worked who would be bound to publish their balance-sheete to admit into their ranks any who cared to join. Ono while the main outline of Dr. Hertzke's plan is o,ee known, the details of the pioneer expedition still buried in the columns of the Vienna " Freiland," thought as on account of a practical experiment they ars". more real value than the Utopia on which the well' ment was based. By October, 1893, 158 pioneers had sent in 05 names and six months later there were 4„ volunteers, all pecuniarily and physically qua". fled for the work. The Committee had OW an ample field for selection, and ultimate! sent out 35 members who were in every sense picked; men. Most r,f these were Germans and Austrians, be there were also six Englishmen in the list, and some le: presentatives of other nationalities. All eittaiir cont buted at least £5O er had that sum advanced by Free. land groups on their behalf. Six were well-Fowl/1); African explorers, and th-re were army officers, phYs'' cians,engineers, fermers,carpenters, and builders; everr one was master of some necesary trade,anci was strong" hopeful, and young. A complete list of the rEquisit! supplies of the expedition covers six columns of " Free land," and these were all furnished be the coinmittee• But eifts also came pouring in ; the list includes then Tana, a steel steamship with engines of forty-hors° pawer and only drawing two feet of water, which vilt designed to ascend the river whose name jr bore, and w tug after it the colonists and their belongings to their new home. On February 27, 1894, there was a gre° banquet at Flensburg, at which Mr. U inev rePre; sented the English group, and the following d% the first fifteen sailed. They were under the ansolut' command of Captain Schindler,an Austrian, and had' hood p tseage. Caerain Schindler reports that there oran 301/le quarrelling on the voyage, but that, on the whole' harmony was well preserved. Meanwhile Wilheim had already been some time Lamu. The committee had appointed him leaderi , with full powers both over the futicla anc, the people. He was expected to consult with the °the' members, but was not bound to go by their advice,' When the new censers arrived on April Ist he had ne'' ready made ample preparations. He had rented a hous° for the Freelanoere and built considerable additions ; had made an excursion to the mouth of the Tana ; had secured the active co-operation of Go tag Denhard' who knew more than anyone else about the Tana district, and who wa.s personally friendly with all the nati/e chiefs. H steoesa the local labour question, and to the apparently serious conclusion that, while the slati„i trade deserved all the evil thiees that had been Sal' about it,the slaves were themselves incomparably better off than the European wage-earners. They had erne subsistence, short hours, and certainty of livelihoew When he explained to them the condition of European, workers,they said hey would rise in open revolt again an attsmpt to reduce them to the level ° modern civuliation. He inquired into land tenure' and found that out there the idea of buyill land had never entered into people's heads ; every ala„ had a right to what land he cultivated, no more and 0" less. The Freela.nders, in fact, instead of establishing $ new system, were simply adopting that of the natives._A The first letters from the pioneers express unnounde" delight with their new surroundings. They had era pected to be compelled to rough it at once, but instea of this found theinselvee established in a luxurious Ina,n; sion, and enjoyiee all the delights of the tropics. within three weeks violent quarrels and dissensll, broke out. Dr. Wilhelm could only compare the neeP.„ to a set of peevish and irritable schoolboys, and charn; ably laid the blame on the climate. When he establisnef, a board of arbitration to deal with trifles the arbitrato7; themselves could never agree. If hard work had be, possible things might have gone better, but everyooceies patience broke down under the strain of intolerable , lay. Nothing could be done without the Tana, for Wilicht there had been no room in the steamer which brot2_l7:st the people ai Government pinnace having been thrhuen into the space chartered by the Freelanders), and w"„er the next vessel arrived with it off. Lunn a month lauve there was so violent a storm that no cargo coolcLeo landed, and the I ana was taken on to Zanzibar. g the rainy season was unexpectedly late and Prevettie 70,.. bar to all action during the month of May. and bY eed sevens,begin nal do fija harmonious ntihoeus people 7glivss werealready alladv at sixes e seemed, 'tee possible. How far the obvious disintegration Lao expedition led to the next calamity it is inj°l;, bar to say. But on June 11 the Sultan of 1.4 clijOC. stepped in and forbade any move in the mab tion of the interior, on the groend than aT3 rbiascinkgeamhigimhtutp, akeannilancnlly e.aThperolhoicbaitl ;605 t their Protectorate. And they acted as well as threatene _ for when a small party of three did make an exeorel?.; edEangnylissh etatluet hrne°ll ; % into the interior none of the natives would trade them or furnish supplies, saying that, much as tu wished to do so, it had been expressly forbirlden• coherent and d ,termined body of pioneers would P... 7; bably have found some way out of the impasse; these men were now neither coherent nor oeterrnine; , and they simply resolved to accept the situation and .11% back home. The main body returned in the beginolee of July a few, including Dr. Wilhelm, stayed to some pioneering on their own account, but even 571,, helm came back in the following February. A little £5,000 h'el been spent on the expedition. £4 200 e. which was met by subscriptions and members' contkri; butions ; Dr. der'zka paid the balance. The shell of organisation is still maintained, and this year neVtiii, tions have been in progress for a settlement in Iraso'l but nothing definite has been decided on. The lesson is these pioneers seems to be that the essential element io the suceess of any scheme of practical Socialism neither a beautiful ideal, nor capital, nor even skill ale experience, all of which the Fieelanders possessed 0, abundance, but a spirit of self-sacrifice which will e able those who take part in the experiment to yield $ minor points in order to secure harmony and union. ,</p>
      </div>
