      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>letter to the Obitor.</head>
        <p>letter to the Obitor.</p>
        <p>THE BOARD OF GUARDIANS AND SUPERANNUATIONS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l consider my writing to you is the best means of hearing the tenor of the Warrington ratepayers on a matter of no little import to them. Although I left your neighbourhood about three years ago, still as an old Warriegtonian and ratepayer, I take a great interest in it, and am kept in " touch " with it by means of your useful paper as well as through others published in War- ington. I was extremely surprised to see in your pub licatieln of Saturday last, that toe Board of Guardians had;../at their meeting, decided to supor-innuate Mr. Mines, superintendent of thO Workhouse schools. Now, I well remember—as somo .of the old Warringtonians may also—that in the case of the late matron of he Union, who resigned her position through no fault of her own, the members of the Board of Guardians did not think they ought to use the ratepayers' money in giving her a pension ; surely, if the superintendent deserves one, she did also. My reason for drawing the attention of the ratepayers to this matter is, that it is they wha will have to pay the superannuation. Why not deal uniformly with public officials and public money ? I should very much like to hear the views of your readers upon this matter. Trusting that I may be favoured by your inserting this, I am, yours faithfully, CANDOUR. Manchester, July 30, 1896.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>FLOWER SHOW AND ATHLETIC SPORTS</head>
        <p>FLOWER SHOW AND ATHLETIC SPORTS</p>
        <p>AT HELSBY,</p>
        <p>On Saturday the annual sports and flower show con- nected with the Helsby Athletic Ctub took place in the cricket field. The weather was fine and the show was in every respect a great success. The entries in both departments were in advance of those of previous years. Mr. James Taylot is the president, and. Dr. Briant acted as general secretary, and the Flower Show Committee consisted of Messrs. J. Dallimore and the Rev. E. Char- ley. The judges were—Messrs. Gaiger (of Messrs. Dickson's, Chester) and Wakefield (gardener. Newton Hall, Chester). A very nice collection of herbaceous plants, not for competition,was sent by Messrs. Dickson. A special feature was the honey section, in which the exhibits numbered over 100. In the clasi opm to the United Kingdom there were 25 exhibits. Dr. Jones,first class expert of the British Beekeepers' Associatian, .cted as judge, and he also gave two demonstrations on bees and bee-keeping, under the auspices of the Cheshire County Council. In this branch the secretarial duties were performed by the Rev. E. Charley, secretary of the Chester Branch of the Lancashire and Cheshire Bee- keepers' Association. The following is a list of the prize winners :</p>
