      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>TEIE SPORTS.</head>
        <p>TEIE SPORTS.</p>
        <p>The prograronse presented was a fairly good one, and the excellent prizes attracted numeroes entries in the varioes events. In the firemen's competition, one man hydrant drill, the prizes were won-1, J. Abbatt ;2, G. Rush; 3, J. Worrell. Four men turn-out drill. G. Rush, G. Abbatt, W. Hoose and G. Thompson. The 120 yards boys' race, handicap, was run in two heats, the winners In the first being—A. Hopley, L. Brandreth and W. Davies; and the second—H. Brandreth, F. Leach and W. -Clarke. The final resulted-1, A. Hopley, Dunham ;2, H. Brandreth, 13elsby ; 3, L. Brandreth, Helsby. The 100 yards race, handicap, was run in six heats, the entries numbering 38, and the winners in the final wera —F. Hopley, Helsby, six yards' start J. Leathwood• Runcorn, two yards; and R. Hinde, Elelsby, six yards, The race was an exceedingly good one. The 100 yards saele race, handicap, resulted-1, W. Johnson, Bridge Trafford, scratch ; T. Garner Elton, four yards ; and E. Davies, Elelsbv, three yards. The awards in the mem- bers' football dribbling race went to-1, T. Barnes; 2, W. Jones • and 3. J. Jones. The race to the top of the hill and back proved very interesting, and the time in which the performance was done was eight minsites 57 secondg, which was remarkly quick. C. Leach, Helsbv, was first: J. Goodall, Frodsham, 2; and W. Jelingon, Bridge Trafford, 3. In the half mile race, handicap, the entries numbered 18, half of whom turned out. The winners were-1, R. Clays. Runcorn, 35 yards Start; 2, F. Aedrews, Frodsham, 40 yards ; and -3, J- CL Webb, Frodsham, 15 yards. The winners in the 75 yards rick-a-back race, Hopley; Elelsby, and Leach, Al- vantev. were -first; R. ninth., and C. Hinds HelstY, second ; and J. Ainsworth and J. Newark, Helsby, third Members' eight runs between wickets race resulted-1. J. Brookirg ;2, T. Hinds ;3,J. M. Harnaman. The 220 yards obstacle race handicap, proved very amusing. In the 11-4 t heat John Owen. Trafford. scratch, waS Ist, J. .6. Webb. Frodsham. two yards, 2ad, and .1. Brooking, Belshy, five yards, 3rd, Second heat. 1, W. Johnson, Ilridee Trafford, scratch; 2, T. Garner. Elton, nine yards; 3. F. Abel. Runcorn, eight yards : The final ended-1, John Owen ;2, J. G. Webbe :3, J. Brooking.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>PADGAT E.</head>
        <p>PADGAT E.</p>
        <p>FLOWER SERVICE AT CRAM CRURCH —The annual fl )wer service was held in Christ Church on Suedav afternoon. The children attending the Sunday school marched in processional order to the church, wtiere they were joined by the Industrial School children. l'he ser- vice was conducted Sy the Rev. J. J. Lang,ham, M.A. The ceremony was of a special character.and (luring the mnging of the two hymns commencing " Oh gather the, flowers together "and "Here, Lord, we offer Thee all' that is fairest," the children took their offerings of .flrmers and fruit into the chancel, where they were placed on and around the altar by the Vicar and Mr. Peter Rathbone. An address was given by the Rev. J.J. Langham.</p>
        <p>The Duke of Devonshire presided at a meeting at Devonshire Ilouse on Tuesday and presented Mr. /19ras- ton,Fecretary of the Liberal Unionist Association, with a silver bowl and a cheque for 1,500 guineas, in recogni tion of his services to the Liberal Unionist party during the last ten years. There was a large attendance of Liberal Unionist members of Parliament.</p>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN A.ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DISTRICT SCHOOLS, EARLESTOWN.-NOTICE TO PARENTS.</p>
        <p>The Midsummer Vacation is extended by one week, so that the re-opening of the school will no', take place until Monday, August 17th.—Alex. BROWN, headmaster.</p>
        <p>NF.WTON HORTICULTURAL SOCIETY'S Snow AND</p>
        <p>SPORTS.</p>
        <p>From an advertisement in another column it will be seen that the above show and sports will be held on the Earlestown Football Ground on Saturday next. There are a large number of entries for the variou3 events,o.nd given a fine day,the affair should be a decided success.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION CLASSES. The results of the Science and Art exam- inations in freehand and model held in connection with the above classes are as follows :—Freehand : W. Bailiff, W. Drummond,T. H. Gee, G. A. Harrison, T. C. Higgins, L. Johnson, and C. T. Seddon, E 2nd. Model: Theo. Clare, M. A. Davies, W. J. Spencer,T. Wilkinson, E 2nd.</p>
        <p>BANK HOLIDA.Y AT EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>This district was remarkably quiet on Monday, owing doubtless to a very large number of the in- habitants leaving for the various places of popular resort, such as Blackpool, Southport, &amp;c. All the works, with the exception ot the Vulcan, were closed,and all the tradesmen clostd their establish- ments for the day. ST. MARY'S R.C. SCHOOL, NEWTON'. The following is an extract, from the Inspector's re. purt of the examination recently held in connec- tion with the above school :—Mixed School: The general order and behaviour of the children and the smartness and industry they display in their ex- amination are highly pleasing and creditable. They again distinguish themselves in their work. .their oral performances being unusually bright and intel- ligent, and their written exercises of high excel- lence.—lnfants' School: The infants are very 'orderly and quiet and seem to enjoy their school life. they do their work very creditably, answer- ing readily and sensibly in their object lessons, and exhibiting much proficiency in the elementary sub- jects and their several occupations. In drawing also the school merited "excellent." HOPE OF EARLESTOWN LODGE 1.0.G.T.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of this lodge was held in the Co-operative Hall on Monday evening, Bro. E. Newman, P.D.S.J.T., presiding over a good at- tendance. It being Bank Holiday several visitors were present. One candidate was initiated for membership and one restored. Representatives were appointed for the next half year to the No. 1 Convention. Arrangements were also decided upon to give a sacred musical entertainment on Sunday afternoon at Ashton Primitive Methodist Chapel. The Rev. A. Lee,grand chaplin,will also be present. Several matters in hand in respect to the coming district lodge which is to be held at Earlestown on a large scale were considered. Short addresses, re- eiting and singing, were given. Votes of thanks to the visitors closed a very good meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>EXPERIENCES OF AN EARLRSTOWN PARTY.</head>
        <p>EXPERIENCES OF AN EARLRSTOWN PARTY.</p>
        <p>On Monday,a party of youths hailing from Earles- town hired a horse and trap for a drive to Liver- pool. They arrived there all right;, and after a long rest they commenced the journey homewards. Some distance had been covered and the party were enjoying themselves when the horse refused to go any further. .They tried all manner of persuasion but without effect and therhad to ultimately take it out of the shafts. After some lapse of time they harnessed the horse again and proceeded a short distance on their way, and whilst singing the well known ditty " Woa. Mare" the horse fell dead in the roadway. This put them in a fix, and with the assistance of a couple of policemen they tumbled the horse into a ditch and ran the trap into a field, and trudged the remainder of the distance home. DEATH OF COUNCILLOR JAMES SMITH, NEWTON.</p>
        <p>We regret to have to announce the death of our townsman, Councillor James Smith of Brookside, Newton-le-Willows, which took place at his resid- ence at midnigtt on Tuesday. The deceased gentle- man after his election to the District Council in March last, went to Harrogate for his health, and whilst there he took cold after a vapour bath. Pneumonia set in and he was in a most critical con- dition, but alter an operation was petformed he recovered somewhat sufficient to enable his removal home. After arriving home however he appeared to decline again, and on Wednesday in last week. another operation was performed. but he never re- covered, and died as stated at the age of 62 years. Mr. Smith took up his residence at Newton a little over two years ago, and after Mr. Gillespie resigned his seat on the old Board of Commissioners, he was appointed in March, 1894, to replace that gentle- man. On the formation of the District Wuncil be contested one of the seats for Crow Lane ward, in which he was successful. He was again returned to the Council in March this year in opposition to Mr. McDermott by a substantial majority. He was chairman of.the Gas Committee, an alderman to the Wigan Corporation, and ex-Mayor of Wigan. Ile was also connected with various cmcerns in and about Wigan, and was chairman of a brewery Company. The funeral will take place on Saturday at Ince Cemetery, near Wigan. There will be a short service at St. -Petet's Church, Newton, at 10.10 a.m. after which the cortege will proceed by real to Ince, The flags at the Earlestown Town Hall and Conservative Club have been flying half mast since the death. ST. YOHN'S CHURCH CHOIR PICNIC.</p>
        <p>The members of the above choir with several friends to the number of 50, had their annual pic- nic on Monday. The party left EarleAown by the 845 a.m. train for Southport, where they arrived shortly before 11 o'clock. They immediately pro- ceeded to Ileyes' Restaurant; wtfere they partook of an excellant dinner. Afterwards several of the party employed their time rambling about the sands' whilst others journeyed to Churchtown Botanical Gardens. Tea was partaken of at fleyeb' at 3.30, and the remainder of the day was spent in visiting the Royal Agricultural Show. At the con- clusion of the tea, Mr. W. Monks, on behalf of the members of the choir, presented Mrs. Latimer, organist,with a handsome ladies reticule, as a token of their esteem and appreciation of her ,ervices.Mr. Latimer suitably acknowledged the gift on behalf of his wife. The party left for home at 7.15 and reached Eatlestown about 9.15. The arrangements for the outing were suceftsfully carried out by 31ftsrs. Monks and Latimer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH SUNDAY SCHOOL TREAT.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH SUNDAY SCHOOL TREAT.</p>
        <p>The annual treat to the scholars attending the above school, and the Mission Hall School, took place on Saturday afternoon in favourable weather. The scholars assembled at the church at one o'clock when a short service was held and an appropriate address was deliveted by the vicar, tke Rev. T. IL Matthews. After the service a procession was tormed, the Viaduct Brass baud heading the female classes, and the Liverpogl Farm school band, the male portion.A very imposing scene was presented, upwards of 1200 scholars being present, the juveniles bringing up the rear in conveyances lent by various tradesmen. They proceeded by way of Sankey-street, Regent-street, Viaduct-street, to Earle Cottage ; returning along Earle-street, Queen- street, King-street, Bridge-street, Market-street,andl thence to Newton Common where the grand stand enclosure had been kindly placed at their disposal by Mr. Pardev, Lord Newton's steward. At four o'clock the younger portion, numbering 700, were served with tea in a lower room, after which the Senior scholars sat down to tea in the upper room. An enjoyable time was afterwards spent in games racing and scrambling--the bands playing for danc- ing until dusk. McCOBQUODALE AND COMPANY'S CRICKET</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>CH ALLE NGE CUP,</head>
        <p>CH ALLE NGE CUP,</p>
        <p>The cup offered by Messrs. McCorquodale and Co. for competition at cricket emongst their employes at Newton, Leeds, and London, has fallen into the possession ot the Newton staff for this year. A Week or two ago they easily defeated Leeds, and on i'onday gained an easy victory over the London stiff. The following are the scores : NEWTON OFFICE. LONDON OFFICF.. J Cooke c Yuill b Grum- Wells run out bridge 62 Cookson b W H Hindley 5 W H Parks b Yuill 0 Cooper b Cooke Whi Hindley lbw b Yuill 10 Culross oW H Hindley b W Gee e Grurnbridge b Cooke Wells ii Pearson c Hamilton b GrumbridgA b Cooke Yuill A Bosbell a Cooper b Fox 57 Hamilton c Parks b H Hlt Hindley St Iteeve b • Hindley Grumbridge 2 Ilt eve b Cook- W Wilkinson run out 6 Crewe not out J Galloway b Fox I Dudgeon St 'lees,. is</p>
        <p>Grumbridgo 6 C W Ithatilev not out ... 0 Extras 5 Extras</p>
        <p>Total 152 Total B3 At the conclusion of the game, the players ad- journed to the Pied Bull Hotel where they partook of an excellent knife and fork tea.the evening being afterwards spent in a convivial manner.</p>
        <p>1.0.G.T. EARLESTOWN EBENEZER LODGE No. "155, The first session of this quarter was held in the Co-operative Hall on Tuesday evening. Lodge called to order at 8-10 p.m. Bro. T. Dean CT. pre- sided, roll of officers was called and responded to, after which Bro. Taylor, Lodge Deputy, read the Grand Chief Templar's circular. The secretary then read the digest of No. 1 Convention,S.W.Lancaghire to be held at Widnes on Saturday, August Bth tlien various rcports were given and accepted by the Lodge, after which the quarterly election took place and the following were duly elected and in- stalled by the Lodge Deputy Bro W. Tdylor,assisted by Sisters Newman and Witty, Chief Templar Bro. J. Taylor, V. r.,Sister Hopkins, sec.,Bro. J. b opkins ; treasurer, Bro. A. Morris; chaplain, Bro. J. Woods and S.J.T. ; financial secretary, Bro. J. Marsh; Ga. Bro. P. Topping ; sen., Bro. T. Topping; assistant secretary, Bro. H. Renning ; Par, C.T.,8r0. T. Dean lodge deputy, Bro. W. Taylor; elec. superintendent; T. Dean; assistant superintendent, Jun Temple ; Sister Woods ; after which the lodge closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY</p>
        <p>MR. GARRATT RONAYNE'S CASE AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>At Warrington County Petty Sessions on Adnesday, Mr. Garratt Ronayne appeared before the bench on a summons for the enforcement of the penalties he had incurred.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woreley said he was happy to say that since the summons was taken out Mr. Ronayne had removed the building which the Newton Council objected to and was prepared now to give bis undertaking to the Council that he would not erect any other building there with- out the sanction of the Council. That being the case he (Mr. Worsley) asked leave tr, withdraw the summons which had been taken out. He would also not apply for costs.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ronayne signified his willingness to abide by what Mr. Worsiey had said and the case was withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council will be held at the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Monday next, to deal with the following among other business.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>This Committee state that the School Attendance Officer read his monthly report. Before accepting the report the Committee instructed the officer to expunge that part relating to outside interference. The attend- ance officer's quarterly statement of attendances showed that for the quarter ending June 30th, 1896, the aver- age attendance at the schools named was as follows : St. Peter's 87±; do. infants, 69 ; Gas-st., 78 ; Wargrave 88%; do. infants 75; Earlestown district 88 7.8; do. infants, 87±; Wesleyan 89 1-5 ; do. infants, 89/ ; Vulcan infants 84. With regard to the Roman Ca.tnolic, the report contains the words, "information received." A letter, dated July Ist, 1896, was read from the Local Government Board, enclosing copy of a letter which had been addressed to the managers' correspondent of St. Mary's Roman Catholic Day Schools and asking for the Committee's observations on the passages of the corres- pondent's letter of the 15th June, which refer to the manner in which the duties of the attendance officer are performed.—The Chairman read a draft letter, which he proposed Is hand to the Clerk to be forwarded by him to the Rlucation Department, which after consideration by the Committee was agreed to. miGHWAYS &amp;C. COMMIT TEE.—MEDICIL OVFICER'S REPORT, &amp;C. The Medical Officer's (Dr. Watkins) report for the quarter ending June 30th was adopted by the Highways Committee. It states that there were registered : Births, males 65 ; females 65; deaths, males 18 ; females 24. The births were equal to an annual rate of 37.1 and the deaths from all causes) to 11.1 per thousand. When the deaths of four persons from injuries and of two ether persons not belonging to the dis*rict were de- ducted the net death-rate from disease was brought down to 101 per thousand. The deaths of children un- der five years of age was 33.3 per cent. of the total number. The cases of infectious diseases notified were scarlatina 30, erysipelas 4. Nine of these cases were removed to the isolation hospital," and,adds the Medical Officer, "if the same common sense that directed the re- moval of seven cnnsecutive occurring cases in little more than the same number of days had prevailed throughout the quarter, we should not have had thirty cases of the disease to record—and certainly not the ctllpable mistake of such a concentrated focus of the disease as five cases in one house." _ _ _</p>
        <p>Tenders for a street watering cart were submitted. It wasresolved that Mr. Emmett,Mr. Beatty, and the Sur- veyor be requested to visit the Manchester and Liver- pool Agricultural Show at Southport, during the early part of August,to inspect, and if approved, purchase a patent distributing street watering cart. The Deputy Sanitary Inspector (Mr. Gregory) reports that ha has visited the bakehouses, slaughter-houses, milk shop, and lodging-houses and found little to com- plain of. The district is now nearly clear of all infec- tious diseases. There is only one house affected which he trurted, would be clear/in a few days: His monthly tolls for street hawking was £5 17s. 10.1. ; market tolls, £22 64. 31. ; total, £23 43. id. For the corresponding month last year they were £24 83. • 91. The Inspector brought before the notice of the Committee the great difficulty he had if, collecting the tolls for street hawking. He cannot make people understand why some have to pay and others not, and: he thinks they will have to do something to make it better understood. The Corm-nit:tee has passed the following resolutions: That the Clerk and the Inspector of Nuisances bo.and they are hereby authorised to enforce the Pcovisions of the P&amp;-ilic Health Act, 1875,with respect to the registra- tion of COMM9FI lodging-housea within the district. That the Inspector of Police for this district—or any Sorgeant or Constable whom he may appoint as his dr- puty—be appcinted an Inspec:or of common lodging. houses on behalf of the C:eincil. 'renders for a shed for the steam road roller were sub- mitted from Messrs. Fitz.ferald Brothers, for £66 104., and Eli Bunting, £66, and it was resolved that the ten- der ot Mr. Bunting he accepted. That Mr. Gilmour, Mr. Beatty, and Mr. Simpson, be a sub-committee to report upon all insanitary property within the district.</p>
        <p>That the Clerk be authorised to make app'ication to the Local Government Board for sanction to borrow the sum of £13,030 for the purposes of private works of street improvement.</p>
        <p>That where the owners of property fronting upon private streets which are not yet paved, flagged, are wiling to pay for such paving, &amp;., on completion. the surveyor be authorised to proceed with the work. Letter dated July 14th read from Messrs. T. Molyneux and T. Taberner requesting the Council to flag the foot- path in front of their property in Wargrave-road, the expense to be borne by the Council. The Clerk was directed to reply that the work would be done on the terms and conditions of a resolution pissed by the Com- missioners on April 20h, 1885, viz :—" That one half of the cost be borne bv the Commisiioners, and the other half by the lessees.'-'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>GAS AND ATER COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>GAS AND ATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Gas Manager (Mr. Swann) reports to this Com- mittee that there is still a great demand for automatic meters and gas pendants as during the past month they have laid eighty servives, fixed 72 automatic meters,and six ordinary ones, and fitted up complete inside forty houses, and they had fifty further applications on hand. The consumption of water for the month amounted to 5,822,000 gallons as compared with 5,415,000 gallons in tho coresporiding month last year. The Fire Appliance Committee recommend the pur- chase of several fire appliances at a cost of £47, and this recchrunendation has been alopted by the Gas and Water Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE,</head>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE,</p>
        <p>This Committee report that a letter dated July 15th, 1896, was read from Mr. J. Higginbotham, superinten- dent of police for this division, stating that appfications for an increase of police should b 5 in the hands of the superintendent the first week in November. The Corn mittee considered that they had no cause to complain of the number of police officers. The Clerk to reply accord- ingly. In pursuance of notice duly given, Mr. Gilmour moved—That in future a copy of the printed minutes of committees' proceedings be placed on the reporters' table—one for each reporter—at the Council meetings, instead of sending an' advance copy as hitherto. This was seconded by Mr. Wood and carried. Mr. Thompson voted to therantrary. RE-APPOINTMENT OF MEDICAL OFFACER. The Chairman of the Council gives notice that he will move in pursuance of notice duly given— hat Dr. J. W. Watkins, of Stonelegh, N.wton-il-Willows, be, and he is hereby, appointed medical offieer of health for the Urban District of Newton-in-Makerfied for a period of ono year from the 29th day of September, 1896, at a salary of £5O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>PENKE'CII.</head>
        <p>PENKE'CII.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN CHATEL.-0a Sunday last the Re Lidgett preached two sermons, morning and evening, at the Wesleyan chapel. The services were well att.,nded. In the afternoon Mr. Lidgott conducted the Young Men's Claes at the Sunday school. v. J. Scott - _ . _</p>
        <p>CHURCH LADS' fiRIGADI4:-00 SAttaday last the St. Mary's company proceeded to South Shore Camp, Black- pool. for their annual encampment. The lad: fell in at headquarters at 1.30 and proceeded by bus to Warring- ton, and then, in compiny with the Warrington Battal- ion entrained for Blackplol. The Sankey Company were in charge of Lieutenants Rin,Yer and Owen, Captain McKecknie and Lioutenant-Surgecn Murray, having gone to the camp the week before. The lais are having glorious weather, and from accounts received are deriv- ing great benefit from their holiday. The inspection was announced for yesterday (Friday).</p>
        <p>Manchester City Council on Wednesday adopted a re- commendation prohibiting any person,either on his own behmlf or for another, from frequenting or using any street for the purposs of bookmaking or betting. Thr penalCy is £5 for each offence, and it is understood that the bye-law will be rigidly enforced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>AUGUST 8, 1596</head>
        <p>AUGUST 8, 1596</p>
        <p>11A.ARIAGE.</p>
        <p>A marriage in which more than usual interest was taken was solemnized in Wycliffe Congregational Church on Monday. The contracting parties were Miss Emma Booth, youngest daughter of Mr. John Booth, 16 and 18, Tilley-street, and Mr. George Thomas Rushton, of Whalley-street. The bride who was elegantly attired, was given away by her father. Miss E. Rushton, sister to the bridegroom, was bridesmaid, and Mr. Joseph Harrison fulfilled the duties of best, man. The ceremony was per- formed by the Rev. E. T. Reed. A wedding break- fast was afterwards served in Wycliffe Cockhedge Mission when about 50 guests were present. The happy couple later in the day deperted for the Isle of Man where the honeymoon is being spent. The presens included the following :—Bridegroom to bride, gold brooch; bride's father, cheque; Mr. George Roger, handsome clock; Mrs. George Boger, set of dishes and tablecloth Mr.and Mrs. Harrison, trinket set' Miss Emelia Booth, a mirror; Mrs. Houghton, kettle; Mr. T. 14 arhurst and Miss E. Rushton, whatnot; Mrs. Rushton, set of vases; Mr. and Mrs. R. Travis, I doz. tea spoons ; Miss A. jack- son and Mr. A. Adcroft, tablecloth; Mr. and Mrs. Henry Maybury, bath towels and trinket set; Mr. and Mrs. Bridge flower stand and copper kettle ; Mr. and Mrs. James Wells, gipsy table; Miss E. A. Harrison, set pin trays; Miss A. Fairbrother, tea kettle; Mr. Thomas Gallop, bird and cage; Miss Pickton, half doz. tea spoons; Miss Houghton, flower stand; Mr. James Houghton, paper rack; Mrs. Worth, cheese dish ; Miss A. Atherton, antima- cassars and pin cushion; Mr. Patrick, tablecloth; Mr. Boswell, set vases; Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Peers flower vases, sugar basin and cream jut; Mrs. John Jackson. set of glass dishes; Miss Gertrude Booth and Mr. Lightfoot, flower stand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>DAHLIA QUEEN FESTIVAL AT</head>
        <p>DAHLIA QUEEN FESTIVAL AT</p>
        <p>BIIRTONWOOD.</p>
        <p>The second festival in connection with the coronation of the Dahlia Queen, promoted by Mr. George Anders, of Burtonwood, was brought off on Monday under favourable and successful circumstances. It was announced that the festivities connected with the coronation would take place in Bold Park, and all ar- rangements wee made in accordance therewith, but at the last moment, unforseen circumstances arose which necessitated an alteration in the plans, and another field close 437 the entrance to Bold Park was secured. Pre- parations for the festival had been in active progress for many weeks past, new dresses and costumes having to be made,the whole arrangements being carried out under the direct supervision of Mr. and Mrs. George Andevi, assisted by Mr. J. H. Anders, and Mr. J. Littler. For the eventful day several residents were pressed into ser- vice to act as a committee, but the task of marshalling the procession proved rather more than the limited assistance could very well manage successfully. The time put down on the bills for starting from the Bridge Inn at Burtonwood was eleven o'clock,but it had turned one o'clock, before everything was ready to start. The Earlestown prize band headed the procession,which,con- sidering that Burtonwood is purely a rural village of a very scattered description, was of a very successful and praiseworthy character. Following the band was the Lord Mayor of Burtonwood (Mr. M. Weirs) and two councillors (Messrs. G. Owen and G. Garner.) Pro- grammes describing the characters in the procession,and the order in hich they would proceed, were issued,but it is only fair to say that the printed order was not ad- hered to.</p>
        <p>The distance to be traversed was very great and in consequence of this almost all the characters were con- veyed in lorries, carts. trans, &amp;C. There were mounted cavalry, a sword bearer (James Kelly), sceptre bearer (John Gawtherpe), Archbishop (hazing crown) William Deakin. The Dahlia Queen who was conveyed in what was called the state carriage of Napoleon 111 was Miss Angelina Jackson, daughter of Mr. Jackson, manager of Collins Green collieries. She looked very pretty in her state robes, and was attired in a crushed strawberry coloured silk dress with ermine train. She was attended by her maids of honour, Misses J.A.Gawthorpe, Hannah Sher- ratt,M. E.Rieby,A. Davies,A.Massey,A. Stnith,R.Elouge - ton, A. Parker, M. E. Naylor, J. Pennington, and A. Jones, the latter presenting the Queen with a handsome bouquet. Behind the carriage were two pages attired in gold plush velvet, and they appeared to be most uncom- fortable in their position. They were S. Houghton and I. Weldin. The Queen was guarded by a company of nicely attired Hiehland soldiers, who had been trained for the occasion by Sergeant T. Williams, flower girls, old men and women, and 20 Dahlia pole dancers were included in the procession ; the Arkevvay troupe of ministrels. Mr. and Mrs. Sloper and family were represented by Mr. John Robinson and Mr. 14. Makin. The ex.Q.teen (Miss F. Horrabin), was conveyed in a landau drawn by a pair of greys, and she was accom- panied by her maids of honour: Misses E. Miller, F. Miller, M. Deakin, E. Weldin, E Barker, and S. Sepliton, page boys, J. floaghton and E. Rabjohns. Little 80-Peep (Miss R. Littler), and Boy Blue, Master Joseph Littler. , Hospital nurses, white slave (Miss M. Houghton), slave driver (Mr. J. Jones), floral dancers, Britannia, (Miss A. Griffiths) with Ireland, (F., Farrimond). John Bull, W. Hart) Scotland, (McMarr), Wales, (Miss E. Oaks). Sailor (H. Baxter), colliers, and a load of cannel from Bold Colliery, gipsies, Italians, red riding hood, bricksettere and labourers, bakers &amp;c. On another lorry were three excellent representations of statuary, and another lurry contained the Fairy Queen (Miss M. Hinigan) and a number of younger fairies. altogether the turnout was a very fair sample of its kind, and made a very brave show. The marshalling was carried out by Mr. J. H. Anders. On arrival at the field the procession formed up round the throne, and after the Queen had taken her seat thereon with the usual formalities, she was crowned by Miss Forster, who, to mark the occasion, presented her with a beautiful lady's gold watch. The dahlia pole dance was afterwards proceeded with, the dancers hav- ing been trained by Mr. J. H. Anders. the other fes- tivities included drills and bayonet exercise by the Highland solthers,entertainment by the Arkaway troupe of Minstrels, incleding solos and dances by Mr. J. Arcus (late of Sam Hague's Minstrels). Serpentine and skirt dance by Miss Flossie Smith, of Sc. Helens, a: child about nine years of age. This part of the entertainment was well worth the applause the child received, her dancing being neat and clever. A washing competition was also held with Sunlight soap,consisting of the wash- ing of a dirty towel and hanging it upon the line in three minutes, the judging being upon the cleanest towels. The first prize (case of silver spoons), was won by Min Hannah Broster, of Earlestown ; second. Mrs. Stnethurst. Burtonwood ; third, Mrs. Sloper, Burton- wood. Prizes were offered for the neatest and cleverest turnouts, the judges being Messrs. J. Pemberton and R. Na.yloteand the awards were :—Farmers' class : 1, Col- lins Green Colliery ; 2,Jonn Fairclough,Yew Tree Farm; 3, William Fairelough, Brookhead Farm. Tradesmen's class i-1, E. L. Bent ; 2, S—Hague ; 3, William Austin. There were other amusements provided and the Lanes- town band played for dancing until dusk. On Tue-day Mr. Anders entertained the children who ttohoekapraide r:;inlntnhe, and afterwards the children enjoyed procession to tea in the field behind themselves in racing for various prizes, and scramb:ing for nuts and fruit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>WARRINGI:ON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGI:ON.</p>
        <p>GIFT BY LADY GREENALL.</p>
        <p>Lady Greenall has promised £lOO each to St. Ann's and St. Peter's Churches, to the former in aid of the vicarage fund, and to the latter in aid of the endowment fund. THE limn COURT OF4 FORESTERS.</p>
        <p>At the annual assembiy of the High Court of Foresters, which is being held at the Kinnaird hall Dundee, the VVarrington district is represented by Bro. L. Davies (Court 240), district secretary, and Bro. D. Jones (Court 430). Music IN THE PARE. On Sunday afternoon St. Paul's band rendered a selection of music in the park. There was a large attendance.—On Monday night the Latchford band played the following programme of dance music : Polka, "Old friends" (J. Robinson); quadrille. " Festivity "(J. Ord Hume); schottishe, '• Belgrave" (J. Robinson) ; lancers, "The banks of Killarney" (W. GL &amp;holes) ; waltz, " tiertrella" 9 • quick step. " Egremont." There was again a good attendance.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF A LOCAL SOLICITORS' CLARK. Mr. Herbert Clarke, twenty-two, a clerk with Messrs. Percy Davies and Fershaw, solicitors, War- rington, has secured the first place in the recent examination :or one third-class clerkship in the Irish Land Commission, and for four clerkships in the congested District Board, Ireland. The exam- ination was open to the United Singdom,and there were seventy-two competitors for the five vacan- cies. Mr. Clarke is the son of Mr. John Clarke, gtocer, Orford-lane Post Mee, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>WESLEYAN PICNIC TO BEESTON CASTLE.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN PICNIC TO BEESTON CASTLE.</p>
        <p>On Monday, about 80 members and friends of the Wesley Guild had a picnic to Beeston Castle, which the party were allowed to inspect by the kind per- mission of Lord Tollemache. They left Warrington at half-past eight in the morning, and reached Bees- ton about half-past twelve, having been delayed by a slight accident to one of the buses. They then inspected the Castle and had tea, after which preparationg were made for the return journey, A start was made from Beeston at seven o'clock, the party arriving at Warrington at about half-past ten, all having spent a most enjoyable day. WARRINGTON LICENSED VICTUALLERS' AsSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>At the annnal meeting of this association the election of °lll tirs was proceeded with. Councillor Evans was re-elected president, and Councillor Shillcock was elected vice-president in place ut Mr. Weave, resigned. Messrs. R. Anderton and J. Weave were appointed trustees, whilst owing to pressure of business, Mr. Peers Brimelow found himseif unable to take the post of hon. secretary. The meeting unanimously elected Mr. T. Grounas as Mr. Brimelow's successor. The following gentle- men compose the committee for the ensuing term : —Messrs. J. Sampson,.T. Walsh, J. Allison, W. Cop- pock, R. Perdue, and P. Lake. It was decidedthat Cue annual picnic should be to the Dukeries.</p>
        <p>LA.WN ;OWERS.—GARLA.Nis, Bridge•street, War- rington. —Aldv t. WALKING DAY AT STitETTON.</p>
        <p>-...-- The annual Walking Day in connection with St. Matthew's Church Schools took place on Friday last, in splendid weather. The children assembled at the schools at one o'clock, and, headed by the band of the Warringten Blue Coat School, marched to the church, where a short address was delivered by the Rev. Canon Dodd. Upon leaving the church the scholars and band proceeded to the Vicarage grounds, where bun and wine were served out to them. After a short rest they again formed in order, and proceeded round the village, headed by the band playing lively airs. On returning a sub- stantial tea was awaiting them in the school. The remainder of the evening was spent in a convivial manner. The usual ball was held in the evening, a large number being present. Dancing was kept up with much spirit till an early hour in the morning. Mr. Howard acted as M C., and also provided refreshments. For NAM R BROOCHES, solid silver name brooches 3.0 d (any name) Government stamped. Preeents or Articles of you-self, you may visit DESERT'S. 10. CHURCH-ST BET, LIV blIP4)01, where you can get hundreds of articles such as Watch., Clocks,-Jeweldery, Electro-Plate. Cut ery,Purses. Pocket Knives, etc., at wholesale prices. See advertisement' (Illustrated Chronograph Wadi, 6s. lad., etc.), on page 7 of this Paper.— idvt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>BANK HOLIDAY AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BANK HOLIDAY AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the gloominess of the weather during last week end August Bank Holiday proved to be a fine day with just sufficient warmth to make it pleasant. Innumerable trips, picnics, etc., small and large, the town for pleasure re- sorts in the immediate neighbourhood or further afield. Railway traffic was again very brisk and some 4,500 people travelled by the Cheshire Lines Railway to Liverpool, Manchester, and Southport. Nearly 1,000. went by the London and North-Western to Blackpool and the several pretty places along the Chester line. Frodsham was crowded with trippers and the hills resounded with music and laughter. At Helsby there was far less bustle, and those who paid a visit, there were rewarded by the restful enjoyment they experienced. Chester, of course, was full all round, and the river was proportionately busy. The places of historic interest were largely viewed, and many Warringtonians spent a pleasant day in the quaint old city. Lymm also received a large influx of visitors, and the trains,especially at night, were crowded to excess. A cheaper, but perhaps not less enjoyable holiday was spent by the hundrida of people who rode or walked to Stockton Heath, Latchford, and Grappenhall. The latter place was roused out of its apathetic existence by the numbers of visitors it received, while the other places named presented a more than usually populous appear- ance. Taken all round the general holiiay has been quite as enjoyable as any of its predeeessors, and the popularity of it as an institution further strengthened.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>WESLEYAN CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>THE WESLEYAN CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>MEETING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A public meeting was held in Bold-street Wesleyan Chapel on Saturday evening, when there was a fair. at- tendance. The chair was occupied by Mr. James Dodds who was accompanied on the platform by the Revs. G. D. Mason, W. L. Triggs, J. Hector (a coloured repre- sentative of the African Methodist Episcopal Church of America), J. Scott Lidgett M.A., (Bermondsey Settle- ment), and H. Meakin (South London Mission). After being introduced by the chairman. the Rev. John Hector joined his family in the rendition of a jubilee melody, " On the Banks by the River Side." Later in the evening other melodies, all of a religious purport were given, with harmonium accompaniment: An earnest and humorous address, chiefly directed against the drink evil,was given by the Rev. John Hee- tor,who said in the course of his remarks that he had been a workingman all his life. He had struggled hard to maintain a po--ition which few men oc his race ever acquired on the °tilt r side of the Atlantic, as he had had the distinct honour of being the first black man that ever fired a locomotive or ran one out of New York. (Applause.) He got a position in the second largest works in the United Stares; drove a 530 horse-p-wer engine, supported his family,and preached the gospel of Jesus Christ. (Applause.) The speakt.r then said that his subject that night was " The benefit that sobriety brings to the working classes." If they wiped the drink traffic out they would be told that they were destroying a great industry. It was not an industry but some- thing which fed the other industries and produced nothing but poverty and crime. (Applause.) When he changed his own life he began to sound the question which faced the working classes of tc-day.. He began to consume what the farmers produced and wear what the manufacturers made, and he asserted that a labour- jog man who drank rum could not do it. (Hear, hear.) It rook three men to make a barrel of beer and 18 to make a suit of woollen clothes from a sheep's back to their own backs.To call the liquor an industry trade he said was cnough"to give a cat convulsions."( Laughter.) Labour was the power that produced wealth and how were they going to be wealthy if the men were net sober enough to labcur to produce it ? (Applause.) He dared say some of them had hears tb3t a man could not work without a glass of bier; that it was good for his health. If they would show him a doctor who would tell them that beer would cure illness he would turn round and show them a doctor that did not know enough about medicine or the human body to cure a ham. (Loud laughter.) Owing to the negro slaves in America hay- log been forbidden whiskey for 200 years they were to- day the more physically healthy, and strong, had bet- ter teeth, and could sing louder, and make more racket generally to the square inch Alan any other nation. (Laughter.) There was only one part of a man that whiskey would strengthen and that was his breath. (Laughter.) He concluded by expressing his surprise that there were so many neinisters who were not • total abstainere. He believed that until the church of God and the ministry were liberated from the rum curse there would he no progress towards heaven. Tne day was not far distant when the flag of old England would not be seen floating over a brewery, a distillery, or legalised rum shop. (Applause.) The Rev. H.MsaxlN said he would tell them how they influenced working men in the southern district of LOll- - Be related how he made successful his church at Loxfields. One day he saw 'outside a newspaper shop a crowd of about 15 people looking at scme newspaper illustrations of horrible murders and other crimes. One woman was crying, and seeing that they could be inter- ested by such matters it occurred to him to gospelise the news of the • day. He set apart each Friday night and reproduced those pictures by limelight. Eventually the people came in crowds and got familiar with the inside of his church. Next they came on ' Sunday and now he had a membership of 330. 168 on trial, 200 meeting in classes, and a Sunday school of 700 scholars. (Applause.) The Rev. J. Scorr LIDCIETT commenced a lengthy ad- dress by tracing the progress of Christianity and its re- sults upon mankind. The Christian Church, he said, entered into the world as a special emancipator of the poor, the weak, the ignorant, the helpless.and the down- trodden. When people said that Christianity was a catholic church, they meant that its religion was for all men. And sue jt was. It was a religion for the Jew as well as for the Gentile, for the black as well as for the white, for every race and class. But,in order for it to be a catholic religion, they must do something more than proclaim. They must take in the whole of human nature. Their religion was not catholic enough unless they claimed all a man had. That was what the early church did. Hospitals were founded, district visitation was commenced, and Christian workers were Sant out to enlighten ignorance in all the darkest and least healthy parts of the great heathen cities. They had heard how from the first the Christian Church stood for the sacred cause of education. It never said that all its blessings were for the wealthy ; no, but for the poor. Lot them stand by education, and don't let people tell them that because their money was being spent in rates that it was being squandered. The cheapest form of xpenditure was a good education. (Applause.) Let very child have an education that should enable him to stand side by side with the (hike and the millionaire of the country. That was the principle on which Chris- tianity went from the first,snd the speaker proceeded to give instances in proof of his assertion. In conclusion, he said they should endeavour to secure the best condi- tions on this earth, to set everything in order. They must be saved from their selfishness and unbelief. Th better life down here the More promise there was of a better life above. The harder the life down here the harder it was to make people believe there was a para- dise above. (Applause.) Elymns were interspersed with the speaking and a collection was made in aid of the expenees of the confer- ence. • Some of the choir members assisted in the service and Mr. Peter Harrison presided.at the organ.</p>
        <p>The Board of Trade has addressed an appeal to the various railway companies to take such steps as may put a stop to outrages on women in railway carriages. Briefly, the Board suggests that the corridor carriage should be generally introduced, and as rapidly as possi. ble. , They suggest also that separate compartments should be universally provided for such ladies as desire to make use of them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL CLUB SPORTS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL CLUB SPORTS.</p>
        <p>Fine weather favoured the twelfth annual athletic festival and gala held under the auspices of the Warring- ton Rugby Football Club on Monday afternoon. There was a very large attendance, and a capital afternoon's sport was provided. The arrangements were catried out in a very satisfactory manner, and the ground con- sidering the weather which had been experienced a few days before ss as in excellent condition for sprinting and bicycle riding. The following were the officials in charge of the arrangements :—Clerk of the course, Mr. T. Bowers ; starter' Mr. E. Kane ; judges, Messrs. ;. Evans, W. Lomas, R. Andrews and E. Pickavance, N.C.U. ; committee, Messrs. T. Davies, W. Hawitt, G. Smith, H. Ashton, F. Barber, James T. Bate, J. Hackett and John T. Bate, J. T. Thorniley, W. houghton and J. Jones; handicapper, Mr. W. Platt ; hon. treasurer, Mr. T. Pem- berton ; hon. secretary, Mr. J. E. Warren; assistant hon. secretary, Mr. S. W. Jeffery. There were thirteen events inclusive of the boxing competition, which was one of the chief attractions. The first event was the _ 100 YARDS HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>For this, there were 33 entries, although seven of the runners failed to put in an appearance. The competi- tors ran in six heats. In the first, J. Cunningham of Warrington,who was scratch nian succeeded in reaching the winning tape first. J. Rabbit, of Latchford, was the winner of the second heat, and success further at- tended local men for in the third heat, T. Horan, of Warrington, although heavily handicapped, gained a popular victory. There were only three runners in the fourth heat which was won by A. Tilther,of Rainhill. In the fifth, W. B. Read, of Haydock, was the first of four runners, including E. B. Joslyn, of Orford, and J. H. Millington. of Earlestown. The winner- of the sixth heat was R. Briscoe, of Legh, who sprinted nicely 'away from the other five competitors. It will be seen that three Warrington men reached the final stage, and it was anticipated thgt one of them would secure the first prise. But W. S. Read, of Haydock, upset these calcu- lations by securing a meritorious victory. Horan ran in second and Rabbit, third. Cunningham was rather heavily handicaped and it proved too much for him. The prizes were :—First, silver plated tea and coffee service with handsome silver plated tray to match; second, 8 day striking clock ; third, cruet,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>ONE MILE NOTICE EICYCLB HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>ONE MILE NOTICE EICYCLB HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>_ . Nearly the whole of the 32 competitors entered for this event turned out on the track. It was run in four beats, the first and second to enter the final. There were six cyclists in the first heat including W. Dutton, of Warrington. A little " spill "upset two riders in this race which was won by J. Birchall, Wigan, and N. Mortimer, Preston. The second heat was evenly con- tested by the five entrants. The two successful men were H. Dutton of Warrington, and G. A. Cowie. of Chester. Representatives of these two places were also the winners of the third heat, their names being J. E. Unsworth of Warrington. and W. H. Gwyn, of Chester. Four Warrington "novices" turned out in the fourth heat, but they were all out-classed,by C. E. Fannon, of Chester and E. J. Rigby. of Sutton. The four Warrington riders were S. Johnson, S. Grant, J. Clare and L. Davies, 15 rears of age, son of Councillor Percy Davies. of Latch- ford. A considerable amount of interest was taken in the performance of the latter youth who received 150 yards start. He raced well for two rounds but lacked staying power and was easily passed. An unfortunate accident marked the final race in this event. In the last lap, Rirchall, of Wigan,. had what has now become known as a " spill," and was also the means of upsett- ing two other riders including Dutton,. of Warrington, who was close upon him. Birchall received an injury to his shoulder, hut the others escaped unhurt. The prizes fell to Cowie"(I), Rigby (2), and Fannon (3), and were as follows:—First, spirit kettle and stand ; second, salad bowl and servers ; third, marble timepiece.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>220 YARDS HANDICAP</head>
        <p>220 YARDS HANDICAP</p>
        <p>32 competitors entered for this event and 24. ran. In the first heat A. Tilther, of Rainhill, just secured first place, and the second, who was also entitled to run in the final, was W. Isherwood, of West Leigh. J. Cun- ningham. of Warringtbn, was scratch man in this heat, and would most probably have won- bad he not been obstructed. Heat 2 was won by J. Wolfe, of the Crewe A.A.C., and J. Rabbit, of Latchford ;and in the third heat, a Worcester man, rejoicing in the name of Piggatt, was first, with J. Whittam, of Elton C.C.,, second. Starting from scratch in the fourth heat, T. Horan, of Warrington, succeeded in getting home second, R. Briscoe, of Leigh, first In the final, Rabbit was the only Warringtonian to gain honours, the result being Wolfe first, Rabbit second and Tilther, third. The fiAt prize was a marble clock and pair of bronze ornaments ; second, cruet ;. third, biscuit jar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>ONE MIZE BICYCLE HANDICAP:</head>
        <p>ONE MIZE BICYCLE HANDICAP:</p>
        <p>There were 21 entries for this event which was run in three heats. In the first heat were Newcombe and Dut- ton of Warrington, R. H. Parkinson (Preston). S. Taylor (Leigh), C. A. Cowie (Chester), and P. W. Killingbeck (Southport). Newcombe was the favourite from . the start, and he surprised everyone by' drawing level with the other competitors in the first lap—after giving seventy cards start—a performance which gained for him unstinted applause. After this, there was no doubt as to the result of the heat, and Newcombe ran out an easy first, and C. A. Cowie, second. In the second heat there were hot four cyclists.including W. Colin,. of War- rington, whose performance was watched with more than usual attention. 13ircha1: of Wigan, made good use of his ten yards start, and ran out first. Collin was second, closely followed ley Green, of Wigan. In the third heat, J. E. Unsworth was the only Warrington cyclist, but he was easily over- hauled by Scott, of Wigan and N. Mortimer, of Preston. The final was a most interesting race. Collin went off with a dash, and soon gained the lead, which he maintained in grand style up to the win- ning post. Newcombe was scratch man and made a great effort, but he only succeeded in getting in third, Cowie. of Chester taking the second priZe. The awards in this event were :—First, solid gold hall-marked albert and seal ; second, combination fruit, sugar and cream frame ; third, hot water jug.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>880', YAiDS. HANDICAP;</head>
        <p>880', YAiDS. HANDICAP;</p>
        <p>Twenty-five competitors set out to run in this handl- cap,and when the race had been in progress a short time and the squad of men had been somewhat thinned, it was seen that J. McCue, of Orford Harriers, T. Red- mond, Salford Harriers and A. Brierley, Manchester Harriers, were rapidly outdistancing the rest, and the result was that the men touched the tape in the follow- ing ora!er :—Brierlev 1, Redmond 2, and McCue 3. The prizes were :—First, silver English lever watch and solid gold ball-marked albert ; second, pickle frame; third, case of carvers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>ONE MILE lIANDICAP.</head>
        <p>ONE MILE lIANDICAP.</p>
        <p>The task of running a mile was attempted by a score of competitors including a number of local runners. Each successive lap saw the number dwindling until in the last lap there would not be more than half-a-dozen competitors. McCue, Orford Harriers, kept close to F. Woods, also of Orford Harriers, until near the finish when be easily outdistanced Woods as did also E.J.Guy, of Smallheath A.C. The runners had now been reduced to these three, and the result of the race was McCue 1, Guy 2, Woods 3. They received prizes as folio s:— First, silver plated tea and coffee set ; second, egg frame; third, aneroid barometer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>TWO MILES BICYCLE RANDICAP.</head>
        <p>TWO MILES BICYCLE RANDICAP.</p>
        <p>This event was run off in two heats. la the first. New combe again rode splendidly, and he was predicted as the winner of the premier prize. Starting almost 100 yards behind his next; competitor, a few laps sufficed to see him drawing level with the other six riders. To- wards the close, he again spurted away, and reached " home" first, followed by Granger, of Daven- ham, and Dutton of Warrington. In the second heat, Harvey, Gf Legh AC.. with 275yd5., was first, W. Collin, Warrington, 245, second, and R. Green, Wigan, 155,third. The final was a most exciting event. C)llin from the first, broke away and almost lead from the start. Newcombe caught up to him near the close, and seemed as if he would overhaul him but he did net do so. Green, who had been working bard throughout the race succeeded in drawing level with them, but with a fin al spurt, Collin reached the winning post first,New- combo second. and Green third. Flrst prim, case of one dcaln pairs desert knives and forks; second, case of carvers with soli A silver mounts ; third, toast rack and butter dish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>BOXING COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>THE BOXING COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>This was one of the chief features of the afternoon's programme, and the various contests were watched v ith great interest. Eight com- petitors had entered, but only six made an appearance in the arena. The first contest was between J. Rowhot- tom, Stockport, and J. Mosedale, Bury. In the first and seccnd rounds, the latter seemed to have the advan- tage, getting home several telling blows, but in the third round he fell away completely and became a vic- tim to Rowbottom's long roach. The judge decided to have an additional round of two minutes, and at the ePeclusion,he gave the decision in favour of Rewbottom. C.Mills of Birmingham, the runner-up for the feather- weight championship, and Owen-Jones,of Tranmere,were the'next contestants. Jones was be far the heavier man of the two, but Mills soon asserted his superiority and was very skilful in the use of his gloves. At the close of the last round, Mills was declared the winner. The next engagement was between T. Parnell, of Birmingham, and F. Mosedale, of Bury. This was a most even con- test and there was very little to judge between them. Mosedale was the heavier and better-set of the two, mid in the last round, gained an advantage, and won the contest. In the setn'-final, Mosedale enjoyed a walk- over. but in order that be should not have the benefit ef thin advantage in the final,l Warrington lad of the name of .J. Houghton was found to box with him for three ' rounds. The other engagement in the Semifinal was between Mills and Rowbottnm. Two rounds sufficed to knock Rowbottom out of time. Mills winning easily. The final was evenly contested. In the first and second rounds, Mille showed to advantage, his scientific hitting prevailing over the strong blows of his . antagonist. The third round was more In favour of Mosdale, but Mills had ecoied sufficient points in the first and seaond rounds to be declared win- ner. Mills was loudly cheered on retiring. Sergeant- Major riaigh,of Manchester Gymnasium was the judge, and his decisions gave every satisfaction. The first</p>
        <p>prize was a silver centre-seconds English lever chrono- graph.and the second,silver plated cup. During the afternoon the Borough Band, under the conductorship of Mr. T. Griffin, added a little liveljnesX to the prooeedings by the performance of excellent music.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the sports, Mrs. Thomas Moult; distributed the prizes to the successful competitor Messrs. E. and A. Eustance, nf Sankey-street, supplied ttie prizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>WARRINGTON SWINDLER.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON SWINDLER.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, the de puty assietant official receiver in bankruptcy (Mr. JAIL' son) made an application tl his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes on behalf ot the official receiver for an order W bring up John Grant,a bankrupt, who is in gaol id re' spect of a criminal offence. He stated that so far as he knew there was no provision in the bankruptcy rules to bring up a prisoner who was in gaol for a criminal offence. There was a form prrscri bed for bringing on a' prisoner who was in custody in connection with a civil matter, but that only 'applied to a prisoner from a County Court. The only provioion that he was aware ei whereby Grant could be brouglYs up was section 112 of the County Court Act, 1888.—His Honour: I dont think that will apply, that deals only in a case betweee a plaintiff and defendant in this. court. —Mr. JOhnsoe said he was afraid his could not refer his Honour to any other authorb.y. But he might say they' had bad prisoners brought up under similar circule- stances in Manchester under that section.—His Honour said with all respect to his learned brethren in Manches-' ter he was very doubtful if that section applied to bank' ruptcy proceedings.—Mr. Johnson said it had been the practice there for some years past.—His Honour saidte could not get over the fact that the form referred to pointed to a suit in that Court, and had no reference to proceedings outside.—Da. Johnson suggested that Hie Honour could alter the forms to meet a particular ease. —His flonour•said he was afraid he could not. He could not make a form apply to bankruptcy proceedings which in its inception applied to an action in the County Court only. That would be introducing a netsf clause into fhe Act of Parliament and dratwing up a Rea' form.—Mr. Johnson : Could your Honour make the order under the Bankruptcy Act, section 100 of the Act of 1883.—After some discussion his Honour ultimateli granted the order under that Act, but remarked that notwithstanding the practice in Manchester, he did not . .</p>
        <p>think he could have granted it under the section of the County Courts Act referred to.</p>
